2 DATE ABT 1287
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1354
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Pedro Afonso
2 CONT
2 CONT [Pedro de Barcelos]
2 CONT
2 CONT d.1354. Portugal. The Count of Barcelos in northern Portugal, he was an illegitimate son of King Dinis of Portugal, and a great grandson of Alfonso X of Castile and León. He dedicated his mature years to intellectual work, composi
2 CONC ng the two most valued historical works of the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Livro de Linhagens do Conde D. Pedro (Lineage Book of Count Pedro) is a compilation of lineage data from the previous Livros de Linhagens , and a revised version of the Navarrese Liber regum and the lost Crónica Portuguesa de 
2 CONC Espanha e Portugal . These sources are a mixture of family memories and fiction (mythical, legendary, traditional and epic materials). The text begins with a survey of the major lineages of the world, from Adam on, where the allus
2 CONC ions to Arthurian legends (via Geoffrey of Monmouth) are particularly noteworthy. The main Portuguese families are set in their Iberian context and the narrative is enriched by several appealing family myths, such as the origin na
2 CONC rratives of Dama Pé de Cabra (the Goat Foot Lady) and Dona Marinha (the Sea Lady), as well as examples of shrewdness (adventures of King Ramiro while trying to rescue his wife from Muslim captivity) and heroic exploits, such as th
2 CONC e feats of Rodrigo Forjaz, vassal of King Garcia of Galicia, against the Cid. This Lineage Book also conveys the epic deeds of the first Portuguese king, Afonso Henriques and of his preceptor, here named Soeiro Mendes, as well as 
2 CONC other local heroes such as Gonçalo Mendes da Maia and battles such as Salado. The book exists only in a version of 228 fols. which incorporates two revisions. It was first compiled in 1340-4 and reworked in 1360-5 and 1380-3, this
2 CONC  last time to praise the Pereira family. This work was very popular in the Iberian Peninsula, with over 60 manuscripts in Portuguese and Castilian translations still extant. The Portuguese manuscripts date mainly from the 16th and
2 CONC  17th century. The two oldest manuscripts have illuminations: A1 is known as Nobiliário da Ajuda or Nobiliário do Colégio dos Nobres:Lisbon, Biblioteca da Ajuda, Códice reservado. It dates from the end of the 14th century and migh
2 CONC t have been a work text used by the reviser of 1380-3. T1 is known as Nobiliário da Torre do Tombo: Lisbon, Torre do Tombo, 1764, late 15th or early 16th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Pedro is also accepted as the author of the first version of the Crónica Geral de Espanha de 1344 (General Chronicle of Spain of 1344). This chronicle was written in Portuguese in 1344. For the Reconquest period, it drew its
2 CONC  information from Alfonsine and post-alfonsine materials: the Estoria de Espanna mainly via a Crónica de Veinte Reyes ; the Versão Galaico-portuguesa da Crónica Geral de Espanha and again the Crónica Portuguesa de Espanha e Portug
2 CONC al. For the earlier periods he used chronologies, genealogic sources and a Portuguese translation of the Crónica de Rasis . There are no extant Portuguese manuscripts of this chronicle, which survives only in its Castilian transla
2 CONC tion, and that in a single manuscript and a few fragments: Salamanca, BU, 2656 (339 fols). It lacks both the beginning (it starts at the 3rd Age of the world) and the end, the birth of the Portuguese kingdom. This chronicle convey
2 CONC s several epic and legendary narratives such as the stories of the Visigothic kings Bamba and Rodrigo, and in the Reconquest period, the deeds of Bernardo del Carpio, Fernán González, the infantes of Lara, the treacherous countess
2 CONC , the infante Garcia, the youth of the Cid and his exploits up to his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel Barros Dias
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Text: J. Mattoso, "Livro de Linhagens do Conde D. Pedro", Portugaliae Monumenta Historica - Nova Série, 1980. D. Catalán & M.S. de Andrés, Edición Crítica del Texto Español de la Crónica de 1344 que ordenó el Conde de Barcelos don
2 CONC  Pedro Alfonso, 1970 [partial edition]. Literature: D. Catalán, De Alfonso X al conde de Barcelos, 1962. L.F.L. Cintra, Crónica Geral de Espanha de 1344, I, 1951. I.B. Dias, Metamorfoses de Babel. A historiografia ibérica (sécs. X
2 CONC III-XIV): Construções e estratégias textuais, 2003. L. Krus, A concepção nobiliárquica do espaço ibérico. Geografia dos livros de linhagens medievais portugueses (1280-1380), 1994. J. Mattoso, "A literatura genealógica e a cultura
2 CONC  da nobreza em Portugal (s. XIII-XIV)", in Portugal Medieval. Novas interpretações, 1992, 309-28. J. Mattoso, "Os livros de linhagens portugueses e a literatura genealógica europeia da Idade Média" and "As fontes do Nobiliário do 
2 CONC Conde D. Pedro", in A Nobreza Medieval Portuguesa, 1994, 37-55 & 57-100. RepFont 8, 545.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel Barros Dias. " Pedro Afonso." Encyclopedia of the Medieval Chronicle. Brill Online , 2012. Reference. Jim Harlow. 06 July 2012 <http://referenceworks.brillonline.com/entries/encyclopedia-of-the-medieval-chronicle/pedro-afon
2 CONC so-SIM_02022>
