1 NOTE {geni:about_me} PEDRO de Aragón (->1314)
2 CONT illegit s/o King Pedro III & Mistress  ([<1260]) MARÍA Nicolosa (->1292)
2 CONT x CONSTANZA Mendez Pelita de Silva, d/o SUERO Mendez de Silva & María Annes Brochardo
2 CONT *PEDRO de Aragón
2 CONT *FERNANDO de Aragón 
2 CONT *CONSTANZA de Aragón 
2 CONT *TERESA Alfonso de Aragón
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''PEDRO de Aragón (-after 1314).'''  The Nobiliario of Pedro Conde de Barcelos names "D. Pedro de Aragon" as the son of "el Rey D. Pedro" and "D. Ines Zapata"[579].  Bofarull names “Da Inés Zapata” as mother of the king’s illegit
2 CONC imate children “D. Fernando señor de Albarracín, D. Pedro que casó en Portugal con Da Constanza Mendez Pelita, y D. Sancho castellan de Amposta : la hija…Da Teresa y casó con D. Garcia Romeu que murió sin sucesion” (no sources cit
2 CONC ed)[580].  Salazar y Castro records the testament of Isabel Queen of Portugal, dated 1314, in which she bequeathed money to “Don Pedro, mi hermano, y à su hijo, qualquiera de ellos, que quedàre por mi muerte”[581].  m (Portugal) C
2 CONC ONSTANZA Mendez Pelita de Silva, daughter of SUERO Mendez de Silva & his wife María Annes Brochardo.  The Nobiliario of Pedro Conde de Barcelos records that "D. Pedro de Aragon", son of "el Rey D. Pedro" and "D. Ines Zapata", marr
2 CONC ied "D. Constança Mendez Petite"[582].  Pedro & his wife had four children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         PEDRO de Aragón
2 CONT b)         FERNANDO de Aragón 
2 CONT c)          CONSTANZA de Aragón 
2 CONT d)         TERESA Alfonso de Aragón
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ARAGON%20&%20CATALONIA.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Infante don PEDRO de Aragón, son of JAIME I "el Conquistador" King of Aragon & his second wife Iolanda of Hungary (1239-Villafranca del Penedés 2 or 11 Nov 1285, bur Monasterio de Santa Cruz).  Under the testament of "Jacobus…Rex 
2 CONC Aragoniæ, Majoricarum et Valenciæ, Comes Barchinonæ et Urgelli, et Dominus Montispessulani", dated 26 Aug 1272, the king made bequests to "…filium nostrum primogenitum Infantem P…"[515].  He was promised the succession of the Bale
2 CONC aric Islands, Valencia and Montpellier 1241, and received Roussillon and Cerdaña after the death of Nuño Sancho de Aragón in 1242.  A new projected partition was agreed 1244, under which Pedro would receive Catalonia on the death 
2 CONC of his father.  Appointed procurator of Catalonia 1257.  Under a further projected partition 1262, Aragon, Catalonia and Valencia were destined for Pedro.  He succeeded his father in 1276 as PEDRO III "el Grande" King of Aragon, C
2 CONC onde de Barcelona and King of Valencia, crowned at Zaragoza Nov 1276.  He suppressed the revolt in Valencia, ending with the capture of Montesa in 1277.  He confirmed his protectorate over Tunis.  He landed at Trapani in Sicily 31
2 CONC  Aug 1282, after the Sicilian revolt against the Angevins, and declared himself PIETRO I King of Sicily.  He was excommunicated by Pope Martin IV, who supported the Angevins, in Nov 1282.  In the course of the escalating dispute, 
2 CONC Philippe III King of France was persuaded to accept the Kingdom of Aragon for his second son Charles in Feb 1284.  In Sep 1284, Pedro crushed the rebellion of Juan Núñez de Lara who attempted to establish an independent lordship o
2 CONC f Albarracín.  King Philippe III invaded Aragon in early 1285 and briefly captured Girona 7 Sep 1285.  The French retreated to Perpignan (where King Philippe III died 5 Oct) after their fleet was destroyed in the Bay of Roses 3-4 
2 CONC Sep by admiral Ruggiero di Loria.  On his deathbed, Pedro renounced Sicily as the price of his peace with the church.  King Pedro’s testament is dated 26 Dec 1282, naming his legitimate children according to Bofarull’s summary[516
2 CONC ].  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña records the death "III Id Nov" in 1285 of King Pedro and his burial "en el monasterio de Santas Cruçs del Orden de Cistells"[517].  The Chronicle of Ramon Muntaner records the death "le jour 
2 CONC de Saint-Martin" in 1285 of "le roi Pierre" and his burial "au monastère de Sainte-Croix"[518].  A manuscript chronicle records the death "circa festum S. Martini" in 1285 of "Petrus rex Aragonum" and his burial "in ecclesia beatæ
2 CONC  Mariæ sanctarum Crucum ordinis Cisterciensis"[519].  His epitaph at Santa Cruz records his death 11 Nov 1285[520]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (2): ([before 1260]) MARÍA Nicolosa, daughter of --- (-after 1292).  Bofarull names “una Señora llamada doña María” as mother of the king’s illegitimate children “D. Jaime Perez, D. Juna y Da Beatriz que casó con D. Ramon
2 CONC  de Cardona” (no sources cited, the last named incorrect as shown below)[527].  Salazar y Acha names her “María Nicolau, muerta después de 1292” (no sources cited)[528].  Cingolani cites a document in “los registros de Cancellería
2 CONC ” which names her “María Nicolosa”, indicating that this was “un apellido claramente italiano, aunque no puedo precisar de qué parte de la península”, and suggests her relationship with the future king “algo antes de 1262, o inclu
2 CONC so de 1260” given the appointment of her son Jaime Pérez 1 Jul 1280 as ”almirante...cargo que dificilmente se justificaría en alguien demasiado joven”[529].  In another article, Cingolani cites numerous other sources relating to M
2 CONC aría Nicolosa[530]. 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ARAGON%20&%20CATALONIA.htm
2 CONT ----
