2 CONT James I the Conqueror (Catalan: Jaume el Conqueridor, Aragonese: Chaime lo Conqueridor, Spanish: Jaime el Conquistador, Occitan: Jacme lo Conquistaire; 2 February 1208 – 27 July 1276) was the King of Aragon, Count of Barcelona, an
2 CONC d Lord of Montpellier from 1213 to 1276. His long reign saw the expansion of the Crown of Aragon on all sides: into Valencia to the south, Languedoc to the north, and the Balearic Islands to the east. By a treaty with Louis IX of 
2 CONC France, he wrested the county of Barcelona from nominal French suzerainty and integrated it into his crown. His part in the Reconquista was similar in Mediterranean Spain to that of his contemporary Ferdinand III of Castile in And
2 CONC alusia. James first married, in 1221, Eleanor, daughter of Alfonso VIII of Castile and Leonora of England. Though he later had the marriage annulled, his one son by her was declared legitimate:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso (1229–1260), married Constance of Montcada, Countess of Bigorre  In 1235, James remarried to Yolanda, daughter of Andrew II of Hungary by his second wife Yolande de Courtenay. She bore him numerous children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Yolanda, also known as Violant, (1236–1301), married Alfonso X of Castile  Constance (1239–1269), married Juan Manuel, Lord of Villena, son of Ferdinand III  Peter (1240–1285), successor in Aragon, Catalonia, and Valencia  James (
2 CONC 1243–1311), successor in Balearics and Languedoc  Ferdinand (1245–1250)  Sancha (1246–1251)  Isabella (1247–1271), married Philip III of France  Mary (1248–1267), nun  Sancho, Archbishop of Toledo (1250–1279)  Eleanor (born 1251, 
2 CONC died young)  James married thirdly Teresa Gil de Vidaure, but only by a private document, and left her when she developed leprosy.
2 CONT
2 CONT James (c.1255–1285), lord of Xèrica  Peter (1259–1318), lord of Ayerbe  The children in the third marriage were recognised in his last Will as being in the line of Successon to the Throne, should the senior lines fale.
2 CONT
2 CONT James also had several lovers, both during and after his marriages, and a few bore him illegitimate sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT By Blanca d'Antillón:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferran Sanchis (or Fernando Sánchez; 1240–1275), baron of Castro  By Berenguela Fernández:
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro Fernández, baron of Híjar  By Elvira Sarroca:
