1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hijo de Pedro Martínez de Luna, junior (ya fallecido en 1282)[1]​ y Elvira Pérez de Sessé, y hermano de Juan Martínez de Luna, abuelo del Papa Luna, Benedicto XIII.[2]​l
2 CONT
2 CONT >Son of Pedro Martínez de Luna, junior (already deceased in 1282)[1] and Elvira Pérez de Sessé, and brother of Juan Martínez de Luna, grandfather of Pope Luna, Benedict XIII.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sentencia del Justicia de Aragón en la causa entre Pedro Martínez de Luna contra Eximén de Foces y los de Almuniente y Grañén. ACA,DIVERSOS,Sástago,Pergaminos,CARPETA 01,PERGAMINO Nº 37 (LIG 018/012) 1313-12-11
2 CONT
2 CONT > Judgment of the Justice of Aragon in the case between Pedro Martínez de Luna and Eximén de Foces and the Almuniente and Grañén families. ACA, MISCELLANEOUS, Sástago, Parchments, FOLDER 01, PARCHMENT NO. 37 (LIG 018/012) 1313-12-
2 CONC 11
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Cariñena’s late 14th century (with continuations) Nobiliario de Aragón records a (contemporary?) manuscript report of the family of Pedro Martínez de Luna “el Viejo”[1125], which is difficult to reconcile with the information sh
2 CONC own below.  No attempt has been made to incorporate this information into what follows.  On the other hand, Cariñena, in another section entitled “Luna de Plata. Almonacir”, includes more detailed information about the descendants
2 CONC  of “el Viejo”. some of which have been included as noted below (although these extracts are not problem free as will be seen)[1126]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Three children.  The primary source which identifies their parents has not been found.  Zurita names “...Pedro Martinez de Luna…” among the nobles who accompanied Jaime I King of Aragon to Morella in 1250, during the disputes be
2 CONC tween the king and his oldest son Infante don Alfonso[1127].  Otal y Valonga records “Pedro Martinez de Luna” among those named in the 1253 agreement between King Jaime I and Margarita Queen of Navarre[1128].  He is named “D. Pedr
2 CONC o Martinez de Luna el viejo” by Zurita in the extract quoted below which names his supposed sons.  The reliability of all these extracts is unknown and depends on the existence of two persons named Pedro Martínez de Luna “el Viejo
2 CONC ”.  . 
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ARAGONESE%20NOBILITY.htm#PedroLunaMMarchesaSaluzzoA
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.      '''   PEDRO Martínez de Luna''' “el Viejo” (-[1326], bur [Zaragoza Nuestra Señora del Pilar]).  A charter dated 9 Feb 1296 requested listed Catalonian nobles to help Fernando IV King of Castile, including “…Roderico Eximen
2 CONC i de Luna, commendatori Montis Albani, Eximino de Luna, eius filio, Petro Martini de Luna…”[1129].  Zurita names “Don Ximeno de Luna Obispo de Zaragoça, hermano de Don Pedro de Martinez y de D. Iuan Martinez de Luna, que fueron hi
2 CONC jos de D. Pedro Martinez de Luna el viejo...D. Lope Ferrench de Luna Governador de Aragon...Don Pedro Martinez de Luna...” as present “en la iglesia de San Salvador” with Jaime II King of Aragon in Aug 1300[1130].  Otal y Valonga 
2 CONC notes the death of Pedro Martínez de Luna “1326” in his genealogical table[1131].  Cariñena records “Pareçe en registro o protocolo de la execución de don Pedro Martínez de Luna, que murió en Sobradiel“, the execution dated “miérc
2 CONC oles, a 28 de mayo, era 1364” (which is presumably wildly misdated) “y que era viva doña Violante de Alagón, su muger”, adding that the testament names “don Pedro de Luna, su hijo, que sucedió en el estado, y de don Ximeno y de do
2 CONC n Artal de Luna, y de don Blasco de Luna, canónigo de Valencia, y don Dionis de Luna, canónigo de Aseo de Çaragoza, y de Lope Ximénez de Luna”, and was witnessed by “Lope Ximénez de Luna, señor de Asso, y no le llama hijo”[1132]. 
2 CONC  Cariñena records the burial of “don Pedro Martínez de Luna, marido de doña Violante de Alagón” in “Nuestra Señora del Pilar” [presumably Zaragoza] “como parece por el testamento de doña María su hija” [this indication not include
2 CONC d in Cariñena’s summary of the testament cited below][1133].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (before 13 Mar ---) '''VIOLANTE de Alagón''', daughter of ---.  Cariñena records the marriage of “don Pedro Martínez de Luna” and “doña Violante de Alagón”, adding that “y parece por registros que en la era [presumably not
2 CONC  “Spanish Era”] de 1361 [year evidently incorrect], a 13 de marzo” the couple lived together then[1134], although “1261” seems early considering the family chronology.  Her marriage and family origin are confirmed by Zurita who re
2 CONC cords that “Don Thomas Cornel”, son of “Doña Violante de Pallas, hermana de la Condesa Doña Sibilia” and “Don Ximeno Cornel hijo de D. Pedro Cornel y de D. Urraca Artal de Luna”, married “Doña Maria de Luna, hija de Don Pedro Mart
2 CONC inez de Luna y de Doña Violante de Alagon”[1135].  According to Otal y Valonga “doña Violante de Alagón, hermana de don Artal VI Señor de Santiago” was wife of “Pedro Martínez de Luna el viejo”, the couple being parents of María d
2 CONC e Luna (wife of Thomas Cornel) shown below[1136]: it is suggested below that, if that is correct, María was presumably one of their youngest children.  The passage in Cariñena relating to the execution of her husband’s testament (
2 CONC see above) indicates that Violante survived him.  Cariñena also records that “el Viejo” bequeathed “a Alcalá y a Torres de Galindo a su muger”[1137].  Cariñena discusses the Alagón family in a section which has not been studied[11
2 CONC 38].  
2 CONT
