2 CONT Violant or Violante of Aragon, also known as Yolanda of Aragon (1236 - 1301) Queen consort of Castile and León (1252-1284).
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Zaragoza, the daughter of King James I of Aragon (1213-1276) and his second wife the queen Yolande of Hungary (ca.1215-1253). Her maternal grandparents were Andrew II of Hungary and Violant of Courtenay.
2 CONT
2 CONT On December 26, 1246 she married in Valladolid with the future King Alfonso X of Castile and Leon (1221-1284). Because of her youth (Violante was only 10 years old at the time of the marriage), she produced no children for several
2 CONC  years and it was feared that she was barren. The oft-repeated claim that Alfonso almost had their marriage annulled is untrue, and they went on to have twelve children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando, died in infancy, and buried in Las Huelgas in Burgos.  Berengaria of Castile (1253-after 1284). She was betrothed to Louis, the son and heir of King Louis IX of France, but her fiance died prematurely in 1260. She entere
2 CONC d the convent in Las Huelgas, where she was living in 1284.  Beatriz of Castile (1254-1280). She married William VII, Marquess of Montferrat.  Ferdinand de la Cerda, Infante of Castile (October 23, 1255-July 25, 1275). He married 
2 CONC Blanche, the daughter of King Louis IX of France, by whom he had two children. Because he predeceased his father, his younger brother Sancho inherited the throne.  Leonor of Castile (1257-1275)  Sancho IV of Castile (May 13, 1258-
2 CONC 1295)  Constanza of Castile (1258-August 22, 1280), a nun at Las Huelgas.  Pedro of Castile (June 1260-October 10, 1283)  Juan of Castile, Lord of Valencia (March or April, 1262-June 25, 1319).  Isabella, died young.  Violante of 
2 CONC Castile (1265-1296). She married Diego Lopez de Haro  Jaime of Castile (August 1266-August 9, 1284)  She died at Roncevalles.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Wikipedia: http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yolanda_von_Ungarn Yolanda von Ungarn aus Wikipedia, der freien Enzyklopädie Wechseln zu: Navigation, Suche
2 CONT
2 CONT Yolanda von Ungarn (* 1219; † 9. Oktober 1251 in Huesca) war eine ungarische Adlige. Leben [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sie war die Tochter von Andreas II. von Ungarn aus zweiter Ehe mit Jolante Courtenay (Tochter von Kaiser Peter (Lateinisches Kaiserreich)).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sie war die zweite Ehefrau von Jakob I. von Aragonien. Sie hatten zehn Kinder. Ihre Tochter, Isabella von Aragon (* 1243; † 1271 in Cosenza) war von 1270 bis 1271 Königin von Frankreic
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Violant of Hungary (Esztergom, Kingdom of Hungary, c. 1216 – 1253) was Queen consort of James I of Aragon. She is also called Jolánta in Hungarian, Iolanda or Violant d'Hongria in Catalan and Yolanda or Violante de Hungría in Span
2 CONC ish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Violant was a daughter of Andrew II of Hungary and Violant of Courtenay.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married James I in 1235, being his second wife, and had several children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Violant of Aragon (1236-1301), queen of Castile by her marriage to Alfonso X.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Constance of Aragon (1239-1269), infanta of Castile by her marriage to Juan Manuel of Castile, son of Ferdinand III of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Peter III of Aragon (1240-1285).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. James II of Majorca (1243-1311).
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Ferdinand of Aragon (1245-1250).
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Sancha of Aragon (1246-1251).
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Isabella of Aragon (1247-1271), married Philip III of France
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Maria of Aragon (1248-1267), nun.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. Sancho, Archbishop of Toledo (1250-1275)
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. Elionor of Aragon (1251-?, young)
2 CONT
2 CONT Violant and her daughter Sancha's remains are at the monastery of Santa Maria de Vallbona in Lleida, Catalonia.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Her marriage produced 4 sons & 5 daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried ~ Tomb is white stone on top with a cross. The lower part is darker with a symbol. It sits on top of two small pillars which has a cross on each.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Yolante Arpád married Jaime I, Rey de Aragón, son of Pedro II, Rey de Aragón and Marie de Montpellier, on 8 September 1235. She died circa October 1251.
2 CONT      She was the daughter of Andreas II Arpád, King of Hungary and Yolande de Courtney.1,2 She was also known as Yolande (?). Children of Yolante Arpád and Jaime I, Rey de Aragón
2 CONT
