1 OCCU Infanta de Castilla y Reina Consorte de Aragón
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT There is some contention that she is the face of the Mona Lisa. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_of_Castile,_Queen_of_Aragon
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Isabella of Castile (1283–1328), Viscountess of Limoges, was the first Queen consort of James II of Aragon and the second wife of John III, Duke of Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was born in Toro, the eldest daughter of Sancho IV of Castile and María de Molina. On 1 December 1291, Isabella married James II of Aragon in the city of Soria. The bride was only eight years old and the groom twenty-four
2 CONC . The marriage was never consummated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho IV died on 25 April 1295. James chose to change his alliances and take advantage of the turmoil inside Castile. He had their wedding annulled and proceeded to marry Blanche of Anjou, second daughter of Charles II of Naples 
2 CONC and Maria Arpad of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella remained unwed for about a decade. In 1310, at Burgos, she married her second husband John III, Duke of Brittany. There were no children from this marriage. She was buried at Priere.
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CASTILE.htm#Isabeldied1328
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         Infanta doña ISABEL de Castilla y León (Toro 1283-24 Jul 1328).  The Livro Velho names "a infanta D. Isabel…a infanta D. Brites" as the daughters of King Sancho IV and his wife[1142].  The Chronicon Domini Joannis Emman
2 CONC uelis records the marriage in Dec 1293 of “Regem Aragonum” and “Infantissam Dnam Elisabeth, filiam Regis Dni Sancii, in Soria”[1143].  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña records the marriage of King Jaime and "el Rey de Castiella…
2 CONC su filla…Maria" (presumably an error for Isabel) despite their consanguinity, commenting that the marriage was never consummated and was annulled[1144].  Señora de Guadalajara.  The Papal dispensation for the marriage of "Johanni 
2 CONC primogenito...Arturi ducs Britanniæ vicecomitis Lemovicensis" and “Isabellæ natæ claræ memoriæ Sancii Castellæ regis”, dated 21 Jun 1310, records her 3o consanguinity with “Isabellis nata...filii...Caroli nati...Philippi regis Fra
2 CONC ncorum prior uxor tua”[1145].  Vicomtesse de Limoges, by grant of her second husband.  "Johannes ducis Britannie primogenitus vicecomes Lemovicensis" granted “vicecomitatum Lemovicensem” to “Ysabellam...Sancii quondam regis Castel
2 CONC le et Legionis filiam” by charter dated Mar 1312[1146].  The testament of "doña Maria…reyna de Castilla, de Leon, y señora de Molina", dated 29 Jun 1321, provides for "…la Infanta doña Isabel mi fija…Infante don Felipe mio fijo…do
2 CONC ña Maria mia sobrina muger que fue del Infante d. Juan"[1147].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Betrothed''' to ALFONSO de la Cerda de Castilla, son of Infante don FERNANDO "él de la Cerda" de Castilla y León & his wife Blanche de France (Valladolid 1270-Piedrahita shortly after 23 Dec 1324).  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly (Soria Dec 1291 or Dec 1293, unconsummated, annulled 1295 for consanguinity) as his first wife, JAIME II King of Aragon, son of PEDRO III "el Grande" King of Aragon & his wife Constanza di Sicilia [Hohenstaufen] (V
2 CONC alencia 10 Aug 1267-Barcelona 5 Nov 1327, bur Barcelona church of San Francisco, transferred to Monastery of Santa Cruz, prov Tarragona).  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly (Burgos 1310) JEAN de Bretagne, son of ARTHUR II Duke of Brittany & his first wife Marie de Limoges (Châteauroux, Indre 8 Mar 1286-Caen, Calvados 30 Apr 1341, bur Ploërmel, Morbihan, Chapelle des Carmes).  Vicomte
2 CONC  de Limoges 1301.  He succeeded his father in 1312 as JEAN III "le Bon" Duke of Brittany. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
2 CONT -------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Lithuanian: Izabelė iš Kastilijos de Castilla y León, Aragono Karalienė, Spanish: Isabel de Castilla y Molina de Castilla y León, reina consorte de Aragón, Italian: Isabella di Castiglia de Castilla y León, reina consorte de Aragó
2 CONC n
