1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ferdinand de la Cerda (1275–1322)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando de la Cerda (1275-1322) was the younger son of Ferdinand de la Cerda, Infante of Castile and his wife Blanche of France. His paternal grandparents were Alfonso V of Castile and Violant of Aragon. His maternal grandparents
2 CONC  were Louis IX of France and Marguerite of Provence. His elder brother was Alfonso de la Cerda.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]The fight for the throne
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando's father died before inheriting Castile, his elder brother should have ascended the throne on his grandfather's death. In 1282 their uncle, Sancho assembled a coalition of nobles to declare for him against Fernando's elde
2 CONC r brother, then took control of the kingdom when Alfonso X of Castile died in 1284. This was all against the wishes of the boys grandfather, but Sancho was crowned in Toledo nevertheless.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho was recognised and supported by the majority of the nobility and the cities, but a sizable minority opposed him throughout his reign and worked for Alfonso and Fernando. One of the leaders of the opposition was Don Juan, hi
2 CONC s uncle, who united to his cause the lord of Vizcaya, Lope Díaz III de Haro. Sancho responded by executing the lord of Vizcaya and incarcerating his uncle.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando was married to Juana Núñez de Lara, who is also known as Lady of Lara. The couple had four daughters,
2 CONT
2 CONT John Núñez de Lara (1313-1350), married Maria de Haro
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche Núñez de Lara (1311-1347), married Juan Manuel, Duke of Peñafiel and was mother of Juana Manuel of Castile, consort of Henry II of Castile and mother of John I of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Núñez de Lara, a nun
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Núñez de Lara, married Charles II of Alençon and was mother of Charles III of Alençon.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Fernando de la Cerda (1275–1322) was the younger son of Ferdinand de la Cerda, Infante of Castile and his wife Blanche of France. His paternal grandparents were Alfonso X of Castile and Violant of Aragon. His maternal grandparents
2 CONC  were Louis IX of France and Marguerite of Provence. His elder brother was Alfonso de la Cerda.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand de la Cerda, Lord consort of Lara Born 1275 Died Shortly after 1 June 1322 Noble family House de la Cerda Spouse(s) Juana Núñez de Lara Father Ferdinand de la Cerda, Infante of Castile Mother Blanche of France
2 CONT
2 CONT The fight for the throne
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando's father died before inheriting Castile, his elder brother should have ascended the throne on his grandfather's death. In 1282 their uncle, Sancho assembled a coalition of nobles to declare for him against Fernando's elde
2 CONC r brother, then took control of the kingdom when Alfonso X of Castile died in 1284. This was all against the wishes of the boys' grandfather, but Sancho was crowned in Toledo nevertheless.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms of the House of La Cerda before 1376 Sancho was recognised and supported by the majority of the nobility and the cities, but a sizable minority opposed him throughout his reign and worked for Alfonso and Fernando. One of the 
2 CONC leaders of the opposition was Don Juan, his uncle, who united to his cause the lord of Vizcaya, Lope Díaz III de Haro. Sancho responded by executing the lord of Vizcaya and incarcerating his uncle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue	
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando was married to Juana Núñez de Lara, who is also known as Lady of Lara. The couple had three daughters and a son,
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Núñez de Lara (1313–1350), married Maria de Haro Blanche Núñez de Lara (1311–1347), married Juan Manuel, Prince of Villena and was mother of Juana Manuel of Castile, consort of Henry II of Castile and mother of John I of Cast
2 CONC ile. Margaret Núñez de Lara, a nun Maria Núñez de Lara, married Charles II of Alençon and was mother of Charles III of Alençon.
