1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Constanza Manuel de Villena y Barcelona
2 CONT
2 CONT De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre: [http://ca.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constan%C3%A7a_Manuel CA], [http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constan%C3%A7a_Manuel DE], [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constance_of_Pe%C3%B1afiel EN], [http://es.wikipe
2 CONC dia.org/wiki/Constanza_Manuel_de_Villena_y_Barcelona '''ES'''], [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constance_de_Castille_%281323-1345%29 FR], [http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Costanza_Manuel IT], [http://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%B3%E3
2 CONC %83%B3%E3%82%B9%E3%82%BF%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B5%E3%83%BB%E3%83%9E%E3%83%8C%E3%82%A8%E3%83%AB%E3%83%BB%E3%83%87%E3%83%BB%E3%82%AB%E3%82%B9%E3%83%86%E3%82%A3%E3%83%BC%E3%83%AA%E3%83%A3 JA], [http://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constance_Manuel_
2 CONC van_Castili%C3%AB NL], [http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konstancja_Manuel PL], [http://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constan%C3%A7a_Manuel PT], [http://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%B0_%D0%9C%D0%B0
2 CONC %D0%BD%D1%83%D1%8D%D0%BB%D1%8C RU]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Constanza Manuel de Villena''' (Castillo de Garcimuñoz 1316- Santarém 13-11-1345). Fue reina consorte de Castilla y de Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hija del infante don Juan Manuel y de la infanta Constanza de Aragón, que a su vez fue hija del rey de Aragón Jaime II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Con tan sólo nueve años, su padre la desposó con el rey Alfonso XI de Castilla que a la sazón contaba catorce años y acababa de alcanzar la mayoría de edad.
2 CONT
2 CONT El matrimonio de Constanza y Alfonso fue ratificado por las cortes de Valladolid (28-11-1325), pero dada la minoría de edad de Constanza el matrimonio no llegó a consumarse, aunque Constanza pasó a titularse reina de Castilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1327 fue repudiada, al estar interesado Alfonso XI en el matrimonio con la infanta María, hija del rey de Portugal, Alfonso IV a fin de estrechar lazos con este reino. Posteriormente quedó recluida en el castillo de Toro bajo e
2 CONC l cuidado de su aya Teresa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Juan Manuel reclamó su hija al rey Alfonso XI y éste se negó a entregársela, por lo que Don Juan Manuel le declaró la guerra a Alfonso XI en sucesivas ocasiones hasta que éste se la devolvió en 1328 después de firmar la paz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Juan Manuel la promete en 1331 al heredero de Portugal, el infante Pedro. Dado que Constanza está cercada en Castillo de Garcimuñoz, Pedro se casa con ella por poderes en el Castillo de Garcimuñoz (28-3-1336). Posteriormente, 
2 CONC una vez que obtiene el permiso del rey Alfonso para viajar a Portugal, se casa en Lisboa el 24 de agosto de 1339.
2 CONT
2 CONT En su séquito iba la noble gallega Inés de Castro de la que se enamoraría don Pedro y con la que se casaría en secreto a la muerte de Constanza.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constanza tuvo tres hijos con el infante Pedro: Luís, que nació en 1340 y que solo viviría 8 días; María, (1342-1367) que se casaría con el infante Fernando de Aragón, marqués de Tortosa e hijo del rey Alfonso IV de Aragón; y Fern
2 CONC ando (1345-1383), que sería el futuro rey Fernando I de Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pocos días después del nacimiento de este su tercer hijo, el 13 de noviembre de 1345, Constanza murió de pauperio en Santarem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constanza Manuel e Inés de Castro son las protagonistas femeninas de la obra teatral de Alejandro Casona Corona de amor y muerte que fue estrenada en el teatro Odeón de Buenos Aires en 1955. Inés de Castro también es la protagonis
2 CONC ta de la obra Reinar después de morir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliografía
2 CONT
2 CONT PRETEL MARÍN, Aurelio. Don Juan Manuel, señor de la llanura. Albacete, 1982.
2 CONT
2 CONT PRETEL MARÍN, Aurelio. El señorío de Villena en el siglo XIV. Albacete, 1998. ISBN 84-87136-86-9
2 CONT
2 CONT SALAS PARRILLA, Miguel. Alarcón, Belmonte y Garcimuñoz. Tres castillos del señorío de Villena en la provincia de Cuenca. Madrid, 1997. ISBN 84-607-2598-7
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Penafiel
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Peñafiel or Constance Manuel of Castile (b. 1315-1323, d. November 13, 1345) was the daughter of Juan Manuel, Duke of Peñafiel (1282-1348), called "el escritor" (the writer), Duke of Peñafiel, and his second wife Cons
2 CONC tance of Aragon. She was Queen consort of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT While still a child she became the first wife of future King Alfonso XI of Castile (1311-50), also a child at the time, in Valladolid. The marriage was annulled in 1327.
2 CONT
2 CONT She became the second wife of King Pedro I of Portugal (1320-67) on August 24, 1339 in Lisbon. They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis (February 27, 1340 - March 6 1340).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary (April 6, 1343 - app. 1367), Married to Ferdinand, Prince of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand I of Portugal (October 31, 1345 - October 29, 1383), 9th King of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died and is buried in Santarém, Portugal in 1349.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT D. Constança Manuel
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta de Castela, filha de D. Constança de Aragão e do duque da localidade castelhana de Penafiel, D. João Manuel, D. Constança Manuel terá nascido em 1320 e falecido em 1345. Foi casada com o infante D. Pedro - que seria o futu
2 CONC ro rei D. Pedro I, o Cruel(também cognominado o Justiceiro, o Bravo ou o Vingativo). Por alturas do seu casamento, D. Afonso IV, pai de D. Pedro, doou-lhe Viseu, Alenquer e Montemor-o-Novo.
2 CONT
2 CONT D. Pedro (que somente seria rei em 1357) casou-se com D. Constança por procuração a 28 de Fevereiro de 1336, em Évora. No entanto D. Constança só chegou a Portugal a 7 de Julho de 1340, pois Afonso XI de Castela, a quem estava pro
2 CONC metida, reteve-a prisioneira na povoação castelhana de Toro. Este facto originou uma guerra que terminou com a vitória de Portugal e a vinda de D. Constança.
2 CONT
2 CONT Como dama de companhia trouxe Inês de Castro, uma fidalga galega, pela qual D. Pedro se apaixonou e da qual acabaria por ter quatro filhos. D. Constança apercebeu-se da atracção do marido pela dama galega e para impedir que alguma
2 CONC  ligação surgisse entre eles pediu a Inês de Castro para ser madrinha de D. Luís, seu segundo filho. Assim, além da condenação do adultério pelo Direito Canónico, havia também o impedimento da ligação carnal entre compadres, unido
2 CONC s por laços de sangue. No entanto, a morte de D. Luís, uma semana depois, deitaria por terra estes planos.
2 CONT
2 CONT D. Pedro e D. Constança tiveram três filhos: D. Maria, nascida a 6 de Abril de 1342 em Évora, que se casou em 1354 com D. Fernando de Aragão e morreu depois de 1363 em Aveiro, sendo sepultada no Mosteiro de Santa Clara em Coimbra;
2 CONC  D. Luís, afilhado de Inês de Castro e que morreu uma semana depois de ter nascido, em 1344; e D. Fernando (futuro rei D. Fernando I), nascido em Coimbra a 1 de Outubro de 1345 e falecido em 1383.
2 CONT
2 CONT D. Constança morreu em 1345 (alguns advogam a data de 1349), pouco tempo após o nascimento do seu último filho. O seu túmulo encontra-se no Museu do Carmo, em Lisboa.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Constanza Manuel of Villena (1315/1323 – 13 November 1345) was the daughter of Juan Manuel, Prince of Villena (1282–1348), called "el escritor" (the writer), Duke of Peñafiel, and his second wife Constance of Aragon, a daughter of
2 CONC  James II of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT While still a child she became the first wife of future King Alfonso XI of Castile (1311–50), also a child at the time, in Valladolid. The marriage was annulled in 1327.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life and first marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance was a Castilian noblewoman, whose exact date of birth is unknown, occurring sometime between 1315 and 1323. Constance was a paternal great-granddaughter of King Ferdinand I of Castile. Her maternal grandparents were Jame
2 CONC s II of Aragon and his second wife Blanche of Anjou.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Valladolid on 28 November 1325, the young Constance married Alfonso XI of Castile, they were married for only two years when Alfonso had the marriage dissolved and remarried to Maria of Portugal, who gave him a son, Peter of Ca
2 CONC stile.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance was imprisoned in a castle in Toro while her father waged war against Alfonso XI until 1329. Eventually, the two reached a peaceful accord after mediation by Juan del Campo, Bishop of Oviedo; this secured Constance's rel
2 CONC ease from prison. Second marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Afonso IV quickly learned that his daughter Maria was being mistreated by her husband King Alfonso (Constance's ex-husband). Constance's father had been rebuffed by the king when she was rejected in favor of the Portuguese infanta
2 CONC . Feeling as though his daughter was being dishonored, Afonso was glad to enter into an alliance with Juan Manuel and married his son and heir, Peter, to Constance. They married on 24 August 1339 in Lisbon.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Constance arrived in Portugal, Inês de Castro, the daughter of an aristocratic Castilian land-owner, accompanied her as her lady-in-waiting. Peter fell in love with Inês very quickly, and the two conducted an affair that last
2 CONC ed until Constance's death in 1345. The scandal of this affair caused Afonso to banish Inês from court, but this did not end the relationship, and the two began living together in secret.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance died on the 13 November 1345, weeks after giving birth to her son and future King of Portugal, Fernando. She was buried four years later in Santarém, Portugal. Her husband presumably married Inês after Constance's death;
2 CONC  however, Inês was later murdered on the orders of King Afonso. Peter became King twelve years after Constance's death in 1357.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had three children: Children
2 CONT
2 CONT     Maria (1342 – aft. 1375), married to Infante Ferdinand of Aragon, son of Alfonso IV of Aragon.
2 CONT     Louis (1344).
2 CONT     Ferdinand I of Portugal (31 October 1345 – 29 October 1383), 9th King of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestry [show]Ancestors of Constanza Manuel References
2 CONT
2 CONT     Castile and Leon, Medieval Lands
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT     Pretel Marín; Rodríguez Llopis, Miguel (1989). El señorío de Villena en el siglo XIV (PDF) (in Spanish). Albacete: Instituto de Estudios Albacetenses «Don Juan Manuel», Excma. Diputación de Albacete. ISBN 84-600-2900-X.
2 CONT     Rodrigues Oliveira, Ana (2010). Rainhas medievais de Portugal. Dezassete mulheres, duas dinastias, quatro séculos de História (in Portuguese). Lisbon: A esfera dos livros. ISBN 978-989-626-261-7.
