1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ferdinand of Majorca
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT A denier tournois minted by Ferdinand during his brief rule in Achaea
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand of Majorca (Catalan: Ferran de Mallorca) (1278 – July 5, 1316), Infante of Majorca, was the third son of James II of Majorca, Viscount of Aumelas and Lord of Frontignan.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was sent by Frederick III of Sicily to take command of the Catalan Company in Frederick's name, but was rebuffed by Bernat de Rocefort, one of their leaders. On his return with the chronicler Ramón Muntaner, he was captured by 
2 CONC the Venetians at Negroponte. He had been released by 1310, when he distinguished himself at the siege of Almería by killing the son of the King of Guadix.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1313, he returned to Sicily to take part in the war then in hand with the Angevins and was created Lord of Catania. Margaret of Villehardouin was then in Sicily, seeking to advance her claim to the Principality of Achaea. She g
2 CONC ave her daughter Isabelle de Sabran to Ferdinand in marriage and resigned Mategrifon and her claim on Achaea to the couple, who were married in Messina. Margaret died in March 1315 in her castle of Akova in the Morea, and her daug
2 CONC hter on May 7, 1315 in Catania, shortly after bearing a son, James III of Majorca.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after her death, Ferdinand set out with a small company for the Morea to uphold the claim now held by his son. He seized Clarenza in June 1315 and briefly took control of the Morea. In the autumn of 1315 he took a second w
2 CONC ife, Isabella of Ibelin, daughter of the Seneschal of Cyprus. However, his rival claimant Matilda of Hainaut, and her husband Louis of Burgundy returned to the Morea in the spring of 1316 with Venetian aid. Ferdinand's expected ai
2 CONC d from Majorca and Sicily was tardy, as was that of the Catalan Company from Athens. Facing superior numbers, he was killed at the Battle of Manolada on July 5, 1316. He was succeeded as heir presumptive of Majorca by his elder so
2 CONC n, James, and as Viscount of Aumelas by his posthumous son Ferdinand.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Ramón Muntaner, translated into English by Lady Goodenough
