2 CONT King Philip IV "the Fair" of France - was born in 1268 in Fontainebleau, Seine-Et-Marne, France and died on 29 Nov 1314 in Fontainebleau, Seine-Et-Marne, France and was buried in Saint Denis, France . He was the son of King Philip
2 CONC  III "the Bold" of France and Princess Isabel of Aragon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Philip married Jeanne of Navarre on 16 Aug 1284. Jeanne was born Jan 1271/1272 in France. She was the daughter of King Henri "le Gros" of Navarre and Queen Blanche Artois. She died on 2 Apr 1305 in France . 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Philip - Philip "Le Bel" called Philip the Fair was born in the year 1268, 0ne hundred and fifty years after the formation of the Knights Templar and was King of France from 1285-1314. How could someone as corrupt as he, be c
2 CONC alled by the people, Philip the Fair? The term "The Fair" was a reference to Philip IV's good looks, being tall and handsome with long blonde hair and blue eyes. Philip Le Bel, in contrast to his pleasing looks, was a cold and sec
2 CONC retive man who had strong wishes for France to be the head of the empire. In order to accomplish this plan he would need great financial resources (which the Templars possessed) and a week and subservient Papal Throne. Philip is w
2 CONC ell known for his battles with Boniface VIII (see chronology below) At one point Philip publicly burned Boniface VIII's Bull Unam Sanctam which gave the Pope absolute supremacy over everyone. Children: (Quick Family Chart) 
2 CONT
2 CONT i.  King Louis X of France was born on 4 Oct 1289 in Paris, France and died on 5 Jun 1316 in Vincennes, France and was buried in St. Denis, France . 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Louis - married Marguerite of Burgandy and then Clemence of Hungary.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ii.  King Philip V of France was born about 1294 in Lyons, France and died on 3 Jan 1322 in Longchamp, France and was buried in St. Denis, France . 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Philip - married Joan of Burgandy.  
2 CONT
2 CONT iii.  Queen Isabella of France was born in 1292, lived in Paris, France and died on 22 Aug 1358 in Hertford Castle, Hertford, England and was buried in Grey Friars, Church, London, England . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Isabella married King Edward II of England on 25 Jan 1307/1308 in Boulogne, Pas-de-Calais, France. King Edward was born on 25 Apr 1274 in Carnarvon Castle, Carnarvon , Wales. He was the son of King Edward I "Longshanks" Plan
2 CONC tagenet and Princess Leonor of Castile and Leon. He died on 21 Sep 1327 in Berkeley Castle,Gloucester,Gloucester,England . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Isabella - When Charles Iv of France seized Edward's territories in that country, the English king sent Charles' sister Isabella who after Gaveston's death had managed to bear Edward four children, including the future Edwar
2 CONC d Iii, who now accompanied her to effect an amicable arrangement. She despised her husband, hated the Despensers and now fell in love with Roger Mortimer who, condemned to life imprisonment for rebellion, had escaped from the Towe
2 CONC r in 1324 and fled to the French court. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hertford Castle 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was the Normans who first built a castle in Hertford after the battle of Hastings, although the oldest walls still standing today were built as part of Henry II's strengthening works in the 1170's. Ever since, the castle has be
2 CONC en the centre of the town and in continual use.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was captured by the French in the 13th century, became a royal palace in the 14th century, and was home to Parliament and the Law Courts when Elizabethan London was gripped by Plague in the 1500's. Shortly after becoming King, 
2 CONC Charles I granted Hertford Castle to William Cecil, the Earl of Salibury (whose descendants still own it) and since 1911 it has been used as council offices.  
2 CONT
2 CONT See Plantagenet family for children.
2 CONT
2 CONT iv.  King Charles IV of France was born about 1294 and died on 1 Feb 1328 in Vincennes, France and was buried in St. Denis, France . 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Charles - married Blanche of Burgandy in 1307, Marie of Luxemburg in 1322 and Joan of Evreux in 1325.  
2 CONT
