2 CONT As chief councillor during the reign of his nephew Louis X, Charles brought about the fall of the famous financial adviser Enguerrand de Marigny. After Louis's death in June 1316, Charles desired the throne, but he gave way to ano
2 CONC ther nephew, Philip V, who died in 1322. Charles had considerable influence with his nephew Charles IV, the new king, and was sent by him on a successful campaign into Guyenne in 1324. He had previously commanded French armies in 
2 CONC Guyenne in 1295 and led them in Flanders in 1297, 1299, 1300, 1303, and 1314. His son, Philip VI (king from 1328 to 1350), was the first of the Valois line.
2 CONT
2 CONT Copyright © 1994-2001 Encyclopædia Britannica, Inc.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT "Moyennement intelligent, démesurément ambitieux et passablement avide, Charles de Valois collectionne les principautés". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dixit wapedia!
2 CONT
2 CONT Et pas seulement les principautés! Par sa seconde épouse  Catherine de Courtenay  il fut aussi, au moins théoriquement, empereur de Constantinople et, par sa mère roi "titulaire" d'Aragon (il n'arriva cependant pas à sa faire "tit
2 CONC ulariser"!).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mais son frère Philippe (le Bel) appréciait ses talents militaires.
2 CONT
2 CONT On trouve aussi mention d'un décès à Nogent le Roi.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles of Valois (March 12, 1270–December 16, 1325) was the fourth son of Philip III of France and Isabella of Aragon. His mother was a daughter of James I of Aragon and Yolande of Hungary. He was a member of the House of Capet a
2 CONC nd founded the House of Valois. In 1284, he was created Count of Valois (as Charles I) by his father and, in 1297, he was created Count of Anjou (as Charles III) by his brother Philip IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles was the father of Philip VI, and paternal uncle to three kings (Louis X, Phillip V, and Charles IV). In 1284, he was given the crown of Aragon by Pope Martin IV, who declared an Aragonese Crusade. In 1285, he gained the ti
2 CONC tle of Count of Valois, and Count of Anjou and Maine in 1290.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his life, he unsuccessfully sought the rule of four other kingdoms: Aragon, Sicily, the Latin Empire and the Holy Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles de Valois was married three times.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first marriage, in 1290, was to Marguerite of Anjou and Maine (1274–1299), daughter of King Charles II of Naples. They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (1292–1309). Married Jean III, Duke of Brittany. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip VI, first King of the Valois Dynasty. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne of Valois (1294–1342). Married William I, Count of Hainaut and had issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite of Valois (1295–1342). Married Guy I of Blois-Châtillon, Count of Blois, and had issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles II, Count of Alençon (1297 – August 26, 1346 at the Battle of Crécy). Married first Jeanne de Joigny and second Marie de la Cerda and had issue from the second marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine of Valois (b. 1299, died young). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1302 he remarried to Catherine I of Courtenay (1274–1308), titular Empress of Constantinople. They had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT John, Count of Chartres (1302–1308). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine II of Valois, Princess of Achaea, titular Empress of Constantinople (1303–1346). She married Philip I d'Anjou, Prince of Taranto and had issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne de Valois (1304–1363). Married Count Robert III of Artois and had issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel of Valois (1305–1349), Abbess of Fontevrault. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, in 1308, he married Mahaut of Chatillon (1293–1358), daughter of Guy III of Châtillon, Count of Saint Pol. They had also four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie of Valois (1309–1332). Married Charles, Duke of Calabria and had issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of Valois (1313 – August 26, 1388). She married Peter I, Duke of Bourbon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Valois (1317–1348). She married Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor. Sometimes called "Marguerite". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis, Count of Chartres (1318–1328)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles of Valois (March 12, 1270 – December 16, 1325) was the fourth son of Philip III of France and Isabella of Aragon. His mother was a daughter of James I of Aragon and Yolande of Hungary. He was a member of the House of Capet
2 CONC  and founded the House of Valois. In 1284, he was created Count of Valois (as Charles I) by his father and, in 1297, he was created Count of Anjou (as Charles III) by his brother Philip IV
2 CONT
