1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Brittany,_Earl_of_Richmond
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Brittany or Jean de Bretagne (c. 1266 – 17 January 1334) 4th Earl of Richmond, was an English nobleman and a member of the Ducal house of Brittany, the House of Dreux. He entered royal service in England under his uncle Ed
2 CONC ward I, and also served Edward II. On 15 October 1306 he received his father's title of Earl of Richmond. He was named Guardian of Scotland in the midst of England's conflicts with the Scotland and in 1311 Lord Ordainer during the
2 CONC  baronial rebellion against Edward II.
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Brittany served England as a soldier and as a diplomat but was otherwise politically inactive in comparison to other Earls of his time. He was a capable diplomat, valued by both Edward I and Edward II for his negotiating s
2 CONC kills. John was never married, and upon his death his title and estates fell to his nephew, John III, Duke of Brittany. Although he was generally loyal to his first cousin Edward II during the times of baronial opposition, he even
2 CONC tually supported the coup of Isabella and Mortimer. After Edward II abdicated in favor of his son Edward III of England he retired to his estates in France, and died in his native Brittany in 1334 with no known issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT John was the second surviving son of John II, Duke of Brittany, and his wife Beatrice, who together had three sons and three daughters who survived to adulthood. Beatrice was the daughter of Henry III of England, which made John t
2 CONC he nephew of Henry's son and heir Edward I. His father held the title of Earl of Richmond, but was little involved in English political affairs. John was raised at the English court together with Edward I's son Henry, who died in 
2 CONC 1274. He participated in tournaments in his youth. but never distinguished himself in his early roles as a soldier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Service to Edward I
2 CONT
2 CONT When in 1294 the French king confiscated King Edward's Duchy of Aquitaine, John travelled to France but failed to take Bordeaux. During Easter of 1295 he had to flee the town of Rions. In January 1297 he shared defeat at the siege
2 CONC  of Bellegarde with Henry de Lacy, Earl of Lincoln. After this defeat, he returned to England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his poor results in France he remained highly regarded by his uncle King Edward I, who treated him almost as a son. After his return to England John became involved in the Scottish Wars. He was probably at the Battle of Fa
2 CONC lkirk in 1298. He was certainly at the Siege of Caerlaverock in 1300. The nobles who joined Edward I at the Siege of Caerlaverock, including John of Brittany, were commemorated in the Roll of Caerlaverock which named each noble an
2 CONC d described their banner.  In this roll, the banner and description of John of Brittany immediately follows that of his uncle King Edward I.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, the Duke of Brittany, died in 1305, and was succeeded as Duke by John's elder brother, Arthur. The following year Edward I invested John with his father's other title, Earl of Richmond. In addition Edward I appointed h
2 CONC im Guardian of Scotland, a position which was confirmed upon the accession of Edward II in 1307.
2 CONT
2 CONT Service to Edward II
2 CONT
2 CONT The English court viewed John of Brittany as a trusted diplomat. He was a skilled negotiator, and his French connections were a useful asset. By 1307 he was also one of the kingdom's oldest earls. As the relationship between Edwar
2 CONC d II and his nobility deteriorated, Richmond remained loyal to the king; in 1309 he went on an embassy to Pope Clement V on behalf of Edward's favourite Piers Gaveston. John was allegedly Gaveston's close personal friend, and did 
2 CONC not share the antagonistic attitudes held by certain other earls.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Ordainers
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1310 the relationship between Edward II and his earls had deteriorated to the point where a committee of earls took control of government from the king. The earls disobeyed a royal order not to carry arms to parliament, and in 
2 CONC full military attire presented a demand to the king for the appointment of a commission of reform. At the heart of the deteriorating situation was the nobles' opinions about Edward II's relationship with Gaveston, and his reputedl
2 CONC y outrageous behavior. On 16 March 1310, the king agreed to the appointment of Ordainers, who were to be in charge of the reform of the royal household. John of Brittany was one of eight earls appointed to this committee of 21, re
2 CONC ferred to as the Lords Ordainers. He was among the Ordainers considered loyal to Edward II and was also by this time one of the older remaining earls.
2 CONT
2 CONT John then travelled to France for diplomatic negotiations, before returning to England. Gaveston, was exiled by the Ordainers but later made an illicit return. Gaveston was killed in June 1312 by Thomas of Lancaster and other nobl
2 CONC es. It fell upon John of Bretagne, together with Gilbert de Clare, Earl of Gloucester, to reconcile the two parties after this event. In 1313 he followed Edward II on a state visit to France, and thereafter generally remained a tr
2 CONC usted subject. In 1318 he witnessed the Treaty of Leake, which restored Edward to full power.
2 CONT
2 CONT War with Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1320 he again accompanied Edward II to France, and the next year he carried out peace negotiations with the Scots. When in 1322 Thomas of Lancaster rebelled and was defeated at the Battle of Boroughbridge, Richmond was present 
2 CONC at his trial, and when Lancaster was sentenced to death. After this, the English invaded Scotland only to have their army starved when Robert the Bruce burned the country before them. The Bruce brought his army into England. Bruce
2 CONC  crossed the Solway Firth in the west, making his way in a south-easterly direction towards Yorkshire;he brought many troops recruited in Argyll and the Isles. The boldness and speed of the attack soon exposed Edward II to danger,
2 CONC  even in his own land. On his return from Scotland, the king had taken up residence at Rievaulx Abbey with Queen Isabella. His peace was interrupted when the Scots made a sudden and unexpected approach in mid-October. All that sto
2 CONC od between them and a royal prize was a large English force under the command of John de Bretagne. John had taken up position on Scawton Moor, between Rievaulx and Byland Abbey. To dislodge John from his strong position on the hig
2 CONC h ground Bruce used the same tactics that brought victory at the earlier Battle of the Pass of Brander. As Moray and Douglas charged uphill a party of Highlanders scaled the cliffs on the English flank and charged downhill into Jo
2 CONC hn of Brittany's rear. Resistance crumbled and the Battle of Old Byland turned into a rout. John himself was taken prisoner and given a tongue lashing for his cowardice by Robert the Bruce. John remained in captivity until 1324, w
2 CONC hen he was released for a ransom of 14,000 marks.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his release, he continued his diplomatic activities in Scotland and France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Final Years
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1325 John of Brittany made a final return to France, where for the first time he made himself a clear opponent of the Edward II. His lands in England were confiscated by the Crown. In France, John aligned himself with Que
2 CONC en Isabella, Edward II's wife, who had been sent on a diplomatic mission to France, and had disobeyed her husband's orders to return to England. Later when Edward II was forced to abdicate and his son Edward III ascended to the En
2 CONC glish throne, John of Brittany's English lands were restored. He spent his last years on his French estates, and he remained largely cut off from English political affairs. He died on 17 January 1334, and was buried in the church 
2 CONC of the Franciscans in Nantes.
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Brittany never married and as far as is known had no issue. He was succeeded as Earl of Richmond by his nephew John (Arthur's son).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_de_Bretagne,_1st_Earl_of_Richmond
