1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Guy II of Châtillon, Count of Saint-Pol
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guy_II_of_Ch%C3%A2tillon English - '''Wikipedia....''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy II of Châtillon, Count of Saint-Pol (died 1289), French nobleman, was a younger son of Hugh I of Châtillon and Marie of Avesnes.
2 CONT
2 CONT While his elder brother John I of Châtillon succeeded to their mother's County of Blois, Guy was given their father's county of Saint-Pol-sur-Ternoise at his death in 1248.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 16, 1255, he married Matilda of Brabant (d. 1288), daughter of Henry II, Duke of Brabant and Marie of Hohenstaufen, and thereafter was a supporter of his brother-in-law Henry III against Guelders. They had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Hugh II of Châtillon (d. 1307), Count of Saint Pol and later Count of Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Guy III of Châtillon (d. 1317), Count of Saint Pol
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Jacques I of Leuze-Châtillon (d. 1302, Battle of the Golden Spurs), first of the lords of Leuze, married Catherine de Condé and had issue; his descendants brought Condé, Carency, etc. into the House of Bourbon.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Beatrix (d. 1304), married John I of Brienne, Count of Eu
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.Jeanne, married Guillaume III de Chauvigny, Lord of Châteauroux
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.Gertrude, married Florent, Lord of Mechelen (French: Malines)
2 CONT
2 CONT A faithful vassal of France, he joined the Eighth Crusade (1270) and the ill-fated Crusade of Aragón of Philip the Bold.
