2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Alfonso II, Count of Provence and Gersende II de Sabran, Countess of Forcalquier. Her maternal grandparents were Thomas I of Savoy and Marguerite of Geneva, daughter of William I of Geneva and Beatri
2 CONC ce de Faucigny.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her younger sisters were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor of Provence became the Queen consort of Henry III of England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sanchia of Provence who became the Queen consort of Richard, Earl of Cornwall and rival King of the Germans. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice of Provence who was the Queen consort of Charles I of Sicily 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 May 1234 at the age of thirteen, Marguerite became the Queen consort of Louis IX of France, by whom she had eleven children. She was crowned on the following day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite, like her sisters, was noted for her beauty, she was said to be "pretty with dark hair and fine eyes",[2] and in the early years of their marriage she and Louis enjoyed a warm relationship. Her Franciscan confessor, Wil
2 CONC liam de St. Pathus, related that on cold nights Marguerite would place a robe around Louis' shoulders, when her deeply religious husband rose to pray. Another anecdote recorded by St. Pathus related that Marguerite felt that Louis
2 CONC ' plain clothing was unbecoming to his royal dignity, to which Louis replied that he would dress as she wished, if she dressed as he wished. However, the chronicler Joinville noted with disapproval that Louis rarely asked after hi
2 CONC s wife and children, and in later years Louis became vexed with Marguerite's ambition.
2 CONT
2 CONT She accompanied Louis on his first crusade and was responsible for negotiations and ransom when he was captured. She was thus for a brief time the only woman ever to lead a crusade. During this period, while in Damietta, she gave 
2 CONC birth to Jean Tristran.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Louis on his second crusade, during which she remained in France, she returned to Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was devoted to her sister Queen Eleanor of England, and they stayed in contact until Eleanor's death in 1291. Marguerite herself died four and a half years after her sister, on 21 December 1295. She was seventy-four years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Children
2 CONT
2 CONT With Louis IX of France:
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche (1240–29 April 1243) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (2 March 1241–28 January 1271), married Theobald II of Navarre 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis (25 February 1244–January 1260) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip III of France (1 May 1245–5 October 1285), married firstly Isabella of Aragon, by whom he had issue, including Philip IV of France and Charles, Count of Valois; he married secondly Marie of Brabant, by whom he had issue, in
2 CONC cluding Marguerite of France. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean (born and died in 1248) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Tristan (1250–3 August 1270) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre (1251–1284) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche (1253–1323), married Ferdinand de la Cerda, Infante of Castile 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite (1254–1271), married John I, Duke of Brabant 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, Count of Clermont (1256–7 February 1317), married Beatrice of Burgundy, by whom he had issue. He was the ancestor of King Henry IV of France. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes of France (c. 1260–19 December 1327), married Robert II, Duke of Burgundy 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Charles Cawley, Medieval Lands, Provence 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Thomas B. Costain, The Magnificent Century, pp.125-26 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Joinville and Villehardouin: Chronicles of the Crusades, translated by M.R.B. Shaw, pages 262-263; Penguin Classics: New York, 1963. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Murray, Jacqueline, Conflicted Identities and Multiple Masculinities, 1999 
2 CONT
2 CONT Costain, Thomas B., The Plantagenets, The Magnificent Century, 1951 
