1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ferdinand de la Cerda (1275–1322)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando was married to Juana Núñez de Lara, who is also known as Lady of Lara. The couple had four daughters,
2 CONT
2 CONT John Núñez de Lara (1313-1350), married Maria de Haro
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche Núñez de Lara (1311-1347), married Juan Manuel, Duke of Peñafiel and was mother of Juana Manuel of Castile, consort of Henry II of Castile and mother of John I of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Núñez de Lara, a nun
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Núñez de Lara, married Charles II of Alençon and was mother of Charles III of Alençon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles II of Alençon
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1336, he married Maria de la Cerda (1310 – November 19, 1379, Paris), the daughter of Fernando de la Cerda, Lord of Lara. They had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles III of Alençon (1337 – July 5, 1375, Lyon)
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip of Alençon (1338–1397, Rome), made Bishop of Beauvais in 1356, later Cardinal, Archbishop of Rouen, Latin Patriarch of Jerusalem, Patriarch of Aquileia, and Bishop of Ostia and Sabina
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II of Alençon (1340 – September 20, 1404)
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (1342 – September 3, 1379, Poissy), became a nun
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert of Alençon (1344–1377), Count of Perche, married April 5, 1374 Jeanne, daughter of Viscount John I of Rohan
