2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was born in Burgos, the capital of Castile. Before becoming king, he was known by the title Prince of Asturias, designating him as the heir apparent. After succeeding to the throne at 11, Henry took power at 14. He was able 
2 CONC to pacify the nobility and restore royal power. During his reign, the Castilian fleet won several victories against the English; Henry sent a naval fleet in 1400 that destroyed Tétouan in North Africa, a pirate base. In 1402, Henr
2 CONC y began the colonization of the Canary Islands, sending French explorer Jean de Béthencourt. He also sent envoys to Timur.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1388, Henry married Katherine of Lancaster (Catalina de Lancaster; 1372–1418), the daughter of John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster and Gaunt's second wife, Constance of Castile, who was the elder daughter of Pedro of Castile. 
2 CONC This ended a dynastic conflict and solidified the House of Trastamara. Henry and Catherine's son became John II of Castile, who succeeded Henry when he died in Toledo, Catherine acting as Regent of Castile because John II was then
2 CONC  underage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT All of them by his wife Catherine of Lancaster:
2 CONT
2 CONT Catalina (1402-?)
2 CONT
2 CONT John II of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Castile. Married Alfonso V of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry III (October 4, 1379 – 1406), sometimes known as Henry the Sufferer or Henry the Infirm (Spanish: Enrique el Doliente) was the son of John I and Eleanor of Aragon, and succeeded him as King of Castile and León in 1390.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was born in Burgos, the capital of Castile. Before becoming king, he was known by the title Prince of Asturias, designating him as the heir apparent. After succeeding to the throne at 11, Henry took power at 14. He was able 
2 CONC to pacify the nobility and restore royal power. During his reign, the Castilian fleet won several victories against the English; Henry sent a naval fleet in 1400 that destroyed Tétouan in North Africa, a pirate base. In 1402, Henr
2 CONC y began the colonization of the Canary Islands, sending French explorer Jean de Béthencourt. He also sent envoys to Timur.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1388, Henry married Katherine of Lancaster (Catalina de Lancaster; 1372–1418), the daughter of John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster and Gaunt's second wife, Constance of Castile, who was the elder daughter of Pedro of Castile. 
2 CONC This ended a dynastic conflict and solidified the House of Trastamara. Henry and Catherine's son became John II of Castile, who succeeded Henry when he died in Toledo, Catherine acting as Regent of Castile because John II was then
2 CONC  underage.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT History: Henry III (of Castile and León) (1379-1406), king of Castile and León (1390-1406), the son and successor of King John I. Although at the beginning of his reign the country was plagued with continual disorder and violent a
2 CONC nti-Semitism, Henry was soon able to placate the nobility and restore royal authority. He won victories over the English at sea, and the conquest of the Canary Islands began under his sponsorship in 1402.
2 CONT
2 CONT History: Microsoft® Encarta® Encyclopedia 2002. © 1993-2001 Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.
2 CONT --------------------
