1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Charles II of Alençon
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_II,_Count_of_Alen%C3%A7on
2 CONT
2 CONT Tomb Effigy of Charles d'AlenconCharles II of Alençon, called the Magnanimous (1297 – August 26, 1346) was the second son of Charles of Valois and his first wife Margaret. He was Count of Alençon, Perche (1325–1346), Count of Char
2 CONC tres and Count of Joigny (1335–1336).
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in April 1314 Jeanne of Joigny, who succeeded her father John II as Countess of Joigny in 1335 but died on September 2, 1336. They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT He showed a marked, if foolhardy courage from the time of his first taking up arms (1324, in Aquitaine), and took part in the Battle of Cassel (1328).
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1336, he married Maria de la Cerda (1310 – November 19, 1379, Paris), the daughter of Fernando de la Cerda, Lord of Lara. They had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles III of Alençon (1337 – July 5, 1375, Lyon) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip of Alençon (1338–1397, Rome), made Bishop of Beauvais in 1356, later Cardinal, Archbishop of Rouen, Latin Patriarch of Jerusalem, Patriarch of Aquileia, and Bishop of Ostia and Sabina 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II of Alençon (1340 – September 20, 1404) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (1342 – September 3, 1379, Poissy), became a nun 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert of Alençon (1344–1377), Count of Perche, married April 5, 1374 Jeanne, daughter of Viscount John I of Rohan 
2 CONT
2 CONT Coat of arms of the counts and dukes of Alençon of the House of Valois. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Coat of arms of the counts of Perche.He entered the War of the Breton Succession in 1340, and was subsequently killed at the Battle of Crécy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded in Alençon by his son Charles, and in Perche by his son Robert.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles II of Alençon
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles II of Alençon, called the Magnanimous (1297 – August 26, 1346) was the second son of Charles of Valois and his first wife Margaret. He was Count of Alençon, Perche (1325–1346), Count of Chartres and Count of Joigny (1335–1
2 CONC 336).
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in April 1314 Jeanne of Joigny, who succeeded her father John II as Countess of Joigny in 1335 but died on September 2, 1336. They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT He showed a marked, if foolhardy courage from the time of his first taking up arms (1324, in Aquitaine), and took part in the Battle of Cassel (1328).
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1336, he married Maria de la Cerda (1310 – November 19, 1379, Paris), the daughter of Fernando de la Cerda, Lord of Lara. They had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles III of Alençon (1337 – July 5, 1375, Lyon)
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip of Alençon (1338–1397, Rome), made Bishop of Beauvais in 1356, later Cardinal, Archbishop of Rouen, Latin Patriarch of Jerusalem, Patriarch of Aquileia, and Bishop of Ostia and Sabina
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II of Alençon (1340 – September 20, 1404)
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (1342 – September 3, 1379, Poissy), became a nun
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert of Alençon (1344–1377), Count of Perche, married April 5, 1374 Jeanne, daughter of Viscount John I of Rohan
2 CONT
2 CONT He entered the War of the Breton Succession in 1340, and was subsequently killed at the Battle of Crécy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded in Alençon by his son Charles, and in Perche by his son Robert.
