1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gertrude_of_Dagsburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude of Dagsburg
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude (1190 or May 1205 – 30 March 1225) was the daughter and heiress of Albert II, count of Metz and Dagsburg (Dabo), a trouvère, and three times a wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude was named after her mother, the daughter of Herman III, Margrave of Baden. Her birth date of May 1205 (or as late as mid-1206) is questionable, as her mother was then 52-years-old.[1] A more plausible date is c.1190.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude succeeded her father as countess on his death in 1212, at which time she had already married Theobald, soon to be Duke of Lorraine (1213), according to the Vitæ Odiliæ. Their betrothal occurred in September 1205, possibly
2 CONC  when she was an infant.[1] On her marriage her husband took over the administration of her inheritance, but he died in early 1220 without siring any children. In May 1220 she married Theobald IV of Champagne, who was only an adol
2 CONC escent, against the wishes of the Emperor Frederick II. In 1222 Theobald repudiated her for either consanguinity (following Alberic de Trois-Fontaines) or sterility (after Richer).[1] In 1224 she married a third time to Simon III,
2 CONC  count of Leiningen, but she died within a year. She was buried in the abbey of Sturzelbronn. Her husband inherited her county.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude is probably the Duchess of Lorraine who composed two lyric poems in Old French. One, Un petit devant le jour, is found in multiple sources, some with accompanying musical notation.[2] The other is found only in manuscript
2 CONC  CH-BEsu MS 389, alongside Un petit devant. They are numbered R1640 and R1995.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ a b c Gertrude von Dagsburg at the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. ^ Maria V. Coldwell, "Lorraine, Duchess of", Grove Music Online, Oxford Music Online (Accessed 16 August 2008).
2 CONT
2 CONT This page was last modified on 24 September 2009 at 08:06.
2 CONT --------------------
