2 CONT Blanche of Navarre (1226–1283) From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Navarre (1226 – 1283), also known as Blanche of Champagne, was the daughter of Theobald I of Navarre, King of Navarre and Count of Champagne, and second his wife Agnes of Beaujeu (daughter of Guichard IV, sire of Beauje
2 CONC u and Sybille of Flanders). Blanche was married in 1236 to John I, Duke of Brittany, they had eight children: John II, Duke of Brittany, married Beatrice of England and had issue Peter of Brittany, Lord of Hade Alix of Brittany, m
2 CONC arried John of Châtillon Theobald (1245-1256) Theobald (died soon after birth) Eleanor (1248) Nicolas of Brittany (1249-1261) Robert (1251-1259) [edit]Source
2 CONT
2 CONT Known as Infanta doña BLANCA de Navarra or Blanche of Champagne
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanche_of_Navarre_%281226%E2%80%931283%29 Blanche of Navarre, Duchess of Brittany From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT   (Redirected from Blanche of Navarre (1226–1283)) Jump to: navigation, search Blanche of Navarre Duchess of Brittany Tenure 	1236-1283 Spouse 	John I, Duke of Brittany Among Others Issue John II, Duke of Brittany Peter, Lord of H
2 CONC ade Alix, Countess of Châtillon House 	House of Champagne Father 	Theobald I of Navarre Mother 	Agnes of Beaujeu Born 	1226 Died 	12 August 1283 Naples Burial 	Abbey de la Joie, Hennebont
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Navarre (1226 – 12 August 1283[1]), also known as Blanche of Champagne, was the daughter of Theobald I of Navarre, King of Navarre and Count of Champagne, and second his wife Agnes of Beaujeu. She was a member of the Ho
2 CONC use of Champagne. By her marriage she became Duchess of Brittany. Contents [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT    Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Her maternal grandparents were Guichard IV, Sire of Beaujeu and his wife Sybille of Flanders, who was daughter of Margaret I, Countess of Flanders and Baldwin V, Count of Hainaut. Blanche's paternal grandparents were Theobald III,
2 CONC  Count of Champagne and his wife Blanca of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that Blanche was the only child from this union but some[2] believe that Blanche had a sister Agnes. After her mother's death, Theobald married Margaret of Bourbon. From this marriage Blanche gained some half-siblin
2 CONC gs including: Margaret, Duchess of Lorraine, Theobald II of Navarre, Beatrice, Duchess of Burgundy and Henry I of Navarre. [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche's father became King of Navarre in 1234, this made her Infanta doña Blanche de Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche was firstly betrothed to Otto III, Count Palatine of Burgundy[3], the marriage contract was signed on 16 January 1236. However the engagment was broken.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche was instead married in 1236 to John I, Duke of Brittany, the main reason he married Blanche was so he could get Navarre and Theobald did make John heir to the throne. However John renounced the claim after Margaret of Bour
2 CONC bon gave Theobald two sons. John and Blanche had eight children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * John II, Duke of Brittany (3 or 4 January 1239-Lyon 16 November 1305), married Beatrice of England and had issue
2 CONT     * Peter of Brittany (2 April 1241-Paris 19 October 1268), Lord of Hade
2 CONT     * Alix of Brittany (6 June 1243-2 August 1288), married John of Châtillon and had issue
2 CONT     * Theobald (1245-1256), died young
2 CONT     * Theobald (died soon after birth)
2 CONT     * Eleanor (1248), died young
2 CONT     * Nicolas of Brittany (1249-1261), died young
2 CONT     * Robert (1251-1259), died young
2 CONT
2 CONT Of their eight children, only their eldest three lived to adulthood.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1270 Blanche founded the Abbey de la Joie near Hennebont, she was later buried there. She died in 1283, her husband outlived her by three years. Blanche outlived six of her eight children. Blanche de Navarre (1226-1283).jpg [ed
2 CONC it] Ancestry References
2 CONT
