1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Joan I de Navarre, also known as Joanna or Joan of Navarre (c. 14 January 1271 – April 4, 1305), Queen regnant of Navarre and Queen consort of France, was the daughter of king Henry I of Navarre and Blanche of Arto
2 CONC is.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1274, upon the death of her father, she became Countess of Champagne and Queen regnant of Navarre. Her mother Queen Blanche was her guardian and Regent in Navarre. Various powers, both foreign and Navarrese, sought to take adva
2 CONC ntage of the minority of the heiress and the "weakness" of the female regent, which caused Jeanne and her mother to seek protection at the court of Philip III of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of 13, Jeanne married the future Philip IV of France on August 16, 1284, becoming Queen of France a year later. Queen Jeanne founded the famous College of Navarre in Paris. She died in childbirth in 1305. The crowns of 
2 CONC Navarre and France were henceforth united for almost half a century. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite (1288–c.1294) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis X of France, King Louis I of Navarre from 1305, France from 1314 (October, 1289 – June 5, 1316) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche (1290–c.1294) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip V of France and Navarre (as Philip II) (1292/93 – January 3, 1322) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles IV of France and Navarre (as Charles I) (c.1294 – February 1, 1328) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (c.1295 – August 23, 1358), married Edward II of England 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert (1297 – July 1308) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their three surviving sons would all become Kings of France, in turn, and their only surviving daughter Queen consort of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne led an army against the Count de Bar when he rebelled against her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne died under mysterious circumstances; one chronicler even accused her husband of having killed her. Her personal physician was the inventor Guido da Vigevano.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan I (also known as Joanna I; 14 January 1271 – 4 April 1305), the daughter of king Henry I of Navarre and Blanche of Artois, reigned as queen regnant of Navarre and also served as queen consort of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan led an army against the Count of Bar when he rebelled against her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan died in 1305 in childbirth, though one chronicler even accused her husband of having killed her. Her personal physician was the inventor Guido da Vigevano.
2 CONT
