1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_III,_Duke_of_Lorraine Frederick III, Duke of Lorraine From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to: navigation, search 	This article does not cite any references or sources. 
2 CONC Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (March 2010)
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick III (French: Ferry) (1238 – December 31, 1302) was the Duke of Lorraine from 1251 to his death. He was the only son and successor of Matthias II and Catherine of Limburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was not yet thirteen years of age when his father died, so his mother assumed the regency for a few years. In 1255, he married Margaret, the daughter of King Theobald I of Navarre and Margaret of Bourbon. Frederick's father-in-
2 CONC law was the Count of Champagne as well, and marriage of Margaret with Frederick signified the Gallicization of Lorraine and the beginnings of tension between French and German influences which characterises its later history. When
2 CONC  Joan I of Navarre, Margaret's niece, (the daughter of her brother, Henry I of Navarre), married, Philip the Fair, the future king of France, in 1284, the ties to France grew. The long-held loyalty of the dukes of Lorraine to the 
2 CONC Holy Roman Emperor had waned in the first half of the thirteenth century and French influence was pervasive leading to its permanent attachment to France in 1766.
2 CONT
2 CONT During Frederick's reign, he fought the bishops of Metz until Pope Clement IV excommunicated him and put his duchy under an interdict.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1257, after the elections following the death of King William of Holland resulted in the contested election of both Richard, Earl of Cornwall and Alfonso X of Castile, Frederick of Lorraine sided with Alfonso who through his mo
2 CONC ther Beatrix was the grandson of the Hohenstaufen Philip of Swabia. The rivalry between the two kings led to little actual combat and after Richard's death the 1273 election of Rudolf of Habsburg and the subsequent withdrawal of A
2 CONC lfonso reestablished unity. [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage to Margaret, he had the following issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Theobald (1263–1312), his successor in Lorraine
2 CONT     * Matthias (died 1282), lord of Beauregard
2 CONT     * Frederick (died 1299), bishop of Orléans (1297–1299)
2 CONT     * Frederick (died c. 1320), lord of Plombiéres, Romont, and Brémoncourt
2 CONT     * Gerard (known 1317)
2 CONT     * Isabelle (died 1335), married (1287) Louis III, Duke of Lower Bavaria; then Lord Henry of Sully; and then (1306) Count Henry III of Vaudémont (died 1348)
2 CONT     * Catherine, lady of Romont, married (1290) Conrad III (died 1350), count of Fribourg
2 CONT     * Agnes, married John II (died 1302), sire of Harcourt
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Dukes of Lorraine family tree
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by Matthias II 	Duke of Lorraine 1251–1302 	Succeeded by Theobald II
2 CONT
2 CONT This page was last modified on 15 July 2010 at 09:23.
2 CONT --------------------
