2 CONT
2 CONT 5) Fernando of Aragón, abade
2 CONT
2 CONT ## Daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Constanza of Aragón and Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Leonor of Aragón and Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Sancha of Aragón and Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) Dulce de Aragón, nun
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sancha_of_Castile,_Queen_of_Aragon
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Sancha of Castile (21 September 1154/5 – 9 November 1208, Sijena) was the only surviving child of King Alfonso VII of Castile by his second queen, Richeza of Poland, who was the daughter of Vladislav II, Duke of Silesia.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 18, 1174 in Saragossa she married King Alfonso II of Aragon. They had 9 children, but only seven would survive into adulthood:
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Aragon, married King Imre of Hungary and later, Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonor, married Count Raymond VI of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II of Aragon (I of Barcelona), b. 1174, killed at the Battle of Muret, September 12, 1213
2 CONT
2 CONT Dolça (nun)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso II, Count of Provence, b. 1180, d. 1209
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando, Abbot of Montearagon, d. after 1227
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon Berenguer, d. in the 1190s
2 CONT
2 CONT A patroness of troubadours such as Giraud de Calanson and Peire Raymond, the queen became involved in a legal dispute with her husband concerning properties which formed part of her dower estates. In 1177 she entered the county of
2 CONC  Ribagorza and took forcible possession of various castles and fortresses which had belonged to the crown there.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband died at Perpignan in 1196, Sancha was relegated to the background of political affairs by her son Pedro II, and she retired from court, withdrawing to the abbey of Nuestra Senora, at Sijena, which she had founded
2 CONC . There she assumed the cross of the Order of St John of Jerusalem which she wore till the end of her life. The queen mother entertained her widowed daughter Queen Constanza of Hungary (1179-1222) at Sijena prior to her leaving Ar
2 CONC agon for her marriage with the emperor Frederick II in 1208. She died soon afterwards, aged fifty-four, and was interred before the high altar of the church at Sijena.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha is the main character of a novel, The Borgia Bride, by American writer Jeanne Kalogridis, portraying life in the Borgia dynasty through the eyes of Princess Sancha of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Plot introduction
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha de Aragon, princess of Naples and illegitimate daughter to the coldhearted duke of Calabria (briefly king of Naples), is used to establish ties to the feared and influential House of Borgia when her father betroths her to t
2 CONC he younger scion, Jofre. War with the French briefly returned her to Naples, but rumors of her beauty reach her lecherous father-in-law, Pope Alexander VI, who recalls her and Jofre to opulent Rome. There, she avoids the pope's ad
2 CONC vances—and her jealous sister-in-law Lucrezia's animosity—but falls into a steamy affair with her brother-in-law, Cesare Borgia. Cesare becomes furious when she refuses to leave Jofre, and he sets out on a warpath that includes he
2 CONC r brother Alfonso, who has also married into the Borgia clan—to Lucrezia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Plot summary
2 CONT
2 CONT Autumn 1488
2 CONT
2 CONT The book starts off with Princess Sancha remembering the thirtieth anniversary of her grandfather's ascension to the Neapolitan throne. Because Naples needed a blessing after many wars and natural disasters, the royal family was t
2 CONC o beseech San Gennaro to witness a miracle. Inside a reliquary was believed to be ancient blood of the royals and if the blood became liquid once again, it is a good omen for the king. After the "miracle" is performed, the royal p
2 CONC rocession makes its way back to Castel Nuovo in Naples. A feast celebrating the anniversary of the king was held later that night, and out of boredom, seeks the chamber of the dead of her her grandfather, King Ferrante. It is said
2 CONC  here that the King had brought his enemies that he had killed, preserved and on occasion visited the dead. She quickly finds the legend of the chamber to be true, and meets her grandfather there. After discussing several matters 
2 CONC with her grandfather, Sancha is told by her grandfather to watch over her brother, for he is considered by Ferrante to be "weak". As the pair return to the party, the Duke of Calabria, Sancha's father, sees them and discovers that
2 CONC  she was in the chamber of the dead, and had not been invited. He tells her that he will speak to her later. Sancha then leaves to be comforted by her brother. Duke Alfonso returns later to tell Donna Trusia (Sancha's mother) that
2 CONC  she will not be allowed to go on a picnic with the other children. He speaks to her in the study and denies her contact with her brother (also named Alfonso) for two weeks for her incorrigible behaviour, since that is the one thi
2 CONC ng she loves above all else. After two weeks pass, Sancha and Alfonso are reunited and Sancha swears that she would never give her father cause to punish her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Late Spring 1492
2 CONT
2 CONT Although a little more less than three years had passed, little had changed in the royal household. Sancha and Alfonso are still close, although they do not share a nursery any longer. A new pope was elected that year, one by the 
2 CONC name of Rodrigo Borgia, Pope Alexander VI. In the beginning of this chapter, Sancha is summoned to the King's chambers. There, she finds that she is betrothed to the Count Onorato Caetani. His manner towards the royal family is de
2 CONC scribed as jovial. The courtship between the count and the princess preceded rapidly. As a whim, Sancha went to see a strega (witch). When she reached the strega's house, she was surprised to find herself required to enter alone. 
2 CONC Immediately, Sancha realizes that the news has a hint of foreboding. After spreading the tarot cards, Sancha chooses the card of a "heart, impaled by two blades, which together made a great silver X".
2 CONT
2 CONT The strega warns Sancha that if she does not resort to evil, she will "condemn to death those whom you most love". She also says that the princess will not marry the Count, but the son of the most powerful man in Italy, and that s
2 CONC he will not love him, nor have many children by him. She ends by saying "Take great care, Sancha, or your heart will destroy all that you love.
2 CONT
2 CONT (from wikipedia)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha of Castile, Queen of Aragon
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Not to be confused with Sancha of Castile, Queen of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha and Alfonso, centre, surrounded by the ladies of their court
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Sancha of Castile (21 September 1154/5 – 9 November 1208, Sijena) was the only surviving child of King Alfonso VII of Castile by his second queen, Richeza of Poland, who was the daughter of Vladislav II, Duke of Silesia.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 18, 1174 in Saragossa she married King Alfonso II of Aragon. They had 9 children, but only seven would survive into adulthood:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Constance of Aragon, married King Imre of Hungary and later, Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Leonor, married Count Raymond VI of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Peter II of Aragon (I of Barcelona), b. 1174, killed at the Battle of Muret, September 12, 1213
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Dolça (nun)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Alfonso II, Count of Provence, b. 1180, d. 1209
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fernando, Abbot of Montearagon, d. after 1227
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ramon Berenguer, d. in the 1190s
2 CONT
2 CONT A patroness of troubadours such as Giraud de Calanson and Peire Raymond, the queen became involved in a legal dispute with her husband concerning properties which formed part of her dower estates. In 1177 she entered the county of
2 CONC  Ribagorza and took forcible possession of various castles and fortresses which had belonged to the crown there.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband died at Perpignan in 1196, Sancha was relegated to the background of political affairs by her son Pedro II, and she retired from court, withdrawing to the abbey of Nuestra Senora, at Sijena, which she had founded
2 CONC . There she assumed the cross of the Order of St John of Jerusalem which she wore till the end of her life. The queen mother entertained her widowed daughter Queen Constanza of Hungary (1179-1222) at Sijena prior to her leaving Ar
2 CONC agon for her marriage with the emperor Frederick II in 1208. She died soon afterwards, aged fifty-four, and was interred before the high altar of the church at Sijena.
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona 	Queen Consort of Aragon
2 CONT
2 CONT 1174–1196 	Succeeded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie of Montpellier
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Sancha of Castile (September 21, 1154 or 1155 – November 9, 1208, Sijena) was the only child of King Alfonso VII of Castile by his second queen, Richeza of Poland, who was the daughter of Vladislav II, Duke of Silesia.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 18, 1174 in Saragossa she married King Alfonso II of Aragon. They had 9 children, but only seven would survive into adulthood:
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Aragon-> married King Imre of Hungary and later, Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonor -> married Count Raymond VI of Toulouse 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II of Aragon (I of Barcelona), b. 1174, killed at the Battle of Muret, September 12, 1213 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dolça (nun) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso II, Count of Provence, b. 1180, d. 1209 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando, Abbot of Montearagon, d. after 1227 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon Berenguer, d. in the 1190s 
2 CONT
2 CONT A patroness of troubadours such as Giraud de Calanson and Peire Raymond, the queen became involved in a legal dispute with her husband concerning properties which formed part of her dower estates. In 1177 she entered the county of
2 CONC  Ribagorza and took forcible possession of various castles and fortresses which had belonged to the crown there.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband died at Perpignan in 1196, Sancha was relegated to the background of political affairs by her son Pedro II, and she retired from court, withdrawing to the abbey of Nuestra Senora, at Sijena, which she had founded
2 CONC . There she assumed the cross of the Order of St John of Jerusalem which she wore till the end of her life. The queen mother entertained her widowed daughter Queen Constanza of Hungary (1179-1222) at Sijena prior to her leaving Ar
2 CONC agon for her marriage with the emperor Frederick II in 1208. She died soon afterwards, aged fifty-four, and was interred before the high altar of the church at Sijena.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sancha_de_Castilla_y_de_Polonia
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha de Castilla y Polonia (¿?, 21 de septiembre de 1154/56 - Sigena, 1208), infanta de Castilla y reina consorte de Aragón (1174-1206).
2 CONT
2 CONT Hija del rey de Castilla Alfonso VII y de su segunda mujer, Riquilda de Polonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT El 18 de enero de 1174 se casó en la catedral de Zaragoza con el rey Alfonso II de Aragón. De este matrimonio nacieron:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Pedro el Católico (1174 - 1213), conde de Barcelona, con el nombre de Pedro I, y rey de Aragón, con el nombre de Pedro II;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Constanza (1179 - 1222), casada en 1198 con Emerico I de Hungría y en 1210 con Federico II Hohenstaufen, Sacro Emperador Romano Germánico, Rey de Sicilia y Rey de Jerusalen;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Alfonso (1180 - 1209), conde de Provenza, con el nombre de Alfonso II;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Leonor (1182 - 1226), casada en 1202 con Ramón VI de Tolosa:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sancha (1186 - 1241), casada en 1211 con Ramón VII de Tolosa;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sancho, muerto joven.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ramón Berenguer, muerto joven.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fernando (1190 - 1249), sacerdote y abad en Montearagón.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Dulce (1192 - ¿?), monja en Sijena.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fue enterrada en el Monasterio de Sigena, que ella había mandado construir.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
