2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Sancha of Castile (September 21, 1154 or 1155 – November 9, 1208, Sijena) was the only child of King Alfonso VII of Castile by his second queen, Richeza of Poland, who was the daughter of Vladislav II, Duke of Silesia.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 18, 1174 in Saragossa she married King Alfonso II of Aragon. They had 9 children, but only seven would survive into adulthood:
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Aragon-> married King Imre of Hungary and later, Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonor -> married Count Raymond VI of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II of Aragon (I of Barcelona), b. 1174, killed at the Battle of Muret, September 12, 1213
2 CONT
2 CONT Dolça (nun)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso II, Count of Provence, b. 1180, d. 1209
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando, Abbot of Montearagon, d. after 1227
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon Berenguer, d. in the 1190s
2 CONT
2 CONT A patroness of troubadours such as Giraud de Calanson and Peire Raymond, the queen became involved in a legal dispute with her husband concerning properties which formed part of her dower estates. In 1177 she entered the county of
2 CONC  Ribagorza and took forcible possession of various castles and fortresses which had belonged to the crown there.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband died at Perpignan in 1196, Sancha was relegated to the background of political affairs by her son Pedro II, and she retired from court, withdrawing to the abbey of Nuestra Senora, at Sijena, which she had founded
2 CONC . There she assumed the cross of the Order of St John of Jerusalem which she wore till the end of her life. The queen mother entertained her widowed daughter Queen Constanza of Hungary (1179-1222) at Sijena prior to her leaving Ar
2 CONC agon for her marriage with the emperor Frederick II in 1208. She died soon afterwards, aged fifty-four, and was interred before the high altar of the church at Sijena.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha of Castile (21 September 1154/5 – 9 November 1208, Sijena) was the only surviving child of King Alfonso VII of Castile by his second queen, Richeza of Poland, who was the daughter of Vladislav II, Duke of Silesia.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 18, 1174 in Saragossa she married King Alfonso II of Aragon. They had 9 children, but only seven would survive into adulthood:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Constance of Aragon, married King Imre of Hungary and later, Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Leonor, married Count Raymond VI of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Peter II of Aragon (I of Barcelona), b. 1174, killed at the Battle of Muret, September 12, 1213
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Dolça (nun)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Alfonso II, Count of Provence, b. 1180, d. 1209
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fernando, Abbot of Montearagon, d. after 1227
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ramon Berenguer, d. in the 1190s
2 CONT
2 CONT A patroness of troubadours such as Giraud de Calanson and Peire Raymond, the queen became involved in a legal dispute with her husband concerning properties which formed part of her dower estates. In 1177 she entered the county of
2 CONC  Ribagorza and took forcible possession of various castles and fortresses which had belonged to the crown there.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband died at Perpignan in 1196, Sancha was relegated to the background of political affairs by her son Pedro II, and she retired from court, withdrawing to the abbey of Nuestra Senora, at Sijena, which she had founded
2 CONC . There she assumed the cross of the Order of St John of Jerusalem which she wore till the end of her life. The queen mother entertained her widowed daughter Queen Constanza of Hungary (1179-1222) at Sijena prior to her leaving Ar
2 CONC agon for her marriage with the emperor Frederick II in 1208. She died soon afterwards, aged fifty-four, and was interred before the high altar of the church at Sijena.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sancha of Castile, b. 21 September 1154 in Toledo, Castile, Spain, d. 9 November 1208 in Jaen, Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT     Father: Alfonso VII, King of Castile, b. 1 March 1105 in Toledo, Castile, Spain, d. 21 August 1157 in La Fresneda, Teruel, Aragon, Spain, He became King of Castile, 1126 in Castile, Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT     Mother: Richilde of Poland, b. ca. 1131 in Wroclaw, Poland, d. 16 June 1185 in Castile, Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT     Married Alfonso II "the Chaste", King of Aragon, b. 1152 on 18 January 1174 in Zaragoza, Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Pedro II, King of Aragon, b. ca. November 1174 in Aragon, Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Alfonso II, Prince of Aragon, b. ca. 1176 in Zaragoza, Spain, m. Gersinde de Sabran, July 1193, d. February 1209 in Palermo, Sicily, Italy
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Constance of Aragon, b. ca. 1190
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Sancha of Castile (September 21, 1154 or 1155 – November 9, 1208, Sijena) was the only child of King Alfonso VII of Castile by his second queen, Richeza of Poland, who was the daughter of Vladislav II, Duke of Silesia.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 18, 1174 in Saragossa she married King Alfonso II of Aragon. They had 9 children, but only seven would survive into adulthood:
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Aragon-> married King Imre of Hungary and later, Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonor -> married Count Raymond VI of Toulouse 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II of Aragon (I of Barcelona), b. 1174, killed at the Battle of Muret, September 12, 1213 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dolça (nun) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso II, Count of Provence, b. 1180, d. 1209 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando, Abbot of Montearagon, d. after 1227 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon Berenguer, d. in the 1190s
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Sancha of Castile married King Alfonso II of Aragon; they had 9 children, but only 7 would survive into adulthood--including our ancestor Alfonso of Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha was a patroness of troubadours, such as Giraud de Calanson and Peire Raymond. She became involved in a legal dispute with her husband concerning properties that formed part of her dower estates. In 1177 she entered the coun
2 CONC ty of Ribagorza and took forcible possession of various castles and fortresses that had belonged to the crown there.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband died at Perpignan in 1196, Sancha was relegated to the background of political affairs by her son Pedro II, and she retired from court, withdrawing to the abbey of Nuestra Señora, at Sijena, which she had founded
2 CONC . There she assumed the cross of the Order of St John of Jerusalem, which she wore till the end of her life. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha entertained her widowed daughter Queen Constanza of Hungary (1179-1222) at Sijena prior to her leaving Aragon for her marriage with our ancestor, the Emperor Frederick II, in 1208. She died soon afterward, aged 54, and was 
2 CONC interred before the high altar of the church at Sijena.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sancha_of_Castile for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
