1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT === Abu Zayd Abd ar-Rahman (c. 1195–1268), last Almohad ruler of Valencia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Zayd Abu Zayd''' (Arabic: '''أبو زيد عبد الرحمن'''; Spanish: '''Zayd Abu Zayd''', also known by his baptismal name '''Vicente Bellvis''') was the final Almohad governor of Valencia and a member of the '''Mu'minid dynasty'''. Hi
2 CONC s full name was '''Abū Zayd ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad Ibn Abī Ḥafṣ ʿUmar Ibn ʿAbd al-Mu'min'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Traditional ancestry ===
2 CONT (See [https://www.geni.com/discussions/310490?msg=1830015 discussion]).
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Father:''' [https://www.geni.com/people/Abu-Abd-Allah-Muhammad/6000000226051022833?through=6000000010671354763 Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad]
2 CONT * '''Grandfather:''' [https://www.geni.com/people/Abu-Hafs-Umar/6000000226050772960?through=6000000226051022833 Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmar]
2 CONT * '''Great-Grandfather:''' [https://www.geni.com/people/ʿAbd-al-Muʾmin-ibn-ʿAlī-ibn-ʿAlwī-ibn-Yaʿlā-al-Kūmī-Abū-Muḥammad/6000000226051093843?through=6000000226050797932 '''Caliph ʿAbd al-Muʾmin'''], the founder of the Almohad Cali
2 CONC phate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zayd succeeded his uncle as governor of Valencia. Following the death of Caliph Yaqub al-Mansur, he acted with autonomy during the resulting dynastic instability. Threatened by rivals, he became a vassal of King James I of Aragon 
2 CONC in 1225. In 1227, he recognized Idris al-Ma'mun as the legitimate Almohad caliph. After being ousted from Valencia by Zayyan ibn Mardanish in 1229, Zayd fled to Aragon, where he secured military support from James I to attempt to 
2 CONC reclaim his territory.  Under the protection of the Crown of Aragon, he held the seigniory over several localities in the Sierra de Espadán, which were inherited by his son, '''Fernando'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1236, Zayd converted to Christianity.  He was baptized as a Catholic, adopted the name '''Vicente Bellvis''', and became the progenitor of the Bellvis family. Some traditions suggest he kept this conversion secret until the [ht
2 CONC tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conquest_of_Valencia_(1238) conquest of Valencia] by the Catalan and Aragonese troops of [https://www.geni.com/people/James-I-the-Conqueror-King-of-Aragon/4924448917970137597 '''King James I of Aragon''
2 CONC '] on 9 October 1238.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Marriage and Offspring ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Spouse:''' Married Isabella Roldán (daughter of Martin Roldán and María López de Luna). Note: Some traditions also associate him with María Ferrandis (or Dominga López).
2 CONT * '''Children:'''
2 CONT ** '''Fernando:''' Documented heir to his father’s lordships.
2 CONT **'''Traditional/Extended List:''' Alda Ferrandis (linked to the Arenós lordship), Sancho Ferrandis, Elisenda, Mahomat Abiceit, Ceyt Abohiara, Zeyt Edris, and Aazón.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogy 
2 CONT
2 CONT *[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abd_al-Mu%27min Wikipedia shows] an Abu Zayd on the Almohad family tree, beginning with his 2nd great grandfather, Ali Al-Kumi.  He was a son of Abu Abd Allah Muhammad.  No further information. Was 
2 CONC he this man?. 
2 CONT * MOORISH SPAIN v5.0 Updated 02 February 2025 Chapter 8.                ALMOHADS https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MOORISH%20SPAIN.htm#_Toc371931154 does not show this man as a son of ABU MOHAMMAD ABD ALLAH (-Tunis 25 Feb 1221, bur
2 CONC  Tunis). 
2 CONT
2 CONT === Genealogical Note ===
2 CONT The descent of Zayd is debated. Some records, such as the 1324 testimony from the Bishop of Segorbe, contested the legitimacy of certain descendants. Furthermore, the connections between Isabella Roldán, Maria Ferrandis, and the w
2 CONC ider López family remain unclear and require verification against primary sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Curator Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Identity Management:''' Use one record for both the Almohad prince and the Christian lord '''Vicente Bellvis''' to prevent duplicate profiles.
2 CONT * '''Documentation:''' Prioritize academic studies by historians like Robert I. Burns and María del Carmen Barceló Torres.
2 CONT * '''Conflicting Data:''' Be aware that English-language academic sources often cite '''Isabella Roldán''' and their son '''Fernando''' as the primary family unit, while Spanish-language historiography often expands the list of ch
2 CONC ildren. Treat the broader list as traditional accounts rather than proven history.
2 CONT * '''Context:''' Frame his conversion as a practical political survival strategy. Avoid unverified, hagiographic tales surrounding the secrecy of his conversion.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Compiled with the assistance of Gemini AI ♊️, 24 June 2026''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Almohad_Caliphate The Almohad Caliphate[a] or Almohad Empire was a North African empire ruled by a Berber Muslim dynasty in the 12th and 13th centuries that lasted from 1121-1269. At its height, it 
2 CONC controlled much of the Maghreb and the Iberian Peninsula (Al-Ándalus). The Almohad movement was founded by Ibn Tumart among the Berber Masmuda tribes, but the Almohad caliphate and its ruling dynasty, known as the Mu'minid dynasty
2 CONC ,[16][17][18] were founded after his death by [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abd_al-Mu%27min Abd al-Mu'min] (c. 1094-1163).
2 CONT *https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3102581
2 CONT * https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zayd_Abu_Zayd
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zayd_Abu_Zayd (retrieved 24 June 2026) cites 
2 CONT **  1. Kennedy, Hugh (2014). Muslim Spain and Portugal: A Political History of al-Andalus. Routledge. p. 269. ISBN 9781317870418.
2 CONT **2. Fancy, Hussein (2016). The Mercenary Mediterranean: Sovereignty, Religion, and Violence in the Medieval Crown of Aragon. Chicago: University of Chicago Press. p. 80. ISBN 9780226329642. Abū Zayd, who had converted to Christia
2 CONC nity after the kingdom's conquest
2 CONT **3. T. Abate, Mark (2018). Convivencia and Medieval Spain: Essays in Honor of Thomas F. Glick. Springer. p. 215. ISBN 9783319964812. Abu Zayd, who married María Ferrández, although this was only after converting to Christianity i
2 CONC n secret
2 CONT **4. A. Catlos, Brian (2014). Muslims of Medieval Latin Christendom, c.1050–1614. Cambridge University Press. p. 63. ISBN 9780521889391. Overthrown in 1229, Abu ̄ Zayd went over to Jaume, eventually converting to Christianity in 1
2 CONC 236 under the name of Vincent
2 CONT **5."Historia de Gotor (History of Gotor)" (in Spanish). Comisión de Fiestas y el Ayuntamiento de Gotor. Archived from the original on 23 January 2010. Retrieved 17 January 2013.
2 CONT **6.  "Gotor, baronía de (Barony of Gotor)" (in Spanish). Archived from the original on 21 August 2014. Retrieved 17 January 2013.
2 CONT **7. "Historia (History)" (in Spanish). Ayuntamiento de Gotor. Archived from the original on 21 August 2014. Retrieved 17 January 2013.
2 CONT * https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MOORISH%20SPAIN.htm#_Toc371931154 Moorish Spain > Chapter 8.    ALMOHADS.  “… After the death of Caliph Yusuf al-Mustansir in 1224, the caliphate was disputed by different members of the family, 
2 CONC eight of whom succeeded until the death in 1269 of Abu Dabbis Idris al-Wathiq.  Ibn Khaldun records the succession of caliphs during this period[681].  Almohad rule in al-Andalus ended when Caliph al-Mamun moved his headquarters t
2 CONC o Marrakesh in 1228.”
2 CONT * Lantschner, Patrick. “City States in the Later Medieval Mediterranean World.” ''Past & Present'', Volume 254, Issue 1, February 2022, Pages 3–49, https://doi.org/10.1093/pastj/gtab004. < [https://academic.oup.com/past/article/25
2 CONC 4/1/3/6395072 link] >.  In al-Andalus, the Almohad governors of Baeza and Valencia, members of the Marrakech-based caliphal dynasty, tried to establish themselves as autonomous rulers in the 1220s. However, their efforts were unsu
2 CONC ccessful, even by the standards of city-based rulers. ʿAbd Allāh al-Bayyāsī of Baeza was assassinated in an urban uprising after only three years, while Abū Zayd of Valencia fled another uprising and ended up in Aragon where he to
2 CONC ok the name of Vicente Belvis.40
2 CONT ** 40 See the early fourteenth-century North African historian Ibn ʿIdhārī al-Marrākushī: Ibn ʿIdhārī al-Marrākushī, al-Bayān al-mughrib fī akhbār al-Andalus wa’l-Maghrib, in III parte de al-Bayān al-mughrib por Ibn ʿIdhārī, ed. A
2 CONC mbrosio Huici Miranda (Tetuan, 1963), 248–9, 250–2, 255, 270, partially trans. in Colección de crónicas árabes de la Reconquista, trans. Ambrosio Huici Miranda, 4 vols. (Tetuan, 1953–4), ii–iii; Ibn Khaldūn, Kitāb al-ʿIbar, i, 340
2 CONC –2. See also Huici Miranda, Historia política del imperio almohade, ii, 453–4, 457–62; and Abigail Krasner Balbale, ‘Affiliation and Ideology at the End of the Almohad Caliphate’, Al-Masāq, xxx (2018), 271–8. For members of the ca
2 CONC liphal dynasty (known as sayyids) as provincial governors, see Pascal Buresi and Hicham El Aallaoui, Governing the Empire: Provincial Administration in the Almohad Caliphate, 1224–1269 (Leiden, 2013), 133–7. 
