2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Hungary (c. 1180 - 6 December 1240) was the second Queen consort of Ottokar I of Bohemia. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance was a daughter of Béla III of Hungary and his first wife Agnes of Antioch. Her older siblings included Emeric of Hungary, Margaret of Hungary and Andrew II of Hungary. Two other siblings, Solomon and Stephen, are mention
2 CONC ed in the "Europäische Stammtafeln: Stammtafeln zur Geschichte der Europäischen Staaten" (1878) by Detlev Schwennicke. They reportedly died young. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Géza II of Hungary and Euphrosyne of Kiev. [3] Her maternal grandparents were Raynald of Chatillon and Constance of Antioch (joint princes of Antioch). [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1199, Ottokar I divorced his first wife, Adelheid of Meissen, on grounds of consanguinity. He married Constance later in the same year. They had nine children.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance is regularly noted as a co-donator with her husband in various documents of his reign. Her petitions to her husband for various donations are also recorded. She is considered to have sold the city Boleráz to her nephew B
2 CONC éla IV of Hungary. In 1247, Béla conferred said city to the nuns of Trnava. An epistle by which Constance supposedly grants freedom to the cities of Břeclav and Olomouc is considered a false document. The same epistle grants lands
2 CONC  in Ostrovany to the monastery of St. Stephen of Hradište. Another epistle has Constance settling "honorable Teutonic men" (viros honestos Theutunicos) in the city of Hodonín and is also considered a forgery. [6] On 15 January 123
2 CONC 0, Ottokar I died and their son Wenceslaus succeeded him. Constance survived her husband by a decade. [7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1231, Pope Gregory IX set Constance and her dowry possessions under the protection of the Holy See. His letter to Constance clarifies said possessions to include the provinces of Břeclav (Brecyzlaviensem), Pribyslavice (Pribizl
2 CONC avensem), Dolni Kunice (Conowizensem), Godens (Godeninensem), Bzenec (Bisenzensem) and Budějovice (Budegewizensem). [8] In 1232, Constance founded Cloister Tichnowitz and retired to it as a nun. She died within the Cloister. [9]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Vratislav of Bohemia (c. 1200 - before 1209).
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith of Bohemia (c. 1202 - 2 June 1230). Married Bernhard von Spanheim, Duke of Carinthia
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna of Bohemia (c. 1204 - 23 June 1265). Married Henry II the Pious, Duke of Wrocław
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes of Bohemia. Considered to have died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenceslaus I of Bohemia (c. 1205 - 23 September 1253).
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladislaus, Margrave of Moravia (1207 - 10 February 1228).
2 CONT
2 CONT Přemysl, Margrave of Moravia (1209 - 16 October 1239). Married Margaret of Andechs-Merano. His wife was a daughter of Otto I, Duke of Merania and Beatrice II, Countess of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelmina of Bohemia? (1210 - 24 October 1281).
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes of Bohemia (20 January 1211 – 6 March 1282). Mother Superior of the Franciscan Poor Clares nuns of Prague. In 1989, Agnes was canonized as a saint by Pope John Paul II. [10]
2 CONT
