2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''María Díaz I de Haro''' "the Good" (1270–1342) was a Spanish noblewoman of the House of Haro. She was the daughter of Lope Díaz III de Haro who was assassinated by order of the king at Alfaro, La Rioja. She is best known for be
2 CONC ing the Lady of Biscay and for her lifelong battle against her uncle, Diego López V de Haro, for the title of the lordship of Biscay.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mar%C3%ADa_D%C3%ADaz_I_de_Haro 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria was the daughter of Lope Díaz III de Haro and, Juana Alfonso de Molina. She inherited the title of Lord of Biscay from her brother Diego López de Haro IV upon his death in 1288, which passed briefly to her husband, John of C
2 CONC astile and which was later taken from her by her uncle, Diego López V de Haro during the unrest following the death of Sancho IV of Castile and the rise to power of his young son, Ferdinand IV of Castile. Her paternal grandparents
2 CONC  were Diego López III de Haro and Constanza de Bearne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria's brother was Diego López IV de Haro. She was the great granddaughter of the king, Alfonso IX of León.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria obtained the title of Lord of Biscay as she was next in line after her father, Lope Díaz III de Haro, died in 1288 and her elder brother, Diego López IV de Haro, died after just one year of holding the title in 1289.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT María I Díaz de Haro died on 3 October 1342 of old age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria continuously fought for the interests of Biscay including entering into several disputes with the crown of Castile. She is remembered in history as having contributed greatly to the development of Biscay, gaining the nicknam
2 CONC e the good. Together with Maria de Molina, she was known as one of the most notable and accomplished political women of her time.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT From her marriage with John of Castile, the following children were born:
2 CONT # Juan de Haro (b. ? - d. 1326) - Inherited all the possessions of his mother and father, married Isabel de Portugal y Manuel, daughter of the infante Alfonso of Portugal and granddaughter of King Alfonso III of Portugal. Assassin
2 CONC ated in Toro in 1326 by order of King Alfonso XI of Castile.
2 CONT # Lope Díaz de Haro (b. - d. 1295) - Died during his childhood.
2 CONT # María Díaz de Haro (b. - d. 1299) - Married Juan Núñez II de Lara el Menor, head of the House of Lara who would go on to marry the daughter of Diego Lopez V de Haro, María. Died without leaving descendants
2 CONT .
