2 CONT
2 CONT In 1272 Henry died, and her son Edward, who was 33 years old, became Edward I, King of England. She remained in England as queen dowager, and raised several of her grandchildren—Edward's son Henry and daughter Eleanor, and Beatric
2 CONC e's son John. When her grandson Henry died in her care in 1274, Eleanor went into mourning and gave orders for his heart to be buried at the priory at Guildford which she founded in his memory. In 1275 Eleanor's two remaining daug
2 CONC hters died Margaret 26th February and Beatrice 24th March.
2 CONT
2 CONT She retired to a convent; however, she remained in contact with her son, King Edward, and her sister, Queen Margaret of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor died on 24/25 June 1291 in Amesbury, eight miles north of Salisbury, England. She was buried on 11 September 1291 in the Abbey of St Mary and St Melor, Amesbury on 9 December. The exact site of her grave at the abbey is un
2 CONC known making her the only English queen without a marked grave. Her heart was taken to London where it was buried at the Franciscan priory.[13]
