1 NOTE Life of Edward I of England
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=9f81d210-09e4-4141-ab7c-4a933fdd26e1&tid=6959821&pid=-1169288156
1 NOTE King Edward I
2 CONT (Longshanks)
2 CONT
2 CONT King Edward I (1239-1307) of England was king during a time in history when a king was both monarch and warrior. Edward was a strong ruler with a strong sense of duty and believed that the king should promote the general welfare a
2 CONC nd place himself above class faction.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Edward was foremost a military man and one of the great generals of medieval Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1264, during the reign of Edward’s father, King Henry III, Baron Simon de Montfort raised an opposition to the monarchy. Edward, believing that Montfort had insulted his mother, engaged Montfort’s army and began a single-minded
2 CONC  pursuit of his foes. When he returned to his father’s main army, Edward found the army defeated and he himself was captured. Eventually he escaped; and in 1265, he finally defeated Montfort.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward declared himself a crusader, and in 1271 reached the Holy Land with an army of 1,000 men and amazed everyone at the time with a series of victories over the Muslim forces. He eventually captured Nazareth, although his force
2 CONC s were too small to consolidate his victories. As testimony to Edward’s skill as a warrior, he survived an assassination attempt. A Muslim mercenary broke into his tent and attacked him with a poisoned knife. Edward awoke from his
2 CONC  rest in time to kill his attacker.
2 CONT
2 CONT En route back to England in 1272, his father died and Edward became king. As king, Edward was reputed to have a "volcanic temper that sometimes erupted in murderous rages," although he was generally too intelligent to let his ange
2 CONC r get the best of him.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the late 1270s, trouble in the Welsh lands brewed as Llewellyn-ap-Gaffyd declared himself Prince of Wales and began expanding his domain at the expense of the Welsh lords loyal to the English crown. Initially, Edward had li
2 CONC ttle interest in Wales; but Llewellyn refused to subject himself to his feudal obligations to the English monarchy, and Edward was determined to bring Llewellyn back into line. Marching into Wales and leaving a string of fortresse
2 CONC s in his wake, Edward encircled Llewellyn from both land and sea, and Llewellyn eventually sued for peace. In 1282, Llewellyn’s brother David raised the standard of revolt, but in this conflict, both Llewellyn and David were kille
2 CONC d. David was executed, and Llewellyn’s head was hung from London Bridge. To stabilize and entrench English roots in the political situation in Wales, Edward decided to build castles in Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 1290s, there were 13 claimants to the Scottish throne, and they asked Edward to choose a successor from their number. Edward chose John Baliol, whom he thought he could control. Scottish nobles were able to stiffen Bali
2 CONC ol enough to defy Edward. Edward could not allow disobedience from what he considered a feudal underling. Edward personally led the invasion of Scotland in 1296, and the result was a general massacre of the inhabitants of the town
2 CONC  of Bayard. Baliol was deposed, and Edward ruled Scotland through a series of military garrisons. Edward’s brutal conquest unleashed a nationalistic spirit among the Scots; and a Scottish knight, William Wallace, gathered an army 
2 CONC and defeated the English at Stirling Bridge in 1297. Again, Edward invaded Scotland to put down the revolt. In 1298, Wallace was defeated at Falkirk and the Scottish forces were slaughtered. Wallace escaped, but he was eventually 
2 CONC captured, executed, drawn and quartered. This did not diffuse the flames of Scottish revolt, and Edward found himself returning to Scotland to crush rebellion after rebellion. Edward died in 1307 en route to put down a rebellion l
2 CONC ed by Robert the Bruce. Edward’s son, Edward II, returned to Scotland in 1314 and was soundly defeated by Robert the Bruce, who won Scottish independence from England. Edward I was best characterized by his foe Robert the Bruce, w
2 CONC ho was quoted as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT "I am more afraid of the bones of the father dead than of the living son; and by the saints, it was more difficult to get half a foot of land from the old king than a whole kingdom from the son".
2 CONT
2 CONT Lessons to be learned from King Edward I:
2 CONT
2 CONT Do not let your volcanic temper get the better of you and allow you to become engulfed in murderous rages. His pursuit of Montfort’s forces took him away from the main army, which was defeated, and he was captured. In a corporate 
2 CONC setting, this will detract you from wise business decisions, and you may risk market share, sales, etc. Remember, Edward I did not have shareholders to answer to.
2 CONT Know when to command with an iron fist, and know when not to. The iron fist technique worked well with the Welsh, with the castles and execution of the leaders of the rebellion and all that. It failed miserably with the Scots, who
2 CONC  got really angry and refused to stay beaten despite Edward’s skill as a military commander. In a corporate environment, the Welsh, who followed Llewellyn-ap-Gaffyd, would be employees who were able to be subjugated through conque
2 CONC st. The Scottish would be the employees who would stage strikes, slowdowns, work stoppages, complain to the National Labor Relations Board, turn you into the local authorities, initiate lawsuits against you, start their own compan
2 CONC ies that would compete against you, etc. These would be employees not to anger.
2 CONT  Bibliography:
2 CONT
2 CONT Niderost, Eric, Military History Magazine, December 1995, "England’s Warrior King Edward I…" page 10.
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________________________________________________________ ___
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward I, b. June 17, 1239, d. July 7, 1307, king of England (1272-1307) completed the conquest of Wales and temporarily subdued Scotland. He was the eldest son of HENRY III. In 1254 he was made duke of Gascony and married Eleanor
2 CONC  of Castile (d. 1290). In contrast to his father, Edward showed masterfulness in the disputes with the English barons following the governmental reforms instituted by the Provisions of Oxford (1258). He supported Simon de MONTFORT
2 CONC  in 1259 but later changed sides. He fought for the king at the Battle of Lewes (1264) and himself defeated Montfort decisively at Evesham (1265), restoring royal power. In 1271-72 he was on crusade at Acre. During the years from 
2 CONC 1272, when Edward succeeded his father, to 1290 striking achievements occurred. Edward conquered the Welsh principality of Llywelyn ap Gruffydd in devastating campaigns in 1277 and 1282-83 and built massive castles to keep it secu
2 CONC re. In England he held regular parliaments. A program of legislation strengthened royal control over the court system and reformed the tangled feudal land law.
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1294 wars in Scotland and France dominated Edward's reign. The death (1290) of Margaret, Maid of Norway, heiress to the Scottish crown, allowed Edward as suzerain to choose a successor, John de BALIOL, and then to claim dire
2 CONC ct rule over Scotland, which he subdued in 1296. In France the conflict concerned the French king's overlordship over Edward's duchy of Gascony. In 1297, Edward attacked France to assert his rights, but the expedition was cut shor
2 CONC t by the rebellion in Scotland of Sir William WALLACE. At the same time the English nobles rebelled, forcing Edward to grant Parliament control over taxes. By a treaty (1303) with PHILIP IV of France, Edward retained Gascony. He f
2 CONC ailed, however, to quell the risings of Wallace and Robert the Bruce (later ROBERT I), and Scotland remained only half- conquered at his death. He was succeeded by his son Edward II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Costain, Thomas, The Three Edwards (1962).Prestwich, M., Edward I (1988).
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________________________________________________________ ___
2 CONT
2 CONT England's warrior-king Edward I won victories against such renowned foes as Baybars, Llewellyn and Wallace.
2 CONT By Eric Niderost
2 CONT
2 CONT A case can be made that Edward I was the greatest English king of the Middle Ages. A strong ruler, he was a man blessed with a strong sense of duty. Although he was no democrat, he believed the king should promote the general welf
2 CONC are and place himself above class or faction--a revolutionary concept in the 13th century. Although he has been called "the English Justinian" because of his legal codes, Edward was first and foremost a military man, one of the gr
2 CONC eat generals of the medieval world.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward was born in June 1239, the son of King Henry III. Weak and indecisive, Henry was not a bad man--just a bad king. He was devoted to his family and took great pleasure in art and architecture. One of his pet projects was the 
2 CONC rebuilding of Westminster Abbey in the Gothic style that was just coming into vogue. Unfortunately, Henry's private virtues became public vices. Because of his devotion to his wife, he gave the queen's undeserving foreign relative
2 CONC s places at court. Worse still, Henry's building projects were a drain on the exchequer, and his excessive piety made him a dupe of the papacy. That mix of piety, politics and penury--he was always short of funds--bore bitter frui
2 CONC t. Simon de Montfort, leader of the baronial opposition, led an open revolt that defeated the king at the Battle of Lewes in 1264.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lewes gave Prince Edward his first real taste of combat. As a headstrong young blade of 25, he took exception to the London troops of Montfort's army, sincerely believing they had insulted his mother. When the battle opened on May
2 CONC  14, Edward led a cavalry charge that scattered the London burghers like dead leaves in a windstorm. Intoxicated by the chase, he began a single-minded pursuit of his fleeing quarry that took him miles from the battlefield. Once h
2 CONC is thirst for vengeance was appeased, Edward returned to Lewes-- only to find that Montfort had defeated his father's main army. Assailed from both flanks by Montfort's knights, the dumbfounded prince was forced to surrender. But 
2 CONC a great lesson had been learned--from then on, with few exceptions, his intellect would govern his passions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually Edward escaped, joined forces with Roger Mortimer, Earl of Gloucester, and together they defeated Simon de Montfort at Evesham on August 4, 1265. By then, Edward was king in all but name, since his father was growing ol
2 CONC d and was as self-absorbed as ever. Fired with chivalric zeal and a surfeit of youthful energy, Prince Edward "took the cross"--that is, declared himself a crusader pledged to free the Holy Land from the grip of the Muslim "infide
2 CONC ls."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1271, Edward reached the Middle East with a small army of 1,000 men and amazed everyone by chalking up a series of victories over the Muslim forces of the Mamluk Sultan Baybars Bundukdari of Egypt. The prince captured Nazareth,
2 CONC  scoring a moral victory by liberating the hometown of Jesus Christ, but his forces were too small to consolidate his gains.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once, when Edward was resting in his tent, a Muslim assassin broke in and attacked him with a poisoned knife. The prince quickly killed his assailant but was wounded in the arm. Soon the limb swelled, and the foul-smelling flesh g
2 CONC rew black. Gangrene had set in. Handicapped by the lack of medical knowledge at the time, the doctors were baffled and lost hope. But one brave physician cut away the blackened tissue and hoped for the best. By some miracle, Edwar
2 CONC d survived. The next year, 1272, a truce was arranged between Baybars and the Crusaders, enabling Edward to go home at last. While en route to England, he received word that his father was dead and he was now king in his own right
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 2, 1274, the new king landed at Dover after an absence of four years. Crowds gave a tumultuous welcome to their new monarch, who, at 6 feet 2 inches, towered over contemporaries. He was handsome, but his piercing blue ey
2 CONC es were slightly offset by a drooping left eyelid. Like most of his Plantagenet dynasty, Edward had a volcanic temper that sometimes erupted into murderous rages. Generally, though, he was too intelligent to let his anger get the 
2 CONC better of him.
2 CONT
2 CONT A few years after his accession to the throne, Edward was forced to deal with Wales, the mountainous land to the west of England. Politically, Wales was a confusing mosaic of divided loyalties. In the south and central portions of
2 CONC  the country, Anglo-Norman barons, called Marcher lords, managed to subdue and pacify the Welsh tribesmen, but in the north the situation was different. There, a line of Gwynedd princes high in the mountains of Snowdonia refused t
2 CONC o submit to the English yoke. One Welsh ruler, Llewellyn-ap-Graffyd, declared himself prince of Wales and set about expanding his domain at the expense of the Marcher lords.
2 CONT
2 CONT Initially Edward had little interest in Wales, and he might have accepted Llewellyn's independence if the latter had rendered lip service to his feudal obligations to the English crown. But Llewellyn's arrogance seemed to grow wit
2 CONC h his power, and he refused to render homage to Edward. Thoroughly aroused, the king was determined to bring his rebellious vassal to heel.
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 1277, in the town of Worcester, Edward gathered one of the biggest armies ever seen in Britain. The feudal levy summoned 1,000 armored knights, while a number of English shires--Cheshire, Derbyshire, Shropshire and others-
2 CONC -supplied about 15,000 foot soldiers, including many Welshmen and Gascon crossbowmen.
2 CONT
2 CONT The northern Welsh under Llewellyn were not prepared to meet Edward on his own terms, so they melted back into the misty valleys and snow-dappled peaks of their mountainous homeland. Natural guerrillas, they lived off the land whe
2 CONC n fighting and generally preferred ambushes to pitched battles.
2 CONT
2 CONT The men of southern Wales generally had spears, but the northern tribes possessed a formidable new weapon--the longbow. One chronicler described it as "made of wild elm, unpolished, rude and uncouth," but in the hands of a trained
2 CONC  archer it was a formidable weapon, hitting targets with such force that a longbow shaft could pierce chain mail and pin a man to his horse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward advanced along the north Welsh coast, marching slowly up the valleys of the Severn and the Dee. Leaving a chain of rising fortresses in his wake, Edward continued on until he reached the mouth of the Conway River. There, th
2 CONC e king unveiled his trump card--sea power. Just off the coast, on the island of Anglesey, was some of the most fertile soil in Wales, the breadbasket of Llewellyn's tribes. Thanks to ships provided by Edward's Cinque Ports, Angles
2 CONC ey was quickly taken.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ringed in by hostile troops and threatened by starvation, Llewellyn sued for peace. After a few years' respite, however, Llewellyn's brother David raised the standard of revolt. The 1282 rebellion was a replay of the 1277 campaign
2 CONC , but this time Llewellyn was killed in a chance encounter, and his head was sent to adorn London Bridge. David was captured and executed, and the rebellion he had hatched collapsed.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward decided that only more castles could help sink English roots and stabilize the shifting political soil of Wales. Luckily for the king, his reign coincided with the great age of medieval military architecture, and he found a
2 CONC  builder of genius in Master James of St. George.
2 CONT
2 CONT Master James' fertile imagination produced a series of elaborate designs, each adapted to the particular needs of an individual site. Even today, Conway, Harlech, Rhuddlan, Beaumaris and Caernarvon castles give an overwhelming imp
2 CONC ression of strength and majesty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wales was pacified, at least for the moment, so Edward turned his attention to Scotland. The Scottish throne was empty, and there were no less than 13 claimants for it. To solve the impasse, the claimants asked Edward to be arbite
2 CONC r and choose a candidate among their number. The English king should have known better; the Scottish succession was a morass of claims and counterclaims.
2 CONT
2 CONT After fevered consultations with barons, lawyers and churchmen, Edward chose John Bailol as king of the Scots. Bailol was a weakling, but the fractious Scottish nobles stiffened his backbone enough to defy Edward. Once again, Edwa
2 CONC rd could brook no disobedience from a man he considered his feudal underling. The English monarch invaded Scotland with a large army, and in March 1296, he proceeded to besiege the important Scottish town of Berwick. Feeling overc
2 CONC onfident, the citizens of Berwick shouted insults at Edward, in particular making fun of his "long shanks."
2 CONT
2 CONT Mounted on his great warhorse Bayard, Edward personally led the assault on Berwick. Hooves flailing, Bayard leapt across a ditch, bounded over a low palisade and brought his royal master into the very heart of the city. Soon Engli
2 CONC sh troops poured into the narrow streets and fighting gave way to a general massacre of the inhabitants.
2 CONT
2 CONT In short order Bailol was deposed, and Edward ruled the northern kingdom through a series of military garrisons. But Edward's brutal conquest had unleashed a sort of early nationalistic spirit among the Scots. A Scottish knight, W
2 CONC illiam Wallace, gathered an army and managed to defeat an English force at Stirling Bridge on September 11, 1297. With his prestige on the line, Edward--though he was now growing old--took to the field once again and invaded Scotl
2 CONC and.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 22, 1298, the English and Scottish armies met at Falkirk. The backbone of Wallace's forces was his infantry, drawn up in four phalanx-style formations called schiltrons. Bristling with spears, the schiltrons seemed invulne
2 CONC rable to the kind of cavalry charge favored by medieval knights. And sure enough, before Edward could fully deploy his unwieldy army, his knights rushed forward in a headlong charge. Try as they might, the English knights could ma
2 CONC ke no impression on the prickly Scottish formations, and round one went to the stubborn Celts.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT But Edward had a surprise waiting in the wings--swarms of Welsh archers, who came forward in large numbers to discharge their deadly shafts. The schiltrons were quickly reduced to heaps of dead and wounded men, and the remaining S
2 CONC cottish infantry became easy prey for Edward's cavalry. Only Wallace and a handful of fugitives escaped the terrible slaughter, and the back of Scottish resistance seemed broken forever. At Falkirk, Edward Long Shanks acquired a n
2 CONC ew nickname: Scottorum malleus (Hammer of the Scots). The battle validated his reputation as a general and showcased his tactical skills. His adoption of the Welsh longbow foreshadowed the English triumphs at Crécy, Poitiers and A
2 CONC gincourt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, Wallace was captured and hanged, drawn and quartered, but his grisly fate left the Scots uncowed. Time and again, Edward had to return to Scotland in an attempt to crush the embers of revolt. Yet every time he returned
2 CONC  home, the flame of Scots nationalism would blaze anew. A new Scottish champion, Robert the Bruce, declared himself king of Scotland and girded himself for another English invasion. It was not long in coming.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward, white-haired and ailing, must have felt he was an English Sisyphus, condemned to roll the rock of conquest forward again and again. At 69--something akin to 90 by the standards of the Middle Ages--the king had little reaso
2 CONC n to find happiness in his waning years. His son and heir, Prince Edward of Caernarvon, was a homosexual and a worthless spendthrift, more interested in fine clothes than the arts of war.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Edward moved forward toward Scotland, but his battle-scarred and aging body could not obey the commands of his iron will. He died on July 6, 1307, a short distance from the Scottish border at Burgh-on-Sands. Later, Edward II 
2 CONC would return to Scotland in force--only to suffer a humiliating defeat at the hands of Robert the Bruce at Bannockburn, on June 23, 1314, by which Scotland won its independence from England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although he was not the equal of a Caesar or Napoleon, Edward I was still a great commander who grasped the essentials of war. Even his enemies recognized his military greatness. Comparing Edward I to his son Edward II, Robert the
2 CONC  Bruce once declared, "I am more afraid of the bones of the father dead, than of the living son; and, by all the saints, it was more difficult to get a half a foot of the land from the old king than a whole kingdom from the son!"
