1 NOTE King Edward I England 
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=3d2847af-40d4-4974-9c77-06bcc3bf2b90&tid=6650027&pid=-1255272783
1 NOTE Edward 1 a
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=d29f9478-171f-4a5a-b7d8-67c9a4ace30a&tid=10844759&pid=-554332360
1 NOTE
2 CONT Edward I, (Longshanks) was King of England (1272-1307) conquering Wales and warring with Scotland. In the struggles of the barons against the crown for constitutional and ecclesiastical reforms, Edward took a vacillating course. W
2 CONC hen warfare broke out between the crown and the nobility, Edward fought on the side of the king, winning the decisive battle of Evesham in 1265. Five years later he leftEngland to join the Seventh Crusade. Following his father's d
2 CONC eath in 1272, and while he was still abroad, Edward was recognized as king by the English barons; in 1273, on his return to England, he was crowned. On the refusal of Llewelyn ab Gruffydd, ruler of Wales, to submit to the English 
2 CONC crown, Edward began the military conflict that resulted, in 1284, in the annexation of Llewelyn's principality to the English crown.  His Model Parliament of 1295 is sometimes considered England's first full parliament.  Edward lo
2 CONC st Gascony in 1293 and did not again come into possession of the duchy until 1303. About the same year in which he lost Gascony, the Welsh rose in rebellion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward was a special child to his father. He was born very late in Henry's life. He was named after the canon, Edward the Confessor, and although his title says Edward I, there were three Edwards previous to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was Henry who arranged for the important marriage of Edward to Eleanor, the half-sister of Alfonso X, King of Castile and Leon. It was an arranged marriage which bore many children and was full oflove.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward was made Overlord of Ireland, before he became King, and was responsible for Gascony and Wales. He was a typically spoiled adolescent, and liked to spend his time setting up jousting tournaments, in which many lives would b
2 CONC e lost at a time. However though he had once recognized the justice of Simon de Montfort's stance against his father, he rallied to help his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was his role as a general that helped quash De Montfort, and after this he became an exemplary figure in the ruling of England. He was his father's Regent and succeeded unchallenged to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT He did not become King until the age of 35 and was devoted to personal and political integrity. He was a devoted ruler of England and developed state relations all around the world. He also fought many wars and used a great deal o
2 CONC f the funds of England in these battles. He borrowed heavily from the Jews in England. They were expert currency manipulators.
2 CONT
2 CONT The King had tried to involve them in more productive occupations, but against all local feeling, and the lack of profit to be attained, the Jews went back to their old vocations. In 1290 Edward expelled the Jews from England. Bei
2 CONC ng unable to borrow money, Edward had to impose high taxes on the local populations, which was of course highly unpopular.
2 CONT
2 CONT After having defeated and slain the last Welsh Prince of Wales, Llywelyn ap Gruffydd, Edward offered his baby son to the Welsh people as the Prince of Wales, in a symbolic gesture. The Welsh gave theEnglish knowledge of the long b
2 CONC ow, one of the most valued weapons before gunpowder, and Edward encouraged its use.
2 CONT
2 CONT The pacification of the Welsh was not instant, and it took years for the land and power to be delegated in face of harsh resentment.
2 CONT
2 CONT War with Scotland was causing great problems. It concerned an attempt to reconcile the center of Scotland from Edinburgh to Scone, the natural but unrecognized seat of Scotland. When Alexander, III, King of Scotland died, the crow
2 CONC n passed to his three year old grand daughter, Margaret. When Margaret was aged six, Edward arranged for her to be betrothed to his heir Edward, which would have led to a peaceful union of England and Scotland. Margaret died in a 
2 CONC ship wreck on the way to her coronation in Scotland, then the succession to the crown was disputed. Edward stepped in to arbitrate and witha balanced Commission took eighteen months to choose John Balliol.
2 CONT
2 CONT This decision led to a revolt, which Edward managed to overcome. He declared himself King of Scotland and carried the Coronation Stone of Scotland from Scone Palace off to England. It was this that led to the revolt by William Wal
2 CONC lace who was eventually defeated by Edward.  Wallace was captured and executed in 1305. No sooner had Edward established his government in Scotland, however, than a new revolt broke out and culminated in the coronation of Robert B
2 CONC ruce as king of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1306 Robert the Bruce was declared King of the Scots by the Scottish people and in the ensuing war he was at first defeated by Edward. In 1307 Edward set out for the third time to subdue the Scots, but he died en route near Car
2 CONC lisle on July 7, 1307   Edward died at Burgh by Sands, after being the monarch of England for 35 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was a good husband and loved his children and it was his offspring that provided England with two of the greatest monarchs of all time. But it is difficult to understand with all the aggressivestances he took, how he earned 
2 CONC his reputation as being a lawgiver and administrator and a caring man who deeply mourned his wife by erecting the Eleanor Crosses (which marked the route taken by QueenEleanor's funeral carriage), and then married the French King'
2 CONC s sister just to seal a peace treaty. Unknown GEDCOM info: MH:N76 Unknown GEDCOM info: C0F09D07-8CC3-4430-8B24-1BEB222E9728
