1 NOTE Edward I
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=7a59a104-c5b6-482c-b6ab-a186fc42e955&tid=2456826&pid=78673074
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. Dict. of Nat'l Biog., Eng. Pub. A, v. 6, p. 432, v. 17, p. 14-48
2 CONT     2. Royal Dau of Engl, Eng. 120, v. 1, p. 61, 172-209
2 CONT     3. Burke's Peerage, Eng. P, 1949, pref. p. 254
2 CONT     4. The Complete Peerage, G.E.C., Eng. V, v. 5, p. 708-12, v. 6, p. 469
2 CONT     5. Espolin (GS #12462, pt 1, p. 98-99)
2 CONT
2 CONT !RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT     A daughter Isabel has been mentioned but cannot be verified--possibly synonymous with one of the others already mentioned (see Eng. 120, v. 1, p. 193)
2 CONT
2 CONT !HISTORICAL NOTES:
2 CONT     Edward I is sometimes referred to as "the English Justinian". He had a lovefor justice, honor, and order in his affairs. At one point in his reign, he faced a declaration of war with France and rebellions from the Welsh and Sc
2 CONC ots. He decided that the only way to overcome his difficulties would be to elicit the support of his people. In 1295 he called together a parliament consisting of representatives of the nobility, the church, and the common people.
2 CONC  This "Model parliament" marked the beginning of parliamentary government in England, a system which has continued to the present day. "What touches all," Edward proclaimed, "should be approved by all, and it is also clear that co
2 CONC mmon dangers should be met bymeasures agreed upon in common." He restricted the power of the king by accepting the rule that taxes could not be levied or laws made except by the consent ofparliament. Sixty-two of the families in R
2 CONC oyal Ancestors are shown to descend from Edward I.
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. Dict. of Nat'l Biog., Eng. Pub. A, v. 6, p. 432, v. 17, p. 14-48
2 CONT     2. Royal Dau of Engl, Eng. 120, v. 1, p. 61, 172-209
2 CONT     3. Burke's Peerage, Eng. P, 1949, pref. p. 254
2 CONT     4. The Complete Peerage, G.E.C., Eng. V, v. 5, p. 708-12, v. 6, p. 469
2 CONT     5. Espolin (GS #12462, pt 1, p. 98-99)
2 CONT !RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT     A daughter Isabel has been mentioned but cannot be verified--possibly synonymous with one of the others already mentioned (see Eng. 120, v. 1, p. 193)
2 CONT !HISTORICAL NOTES:
2 CONT     Edward I is sometimes referred to as "the English Justinian". He had a lovefor justice, honor, and order in his affairs. At one point in his reign, he faced a declaration of war with France and rebellions from the Welsh and Sc
2 CONC ots. He decided that the only way to overcome his difficulties would be to elicit the support of his people. In 1295 he called together a parliament consisting of representatives of the nobility, the church, and the common people.
2 CONC  This "Model parliament" marked the beginning of parliamentary government in England, a system which has continued to the present day. "What touches all," Edward proclaimed, "should be approved by all, and it is also clear that co
2 CONC mmon dangers should be met bymeasures agreed upon in common." He restricted the power of the king by accepting the rule that taxes could not be levied or laws made except by the consent ofparliament. Sixty-two of the families in R
2 CONC oyal Ancestors are shown to descend from Edward I.
1 NOTE King of England, Wales, Scotland, and Ireland, the Isle of Man (from 1290) and ruler in south-west France (Duke of Aquitaine).
2 CONT House of Plantagenet.
2 CONT The first truly English king, Edward proved a masterful ruler, military genius and great legislator, taking advice from able lawyers and ministers.
2 CONT  To raise cash for government and the army in 1295, Edward called the Model Parliament.  Representing nobles, Church, and commoners, this foreshadowing representative government and decreed that the King needed Parliament's approv
2 CONC al to make laws or raise non-feudal taxes.  Edward backed laws curbing Church power and curtailing feudal rights.  Under Edward, the great common law courts (King's Bench, Exchequer, Common Pleas) took shape.
2 CONT Edward forcefully stamped his authority on Wales and Scotland.  He invaded Wales, killing Welsh princes, establishing English control (1277-83) with the aid of nine new castles. On 7 February 1301 hecreated the title Prince of Wal
2 CONC es for his fourth son, born at Caernarvon Castle.  From 1296 to his death, Edward fought wars to conquer Scotland.  He defeated John de Balliol (1296) and William Wallace (1298) but died on his way to subdue Robert the Bruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward I is the outstanding English king of the Middle Ages.  A great soldier and a wise statesman, he initiated constitutional reforms that laid the foundations of parliamentary government.
2 CONT Edward, who was named after the revered Edward the Confessor, inherited none of his father's weaknesses and took far more after his very able uncle Richard of Cornwall.  He also possessed his mother's strength of character without
2 CONC  her pleasure-loving frivolity.  The young 'Lord Edward', as he was known, loyally supported his father throughout the civil war and Montfort's rebellion.  At the age offifteen, Edward journeyed to Spain where he received the hono
2 CONC r of knighthood from King Alfonso X of Castile and the hand of Alfonso's half-sister the Infanta Eleanor.  She became the love of his lifeand, like his father before him, he was a faithful husband.
2 CONT By 1270 peace had been restored to the country and Edward went off on crusades, accompanied by Eleanor.  When Henry III died in November 1272, Edward was in Sicily making his slow way back.  England,he felt, was safe under his mot
2 CONC her's regency and he did not hurry, arriving in the summer of 1274.  He and Eleanor were crowned together at Westminster Abbey on 19 August.
2 CONT Edward's encouragement of 'parliaments', which he used to keep in touch with the problems and needs of the country, has led him to be described as the 'Father of the Mother of Parliaments'.  His relentless, but unsuccessful, attem
2 CONC pts to assert his overlordships of the Scottish kingdom have earned him the grim title of 'Hammer of the Scots', which was to be inscribed on his simple tomb in Westminster.  His Welsh campaign was more successful and the country 
2 CONC was completely subjugated in two wars, ending with the deaths of Llywellyn and David, the last two native princes.  A policy of castle building ensured that Wales remained under English rule and the mighty fortresses of Rhuddlan, 
2 CONC Conway, Denbigh, Harlech and Caernarvon still stand.
2 CONT Edward's reign was one of architectural flowering and many cathedrals and abbey churches were begun or rebuilt, including Exeter, Lichfield and York Minster, while the first scientific attempt at town planning took place at Winche
2 CONC lsea, which remains a delight to visitors today.
2 CONT Edward's beloved Eleanor died in 1290 and nine years later, at the age of sixty, he married the twenty-year-old Margaret of France.  The marriage was not unhappy, in spite of the great disparity in age, and three more children wer
2 CONC e born, making Edward the father of more legitimate children than any other English king before or since.
2 CONT In June 1307 Edward was again campaigning in the north when he was struck down by dysentery.  He died at Burgh-on-the-Sands, near Carlisle, on 7 July , having just completed his sixty-eight year.  Heis buried in Westminster Abbey,
2 CONC  despite his wish that his bones were to be carried by his son until the Scottish people had been totally subdued.  On his tomb is inscribed Eduardus primus, Scotorum malleus hic est - Here is Edward, hammer of the Scots.  A great
2 CONC  King, who said of himself, 'I should not be a better one however splendidly I was dressed'.
