1 NOTE 	1  NAME Crouchback //
2 CONT 	2  GIVN Crouchback
2 CONT 	2  SURN
2 CONT 	2  NICK Crouchback
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Edmund Plantegenet by name Crouchback (b. Jan. 16, 1245, London, England - d. c.\June 5, 1296, Bayonne, France), fourth (but second surviving) son of King Henry III of England and Eleanor of Provence, who founded the house of Lanc
2 CONC aster.      At the age 10, Edmund was invested by Pope Innocent IV with the kingdom of Sicily (April 1255) , as an expression of his conflict with the Holy Roman emperor, who held Sicily; but Edmund was never more than an absentee
2 CONC  titular king, and Pope Alexander IV canceled the grant (December 1258).      In 1265 Edmund received the earldom of Leicester, and two years later was created Earl of Lancaster. He joined the crusade of his elder brother, the Lor
2 CONC d Edward (1271-1272); and Edward on his accession as King Edward I, found in Edmund a loyal supporter. In 1275, two years after the death of his first wife, Edmund married Blanche of Artois, the widow of Henry III of Navarre and C
2 CONC hampagne, and assumed the title Count Palatine of Champagne and Brie. When the court of King Philip IV of France pronounced that the king of England had forfeited Gascony, Edmund renounced his homage to Philip and withdrew with hi
2 CONC s wife to England. He was appointed lieutenant of Gascony in 1296but died in the same year, leaving his Son Thomas to succeed him in his English possession.      Edmund's nickname "Crouchback" (meaning "Crossback," or crusader) wa
2 CONC s misinterpreted, probably intentionally, by his direct descendant. King Henry IV, who, in claiming the throne (1399), asserted that Edmund had really been Henry III's eldest son but had been disinherited as a hunchback.
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. The Complete Peerage, G.E.C., Eng. V, v. 7, p. 368-401, 547-48
2 CONT     2. Dict. of Nat'l Biog., Eng. Pub. A, v. 32, p. 33-35
2 CONT     3. Royal Dau of Engl, Eng. 120, v. 1, p. 60-61
2 CONT     4. Tab. Souv. Gen., France 22, Tab. 21, 46
2 CONT     5. Burke's Peerage, Eng. P, 1949, preface p. 253
