1 NOTE Comte John de Monfort, imprisoned by Charles of Blois, was helped to escape by his wife from the Louvre in disguise, only to die after reaching Brittany about 1345.
2 CONT
2 CONT When John of Monfort, half brother of the John  Duke of Brittany, became one of the heirs to Brittany in 1341, this became a battle with his niece, Jeanne de Penthievre, a rival heir.  Her claim was taken up by her husband, Charle
2 CONC s of Blois, who claimed the dukedom for himself. Charles then pursued Jean de Montfort, aka as John, and sent him to Paris to be held prisoner by Philip VI.
1 NOTE In the dispute over claim to the duchy of Brittany, John IV de Montfort fought Charles of Blois for the right between 1341-1345.
1 NOTE DUKE OF BRITTANY; COUNT OF MONTFORT; LORD OF RICHMOND; 4TH SON OF ARTHUR II;
2 CONT AFTER HIS INITIAL BURIAL, HIS BODY WAS TRANSFERRED TO THE JACOBIN CONVENT
