2 CONC nclave within Brabant: it was agreed that it would now come under full Brabançon control. Despite the diplomacy of Edward, John remained true to his French commitments until his death in December 1355.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1311, as his father's gesture of rapprochement with France, John married Marie of Évreux (1303–1335),[3] the daughter of Prince Louis, Count of Évreux and Margaret of Artois. They had:
2 CONT
2 CONT #     Joanna, Duchess of Brabant (24 June 1322 – 1406), married first to William IV, Count of Holland and second to Wenceslaus I, Duke of Luxembourg.[8]
2 CONT #     Margaret of Brabant (9 February 1323 – 1368), married at Saint-Quentin on 6 June 1347 Louis II, Count of Flanders,[9] she is the ancestor of later dukes
2 CONT #     Marie of Brabant (1325 – 1 March 1399), Lady of Turnhout, married at Tervuren on 1 July 1347 to Reginald III of Guelders[10]
2 CONT #     John of Brabant (1327–1335/36), married Marie of France (1326–1333), daughter of King Philip VI of France,[a] but died soon after with no issue, buried in Tervueren.
2 CONT #     Henri of Brabant (d. 29 October 1349), Duke of Limburg and Lord of Mechelen in 1347. Died young and buried in Tervuren in 1349.
2 CONT #     Godfrey of Brabant (d. aft. 3 February 1352), Lord of Aarschot in 1346. Also died young and buried in Tervuren.
2 CONT
2 CONT John also had a son born from Elisabeth Maria of Huldenberg (d. 1365), who founded the House of Brant: John I Brant, 1st Lord of Ayseau.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1355, after all of his three legitimate sons had died, John was forced to declare his eldest daughter Joanna his heiress,[8] which provoked a succession crisis after his death. John III was buried in the Cistercian Abbey of Vil
2 CONC lers, Belgium.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Henneman refers to Marie's husband as "duke of brabant's heir".[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree/person/LZ6F-VF9
