1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Blanche of France, Duchess of Orléans
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of France (1328-1382) was the posthumous daughter of Charles IV of France by his third wife Jeanne d'Évreux. Her paternal grandparents were Philip IV of France and Joan I of Navarre, her maternal grandparents were Louis d'
2 CONC Évreux and Margaret of Artois.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche married on 8 January 1345 to her cousin Philip of Valois, Duke of Orleans (1336-1375), son of Philip VI of France and Joan the Lame. They had no children but Philip had illegitimate children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip died in 1376, his title and lands returning to the royal domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche died in 1382 and is buried in the chapel of Notre-Dame.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Succession
2 CONT
2 CONT As with his brother before him, Charles IV died without a male heir, thus ending the direct line of the Capetian dynasty. Twelve years earlier, a rule against succession by females, arguably derived from the Salic Law, had been re
2 CONC cognized as controlling succession to the French throne. Application of this rule barred Blanche's 1-year-old daughter Mary by his third wife, Jeanne d'Évreux, from succeeding as the monarch. Jeanne was also pregnant at the time o
2 CONC f his death. Since it could have been possible that she would give birth to a son, a regency was set up with the heir presumptive Philip of Valois, a member of the House of Valois (the next-most-senior branch of the Capetian dynas
2 CONC ty), being the regent. After two months, Jeanne gave birth to Blanche. The regent thus became the King and in May was consecrated and crowned Philip VI. At this time, a further rule of succession, again arguably based on the Salic
2 CONC  Law, was recognized as forbidding not only inheritance by a woman, but also inheritance through a female line.
