1 NOTE
2 CONT Dear Newsgroup ~
2 CONT
2 CONT Contemporary medieval records indicate that King Henry VI of England
2 CONT was related to King Alfonso V, King of Aragon [Reference: Thomas
2 CONT Rymer, Foedera 11(1727): 18 ([Alfonso V], King of Aragón styled
2 CONT "cousin")].
2 CONT
2 CONT Were King Henry VI of England and King Alfonso V of Aragaon related
2 CONT within the 5th degree on at least one side.  Answer: Yes, they were.
2 CONT
2 CONT Below please find a chart which shows King Henry VI and King Alfonso
2 CONT V's common descent from Fadrique II, King of Sicily (died 1337).  The
2 CONT chart indicates the two kings were related in the 5th degree of
2 CONT kindred (that is, 4th cousins).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT                     Fadrique II, King of Sicily
2 CONT           __________________/_________________
2 CONT          /                                   /
2 CONT Isabelle/Elizabeth of Sicily         Pedro II, King of Sicily
2 CONT =Stephen II, Duke of Bavaria                 /
2 CONT         /                                    /
2 CONT Stephen III, Duke of Bavaria         Leonora of Sicily
2 CONT         /                            = Pedro IV, King of Aragon
2 CONT         /                                    /
2 CONT Isabeau of Bavaria                   Eleanore of Aragon
2 CONT = Charles VI, King of France         = Juan I, King of Castile
2 CONT         /                                    /
2 CONT Katherine of France                  Fernando I, King of Aragon
2 CONT =Henry V, King of England              and Sicily
2 CONT         /                                    /
2 CONT Henry VI, King of England            Alfonso V, King of Aragon
1 NOTE As Alfonso I, king of Naples. Called The Magnanimous.  He earned his 
2 CONT nickname when, upon his ascension to the throne, he destroyed a list 
2 CONT containing the names of nobles who had been hostile to him. He is renowned 
2 CONT chiefly for having brought southern Italy under the dominion of Aragón. In 
2 CONT 1420 Alfonso attacked Corsica, but hastened to Naples at the request of 
2 CONT its queen, Joanna II (1371-1435), who, in return for his assistance 
2 CONT against Louis III of Anjou, named Alfonso her heir. In 1423 the queen 
2 CONT transferred her favor from Alfonso to his rival, Louis. On her death in 
2 CONT 1435 Alfonso claimed the kingdom, but Duke René of Anjou (1409-80), 
2 CONT successor of Louis, opposed him. The pope and Genoa sided with René. The 
2 CONT Genoese fleet defeated the fleet of Aragón, and Alfonso was captured. He 
2 CONT was sent to Francesco Sforza, duke of Milan, who set him at liberty and 
2 CONT formed an alliance with him. After several battles Alfonso defeated René 
2 CONT and entered Naples in triumph, transferring his court there and becoming 
2 CONT the founder of Spanish power in Italy as Alfonso I.
1 NOTE KING OF ARAGON 1416-1458
