2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1217, Berengaria's brother Ferdinand III had inherited the throne of the Kingdom of Castile through abdication of their mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1223, John of Brienne, aged 53, visited Santiago de Compostela, as a supposed pilgrim. He was by then twice a widower: first, in 1213 of Queen Maria of Jerusalem and then of Stephanie, daughter of Leo II of Armenia, (1150 - kin
2 CONC g of Cilicia in Armenia 1199 - 1219).
2 CONT
2 CONT As a consequence of his visit to Santiago de Compostela, Alfonso IX invited him to marry Sancha and, presumably, through her inherit the Leonese throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT However Berengaria of Castile, a long time divorced and an inheritor in her own right of the Castilian throne, main advisor of her son Ferdinand III, offered one of her own daughters to John instead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aging John chose Berengaria of Leon, from Alfonso IX's second doomed marriage, who was a decade younger than her half-sister Sancha, from Alfonso IX's first doomed marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage took place at Toledo in 1224.
2 CONT
2 CONT Empress
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1229, the throne of the Latin Empire had been inherited by Baldwin II of Courtenay, a twelve-year -old boy. The barons of the Empire decided to secure the safety of the Empire by appointing a regent-Emperor for Baldwin. They ch
2 CONC ose John who accepted the assignment as a sort of Senior Tutor. In April 1229, John was proclaimed regent at Perugia. They did not arrive at Constantinople until 1231, when John was officially crowned in his new city.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baldwin II remained the junior co-emperor and only heir to the throne. By agreement, 12 years old Baldwin had been betrothed to around 4 or 5 years old Marie of Brienne, a daughter of John and Berengaria, since 19 April 1229 to fi
2 CONC rmly establish the dynastic alliance of the two co-emperors and the Western Land for Crusades also, Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage did not take place until 1234, when Marie was about nine-years-old and Baldwin about 16.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alberic of Trois-Fontaines records that John died on 27 March 1237, aged around 61. The "Obituaires de Sens Tome" of the Abbey of Maubuisson record that Berenguela died on 12 April 1237, aged around 33, surviving her husband by on
2 CONC ly sixteen days.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is buried in a beautiful marble coffin in the Cathedral of Santiago de Compostela, Spain, but many books and tourist leaflets mention her as if she was Queen Berenguela of Castile, her mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other books however, mention this coffin as being that of another Berengaria (1228–1288) and/or other dates, her niece, daughter of her uncle the King Ferdinand III of Castile, sister of King Alfonso X of Castile. But this niece, 
2 CONC Infanta Berengaria was a nun at the Monastery of las Huelgas, Burgos, of Royal patronage, where Berengaria of Leon's mother, Queen Berenguela of Castile, retired as a former monarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further, Alfonso VIII of Castile and Eleanor of England, grandparents of Berengaria of Leon are buried there. Much later, Ferdinand III's and Alfonso X's bodies at Las Huelgas Monastery, near Burgos, would be moved to conquered Se
2 CONC villa's new Cathedral, where they are today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria and John of Brienne had four known children, known to have been residents in Spain with his cousin, King Alfonso X of Castile, signing widely in many documents for many years with other high nobility witnesses, Bishops 
2 CONC and Archbishops, Royal legitimate and illegitimate offspring and so on :
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Marie of Brienne (1225–1275), who married Emperor Baldwin II of Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Alphonso of Brienne (c. 1228-1270), who married Marie d'Issoudon, countess of Eu, and became count of Eu in right of his wife, and was also Great Chamberlain of France. Died of a plague while involved in the Eighth Crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. John of Brienne (c. 1230-1296), who in 1258 became Grand Butler of France. Married Jeanne, Dame de Chateaudun, daughter of Geoffrey VI, Viscount de Chateaudun, as his first wife. His second wife was Marie de Coucy, widow of 
2 CONC King Alexander II of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Louis of Acre (c. 1235-1263), who married Agnes of Beaumont and became Viscount of Beaumont in her right.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Berengaria de Castilla was born circa 1198/99. She was the daughter of Alfonso IX, Rey de Castilla y León and Berengaria de Castilla.1 She married Jean I de Brienne, Emperor of Constantinople, son of Erard II, Comte de Brienne and
2 CONC  Agnes de Montfaucon, in 1223.2 She died on 12 April 1237.2 Children of Berengaria de Castilla and Jean I de Brienne, Emperor of Constantinople
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louis de Brienne+1 d. a 1263
2 CONT     * Alphonse, Comte d'Eu+2 d. 25 Aug 1270
2 CONT     * Marie de Brienne+2 b. 1225, d. a 5 May 1275
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p4190.htm#i41898
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berenguela_of_Leon
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Berengaria of León (1204 – 12 April 1237) was the third wife but only empress consort of John of Brienne, Latin Emperor of Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the chronicle of Alberic of Trois-Fontaines, Berengaria was a daughter of King Alfonso IX of León and Queen Berengaria I of Castile. She was a younger sister of Ferdinand III of Castile and Alfonso of Molina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their paternal grandparents were Ferdinand II of León and Urraca of Portugal. Their maternal grandparents were Alfonso VIII of Castile and Eleanor of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1217, Berengaria's brother Ferdinand III had inherited the throne of the Kingdom of Castile through abdication of their mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1223, John of Brienne, aged 53, visited Santiago de Compostela, as a supposed pilgrim. He was by then twice a widower: first, in 1213 of Queen Maria of Jerusalem and then of Stephanie, daughter of Leo II of Armenia, (1150 – kin
2 CONC g of Cilicia in Armenia 1199–1219).
2 CONT
2 CONT As a consequence of his visit to Santiago de Compostela, Alfonso IX invited him to marry Sancha and, presumably, through her inherit the Leonese throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT However Berengaria of Castile, a long time divorced and an inheritor in her own right of the Castilian throne, main advisor of her son Ferdinand III, offered one of her own daughters to John instead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aging John chose Berengaria of León, from Alfonso IX's second doomed marriage, who was a decade younger than her half-sister Sancha, from Alfonso IX's first doomed marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage took place at Toledo in 1224.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1229, the throne of the Latin Empire had been inherited by Baldwin II of Courtenay, a twelve-year-old boy. The barons of the Empire decided to secure the safety of the Empire by appointing a regent-Emperor for Baldwin. They cho
2 CONC se John who accepted the assignment as a sort of Senior Tutor. In April 1229, John was proclaimed regent at Perugia. They did not arrive at Constantinople until 1231, when John was officially crowned in his new city.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baldwin II remained the junior co-emperor and only heir to the throne. By agreement, 12-year-old Baldwin had been betrothed to around 4- or 5-years-old Marie of Brienne, a daughter of John and Berengaria, since 19 April 1229 to fi
2 CONC rmly establish the dynastic alliance of the two co-emperors and the western land for Crusades also, Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage did not take place until 1234, when Marie was about nine years old and Baldwin about 16.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alberic of Trois-Fontaines records that John died on 27 March 1237, aged around 61. The "Obituaires de Sens Tome" of the Abbey of Maubuisson record that Berenguela died on 12 April 1237, aged around 33, surviving her husband by on
2 CONC ly sixteen days.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is buried in a beautiful marble coffin in the Cathedral of Santiago de Compostela, Spain, but many books and tourist leaflets mention her as if she was Queen Berenguela of Castile, her mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other books however, mention this coffin as being that of another Berengaria (1228–1288) and/or other dates, her niece, daughter of her uncle the King Ferdinand III of Castile, sister of King Alfonso X of Castile. But this niece, 
2 CONC Infanta Berengaria was a nun at the Monastery of las Huelgas, Burgos, of Royal patronage, where Berengaria of León's mother, Queen Berenguela of Castile, retired as a former monarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further, Alfonso VIII of Castile and Eleanor of England, grandparents of Berengaria of León are buried there. Much later, Ferdinand III's and Alfonso X's bodies at Las Huelgas Monastery, near Burgos, would be moved to conquered Se
2 CONC ville's new cathedral, where they are today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria and John of Brienne had four known children, known to have been residents in Spain with his cousin, King Alfonso X of Castile, signing widely in many documents for many years with other high nobility witnesses, Bishops 
2 CONC and Archbishops, Royal legitimate and illegitimate offspring and so on :
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie of Brienne (1225–1275), who married Emperor Baldwin II of Constantinople.  Alphonso of Brienne (c. 1228–1270), who married Marie d'Issoudon, countess of Eu, and became count of Eu in right of his wife, and was also Great Cha
2 CONC mberlain of France. Died of a plague while involved in the Eighth Crusade.  John of Brienne (c. 1230–1296), who in 1258 became Grand Butler of France. Married Jeanne, Dame de Chateaudun, daughter of Geoffrey VI, Viscount de Chatea
2 CONC udun, as his first wife. His second wife was Marie de Coucy, widow of King Alexander II of Scotland.  Louis of Acre (c. 1235–1263), who married Agnes of Beaumont and became Viscount of Beaumont in her right. 
2 CONT
