1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-2782
1 NOTE Armorial Général de France: Les SABRAN
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-7369
1 NOTE     FGRA; MORIARTY P.68, 124
1 NOTE One of the last great Provencal poets, whose court was renowned for its patronage of the troubadours.
1 NOTE !Name is;  Raymond Berenger V, Count Of /PROVENCE AND FORCALQUIER/
1 NOTE Ramon Berenguer IV (1195 - 19 August 1245), Count of Provence and Forcalquier, was the son of Alfonso II of Provence and Garsenda of Sabran, heiress of Forcalquier. After his father's death (1209), Ramon was imprisoned in the cast
2 CONC le of Monzón, in Aragon until he was able to escape in 1219 and claim his inheritance. He was a powerful and energetic ruler who added Forcalquier to his domain. Giovanni Villani in his Nuova Cronica had this to say about Raymond
2 CONC :
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Raymond was a lord of gentle lineage, and kin to them of the house of Aragon, and to the family of the count of Toulouse, By inheritance Provence, this side of the Rhone, was his; a wise and courteous lord was he, and of nob
2 CONC le state and virtuous, and in his time did honourable deeds, and to his court came all gentle persons of Provence and of France and of Catalonia, by reason of his courtesy and noble estate, and he made many Provençal coblas and c
2 CONC anzoni of great worth.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 5 June 1219, Ramon married Beatrice of Savoy, daughter of Thomas I of Savoy. She was a shrewd and politically astute woman, whose beauty was likened by Matthew Paris to that of a second Niobe. Along with two stillborn sons (122
2 CONC 0 & 1225), Ramon and Beatrice had four daughters, all of whom married kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite of Provence (1221-1295), wife of Louis IX of France 
2 CONT Eleanor of Provence (1223-1291), wife of Henry III of England 
2 CONT Sanchia of Provence (1228-1261), wife of Richard, Earl of Cornwall 
2 CONT Beatrice of Provence (1234-1267), wife of Charles I of Sicily 
2 CONT Ramon Berenguer IV died in Aix-en-Provence. At least two planhs (Occitan funeral laments) of uncertain authorship (one possibly by Aimeric de Peguilhan and one falsely attributed to Rigaut de Berbezilh) were written in his honour.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT Howell, Margaret. Eleanor of Provence: Queenship in Thirteenth-Century England, 2001 
2 CONT FMG on Raymond Berenger de Provence, the fourth Count of Provence 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT ^ Giovanni Villani, Rose E. Selfe, ed. (1906), "?90-Incident relating to the good Count Raymond of Provence.", Villani's Chronicle, Being Selections from the First Nine Books of the Croniche Fiorentine of Giovanni Villani (London:
2 CONC  Archibald Constable & Co.), 196. The Provençal coblas and cansos referred to do not survive and Ramon Berenguer is not listed among the troubadours, though he was their patron. 
1 NOTE O GeneAll e a Wiki em espanhol se referem a ele como Ramon Berenger V. A Wiki em inglês cita Ramon Berenger IV como pai de Margarita de Provença, embora datas e outras informações estejam em acordo com s demais fontes.
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-887
1 NOTE Il se prononce contre les Albigeois et participe, avec le Roi de France Louis VIII áa la prise d'Avignon en 1226.
2 CONT Il marie ses filles avec :
2 CONT - le roi de France Louis IX (Marguerite)
2 CONT - le roi d'Angleterre Henri III (Aliâenor)
2 CONT - Richard de Cornouailles, Roi des Romains
2 CONT - Charles d'Anjou, fráere de Louis XI (Bâeatrice)
