1 NOTE Vainqueur de Jean Sans Terre áa la Roche-aux-Moines, práes d'Angers en 1214, il s'oppose áa lui lors de la râevolte des barons anglais. Il dâebarque en Angleterre en 1216 mais il est battu en 1217.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il enláeve aux Anglais le Poitou et l'Aunis en 1224.
2 CONT Au cours de la croisade contre les Albigeois, il s'empare d'Avignon en 1226.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il constitue pour ses fils des apanages :
2 CONT - l'Artois pour Robert
2 CONT - l'Anjou et le Maine pour Charles
2 CONT - le Poitou et l'Auvergne pour Alphonse.
2 CONT (note râedigâee pour le logiciel Heredis)
1 NOTE Coronation_of_Louis_VIII_and_Blanche_of_Castile_1223
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=ba3d1d9d-58a8-495e-b1d4-e3914d8cf889&tid=8627488&pid=-914595161
1 NOTE Alias:<ALIA> The /Lion/
2 CONT REFN: P4192
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis VIII the Lion (French: Louis VIII le Lion) (September 5, 1187 – November 8, 1226) reigned as King of France from 1223 to 1226. He was a member of the Capetian dynasty. Louis VIII was born in Paris, France, the son of Philip 
2 CONC II of France and Isabelle of Hainaut.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1216 the English barons rebelled in the First Barons' War against the unpopular King John of England (1199–1216) and offered the throne to Louis. Louis invaded and was proclaimed King in London inMay 1216, although he was not c
2 CONC rowned. There was little resistance when the prince entered London. At St Paul's Cathedral, Louis was accepted as ruler with great pomp and celebration in the presence of all of London. Many nobles, including Alexander II of Scotl
2 CONC and (1214–49), gathered to give homage to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 14 he captured Winchester and soon conquered over half of the English kingdom. Since other English Kings such as Edward V (1483) and Edward VIII (1936) were not crowned but only proclaimed, there is a good case for includi
2 CONC ng Louis VIII in the list of Kings of England. After a year and a half of war, most of the rebellious barons had defected and so Louis had to give up his claim to be theKing of England by signing the Treaty of Lambeth in 1217.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis VIII succeeded his father on July 14, 1223; his coronation took place on August 6 of the same year in the cathedral at Reims. As King, he continued to seek revenge on the Angevins and seized Poitou and Saintonge from them in
2 CONC  1224. There followed the seizure of Avignon and Languedoc.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 November 1223, he issued an ordinance that prohibited his officials from recording debts owed to Jews, thus reversing the policies set by his father Philip II of France (1180–1223). Usury (lending money with interest) was ill
2 CONC egal for Christians to practice, according to Church law it was seen as a vice in which people profited from others' misfortune (like gambling), and was punishable by excommunication, a severe punishment. However since Jews were n
2 CONC ot Christian, they could not be excommunicated, and thus fell in to a legal gray area which secular rulers would sometimes exploit by allowing (or requesting) Jews to provide usury services, often for personal gain to the secular 
2 CONC ruler, and to the discontent of the Church. Louis VIII's prohibition was one attempt at resolving this legal problem which was a constant source of friction in Church and State courts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Twenty six barons accepted, but Theobald IV of Champagne (1201–53), a powerful baron from Champagne did not, since he had an agreement with the Jews that guaranteed him extra income through taxation.Theobald IV would become a majo
2 CONC r opposition force to Capetian dominance, and his hostility was manifest during the reign of Louis VIII. For example, during the siege of Avignon, he performed only theminimum service of 40 days, and left home amid charges of trea
2 CONC chery.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1225, the council of Bourges excommunicated the count of Toulouse, Raymond VII, and declared a crusade against the southern barons. Louis happily renewed the conflict in order to enforce his royalrights. Roger Bernard the Great
2 CONC , count of Foix, tried to keep the peace, but the king rejected his embassy and the counts of Foix and Toulouse took up arms against him. The king was largely successful, but he did not complete the work before his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT While returning to Paris, King Louis VIII became ill with dysentery, and died on November 8, 1226 in the chateau at Montpensier, Auvergne.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Saint Denis Basilica houses the tomb of Louis VIII. His son, Louis IX (1226–70), succeeded him on the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 23, 1200, at the age of twelve, Louis married Blanche of Castile (March 4, 1188 – November 26, 1252).
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT Philippe (September 9, 1209 – 1218)
2 CONT Louis IX (April 25, 1214 – August 25, 1270)
2 CONT Robert (September 25, 1216 – February 9, 1250)
2 CONT Jean (July 21, 1219 – 1232)
2 CONT Alphonse of Toulouse (November 11, 1220 – August 21, 1271)
2 CONT Philippe Dagobert (February 20, 1222 – 1232)
2 CONT Isabel (June 1225 – February 23, 1269)
2 CONT Etienne (born and died 1226)
2 CONT Charles I of Sicily (March 1227 – January 7, 1285)
2 CONT
2 CONT This is a grandson of Eleanor of Aquitaine, Past Queen consort of England, Ruler of Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married his cousin.    Blanche from Spain and England,    and Louis from France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis is the grandson of Eleanor of Aquitaine and  Philip Augustus Capet,
2 CONT
2 CONT and his wife Blanche is a granddaughter of Eleanore of Aquitaine and King Henry II of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also Count of Artois from 1190, inheriting the county from his mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 November 1223, he issued an ordinance that prohibited his officials from recording debts owed to Jews, thus reversing the policies set by his father Philip II Augustus. Usury (lending money withinterest) was illegal for Chris
2 CONC tians to practice. According to Church law it was seen as a vice in which people profited from others' misfortune (like gambling), and was punishable by excommunication,a severe punishment. However since Jews were not Christian, t
2 CONC hey could not be excommunicated, and thus fell in to a legal gray area which secular rulers would sometimes exploit by allowing (or requesting) Jews to provide usury services, often for personal gain to the secular ruler, and to t
2 CONC he discontent of the Church. Louis VIII's prohibition was one attempt at resolving this legal problem whichwas a constant source of friction in Church and State courts.
