1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Blanca de Castilla
2 CONT
2 CONT De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre
2 CONT
2 CONT http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanca_de_Castilla
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca de Castilla, también conocida como Blanca de Borgoña (Palencia, 4 de marzo de 1188 – Melun, 1252), fue infanta de Castilla y reina consorte de Francia. Novena de los doce hijos de Alfonso VIII el Noble o el de Las Navas y L
2 CONC eonor de Plantagenet, era por lo tanto nieta de Enrique II Plantagenet, rey de Inglaterra, y de Leonor de Aquitania, sobrina por esta rama de Ricardo I, Corazón de León, y de Juan sin Tierra, ambos reyes de Inglaterra, y tía de Fe
2 CONC rnando III el Santo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Felipe II Augusto de Francia y Juan I de Inglaterra acordaron que el heredero al trono de Francia, el futuro Luis VIII, se casara con una infanta de Castilla. Fue la mismísima y anciana Leonor de Aquitania quien en 1200 se desplaz
2 CONC ó a Castilla, donde reinaba su hija Leonor, para conocer a sus nietas y decidir cuál sería la futura «reina de la Flor de Lis». En un inicio, la intención era llevar como prometida del príncipe Luis a la entonces hija mayor solter
2 CONC a de los reyes de Castilla, Urraca, pero al final Leonor escoge a su segunda nieta Blanca, ya que a su parecer ella encajaría mejor en la corte de Francia. Con apenas 12 años la infanta se despide de sus padres y hermanos, saliend
2 CONC o con su abuela hacia Francia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Al morir Felipe, el 14 de julio de 1223, ocupó el trono el marido de Blanca con el nombre de Luis VIII. A los tres años, Luis murió de disentería a la vuelta de una campaña militar por la que pretendía recuperar el Mediodía para F
2 CONC rancia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca fue una eficaz consejera de su marido. Madre de nueve hijos, el segundo de ellos, Luis, futuro Luis IX, San Luis IX, fue proclamado rey a la muerte de su padre, contando solamente 12 años de edad, en 1226. Blanca fue nombra
2 CONC da regente, enfrentándose a los problemas acuciantes de su tiempo, como las pugnas con Inglaterra, el conflicto con los cátaros y las presiones de la nobleza.
2 CONT
2 CONT Una vez que su hijo mostró capacidad para llevar los asuntos del Estado, se retiró a la abadía de Maubuisson, donde murió en 1252.
2 CONT
2 CONT De su matrimonio tuvo, como dijimos, nueve hijos:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Felipe (1209 – 1218), príncipe heredero
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Luis (San Luis) (1214 – 1270), rey
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Roberto (1216 – 1250), conde de Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) Juan (1219 – 1232)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5) Alfonso (1220 – 1271), conde de Poitiers y de Toulouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6) Felipe Dagoberto (1222 – 1232)
2 CONT
2 CONT 7) Isabel (1225 – 1269)
2 CONT
2 CONT 8) Etienne (nacida y muerta en 1226)
2 CONT
2 CONT 9) Carlos (póstumo) (1227 – 1285), conde de Anjou y Maine y Rey de Sicilia y de Nápoles.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Castile, Queen consort of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father Alfonso VIII of Castile 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother Eleanor of England 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born March 4, 1188(1188-03-04)
2 CONT
2 CONT Palencia, Castile 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died November 26, 1252 (aged 64) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Paris, France 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Castile, Blanca de Castilla in Spanish, (March 4, 1188 – November 26, 1252), wife of Louis VIII of France. She was born in Palencia, Spain, the third daughter of Alfonso VIII, king of Castile, and of Eleanor of England.
2 CONC  Eleanor was a daughter of Henry II of England and his Queen consort Eleanor of Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT In consequence of a treaty between Philip Augustus and John of England, Blanche's sister Urraca was betrothed to the former's son, Louis. Their grandmother Eleanor, upon getting acquainted with the two sisters, judged that Blanche
2 CONC 's personality was more fit for a queen of France. In the spring of 1200 she brought her to France instead. On May 22, 1200 the treaty was finally signed, John ceding with his niece the fiefs of Issoudun and Gracay, together with 
2 CONC those that André de Chauvigny, lord of Châteauroux, held in Berry, of the English crown. The marriage was celebrated the next day, at Portmort on the right bank of the Seine, in John's domains, as those of Philip lay under an inte
2 CONC rdict.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche first displayed her great qualities in 1216, when Louis, who on the death of John claimed the English crown in her right, invaded England, only to find a united nation against him. Philip Augustus refused to help his son, 
2 CONC and Blanche was his sole support. The queen established herself at Calais and organized two fleets, one of which was commanded by Eustace the Monk, and an army under Robert of Courtenay; but all her resolution and energy were in v
2 CONC ain. Although it would seem that her masterful temper exercised a sensible influence upon her husband's gentler character, her role during his reign (1223-1226) is not well known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death he left Blanche regent and guardian of his children. Of her twelve or thirteen children, six had died, and Louis, the heir – afterwards the sainted Louis IX – was but twelve years old. The situation was critical, fo
2 CONC r the hard-won domains of the house of Capet seemed likely to fall to pieces during a minority. Blanche had to bear the whole burden of affairs alone, to break up a league of the barons (1226), and to repel the attack of the king 
2 CONC of England (1230). But her energy and firmness overcame all dangers. There was an end to the calumnies circulated against her, based on the poetical homage rendered her by Theobald IV of Champagne, and the prolonged stay in Paris 
2 CONC of the papal legate, Romano Bonaventura, cardinal of Sant' Angelo.
2 CONT
2 CONT The nobles were awed by her warlike preparations or won over by adroit diplomacy, and their league was broken up. St Louis owed his realm to his mother, but he himself always remained somewhat under the spell of her imperious pers
2 CONC onality. After he came of age, in 1234, her influence upon him may still be traced. The same year, he was married, and Blanche became Queen mother. Louis IX married Marguerite of Provence, who was the eldest of four daughters of R
2 CONC amon, count of Provence, and Beatrice of Savoy. In 1248 Blanche again became regent, during Louis IX's absence on the crusade, a project which she had strongly opposed. In the disasters which followed she maintained peace, while d
2 CONC raining the land of men and money to aid her son in the East. At last her strength failed her. She fell ill into a bale of hay at Melun in November 1252, and was taken to Paris, but lived only a few days. She was buried at Maubuis
2 CONC son.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Castile (March 4, 1188 – November 26, 1252), wife of Louis VIII of France. She was born in Palencia, Spain, the third daughter of Alfonso VIII, king of Castile, and of Eleanor of England. Eleanor was a daughter of Henry
2 CONC  II of England and his Queen consort Eleanor of Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT In consequence of a treaty between Philip Augustus and John of England, Blanche's sister Urraca was betrothed to the former's son, Louis. Their grandmother Eleanor, upon getting acquainted with the two sisters, judged that Blanche
2 CONC 's personality was more fit for a queen of France. In the spring of 1200 she brought her to France instead. On May 22, 1200 the treaty was finally signed, John ceding with his niece the fiefs of Issoudun and Gracay, together with 
2 CONC those that André de Chauvigny, lord of Châteauroux, held in Berry, of the English crown. The marriage was celebrated the next day, at Portmort on the right bank of the Seine, in John's domains, as those of Philip lay under an inte
2 CONC rdict.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche first displayed her great qualities in 1216, when Louis, who on the death of John claimed the English crown in her right, invaded England, only to find a united nation against him. Philip Augustus refused to help his son, 
2 CONC and Blanche was his sole support. The queen established herself at Calais and organized two fleets, one of which was commanded by Eustace the Monk, and an army under Robert of Courtenay; but all her resolution and energy were in v
2 CONC ain. Although it would seem that her masterful temper exercised a sensible influence upon her husband's gentler character, her role during his reign (1223-1226) is not well known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death he left Blanche regent and guardian of his children. Of her twelve or thirteen children, six had died, and Louis, the heir – afterwards the sainted Louis IX – was but twelve years old. The situation was critical, fo
2 CONC r the hard-won domains of the house of Capet seemed likely to fall to pieces during a minority. Blanche had to bear the whole burden of affairs alone, to break up a league of the barons (1226), and to repel the attack of the king 
2 CONC of England (1230). But her energy and firmness overcame all dangers. There was an end to the calumnies circulated against her, based on the poetical homage rendered her by Theobald IV of Champagne, and the prolonged stay in Paris 
2 CONC of the papal legate, Romano Bonaventura, cardinal of Sant' Angelo.
2 CONT
2 CONT The nobles were awed by her warlike preparations or won over by adroit diplomacy, and their league was broken up. St Louis owed his realm to his mother, but he himself always remained somewhat under the spell of her imperious pers
2 CONC onality. After he came of age, in 1234, her influence upon him may still be traced. The same year, he was married, and Blanche became Queen mother. In 1248 she again became regent, during Louis IX's absence on the crusade, a proje
2 CONC ct which she had strongly opposed. In the disasters which followed she maintained peace, while draining the land of men and money to aid her son in the East. At last her strength failed her. She fell ill into a bale of hay at Melu
2 CONC n in November 1252, and was taken to Paris, but lived only a few days. She was buried at Maubuisson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Literature
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides the works of Joinville and William of Nangis, see Élie Berger, "Histoire de Blanche de Castille, reine de France," in Bibliothèque des écoles francaises d’Athènes et de Rome, vol. lxx. (Paris, 1895); Le Nain de Tillemont, 
2 CONC "Vie de Saint Louis," ed. by J. de Gaulle for the Société de l'histoire de France (6 vols., 1847-1851); and Paulin Paris, "Nouvelles recherches sur les mœurs de la reine Blanche et de Thibaud," in Cabinet historique (1858).
2 CONT
2 CONT References
