1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In 1369… “Even though not all of this subjects were equally grateful to the Breton, Enrique II did not fail to reward Bertrand as he had promised. After Pedro’s head had been exposed in Seville, and Toledo taken, t
2 CONC he King made over to du Guesclin all the towns with which his rival had tried to entice him to change sides.  The donation was formally made in Seville on 4 May, whereby the Breton warlord became lord of Soria, Almazan, Atienza, M
2 CONC onteagudo, Deza, etc. as hereditary fiefs with all rights, revenues and prerogatives appertaining to them. Moreover, the list was headed by the donation of the dukedom of Molina, as fair compensation for Trastamara, lost after the
2 CONC  defeat of Najera.  Ayala mistakenly dates this grant from Enrique’s first entry into Burgos in 1366.  However, the formal act of donation is dated 4 May 1407, Spanish style (A.D. 1369) The similarity of its terms to the alleged o
2 CONC ffered made by Pedro at Montiel may well suggest a quid pro quo.  Du Guesclin’s companions were not forgotten in the second round of mercedes enriqueñas: the Bègue de Villaines was made Count of Ribadeo, Arnaud de Solier Lord of V
2 CONC illalpando, and Bernard de Béarn, married to the heiress of the royal blood of Castile, Count of Medinaceli.  They settled in their new estates, but once again Bertrand had little leisure to enjoy his new seigneuries.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Vernier, Richard.  The Flower of Chivalry.  Woodbridge: The Boydell Press, 2003.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mosen Arnaud de Solier was the son of Bertrand’s sister Mathilde du Guesclin.   Mosen Arnaud married Luisa de Béarn de la Cerda, daughter of Bernard de Béarn Count of Medinaceli and war companion of Mosen Arnaud and Bertrand du Gu
2 CONC esclin.
