2 CONT
2 CONT Marie of Champagne (c. 1174 - 9 August 1204) was the Empress consort of Baldwin I of Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Marriage 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Empress consort 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Sources 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 References 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 External links 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a daughter of Henry I, Count of Champagne and Marie of France, Countess of Champagne. Her maternal grandparents were Louis VII of France and Eleanor of Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brothers were Henry II of Champagne and Theobald III, Count of Champagne. Her sister Scholastique of Champagne married William V of Macon. Both sisters are mentioned by name in the chronicle of Alberic of Trois-Fontaines.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the chronicle of Gislebert of Mons, Marie was bethrothed to "Theobald", son of the count of Flanders and Hainaut in 1179. Gislebert is presumed to have misrecorded the name of Baldwin. Her betrothed was a son of Baldw
2 CONC in V, Count of Hainaut and Margaret I, Countess of Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 6 January, 1186, Marie and Baldwin were married. They had two known children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne, Countess of Flanders (1199/1200 - 5 December 1244). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret II, Countess of Flanders (2 June 1202 - 10 February 1280). 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Empress consort
2 CONT
2 CONT On 14 April 1202 her husband left Flanders to join the Fourth Crusade. This Crusade was diverted to Constantinople, capital of the Byzantine Empire. The crusaders captured and sacked the city. Then they decided to set up a Latin E
2 CONC mpire in place of the fallen Greek one. On 9 May, 1204, Baldwin was elected its first emperor. Making Marie an Empress consort.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie herself left Flanders to join her husband but decided to visit Outremer first. According to Geoffrey of Villehardouin she could not join him in the crusade before because of being pregnant. Having given birth to Margaret and
2 CONC  sufficiently recovered, she set forth to join him. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT She set sail from the port of Marseille and landed in Acre. According to A History of the Crusades: Volume 3, The Kingdom of Acre and the Later Crusades (1954) by Steven Runciman, she there received tribute by Bohemond IV of Antio
2 CONC ch. In Acre news reached her of the fall of Constantinople and the proclamation of Baldwin as the new emperor. She wanted to set sail for Constantinople but fell sick and died in the Holy Land. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT News of her death reached Constantinople through Crusading reinforcements from Syria. Baldwin was reportedly afflicted by the death of his wife. Villehardouin reports that Marie "was a gracious and virtuous lady and greatly honour
2 CONC ed". [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal titles 
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Hungary Latin Empress consort of Constantinople
2 CONT
2 CONT 1204 Succeeded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes of Montferrat 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel de Warenne, 4th Countess of Surrey (died 12 July 1203) was an English peeress. She was the only surviving heir of William de Warenne, 3rd Earl of Surrey and his wife, Adela, the daughter of William III of Ponthieu.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1148, de Warenne inherited her father's lands and the earldom of Surrey and was married to William of Blois, the younger son of King Stephen, that year. The marriage occurred at a critical moment in The Anarchy as part of the k
2 CONC ing's attempt to control the de Warenne lands. The couple did not have any children and after William's death in 1159, William X, Count of Poitou sought her hand in 1162/3, but Thomas Becket refused a dispensation from affinity on
2 CONC  the grounds of consanguinity.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1164, the countess married Hamelin Plantagenet, the half-brother of King Henry II, who became jure uxoris Earl of Surrey. They had four surviving children:
2 CONT
2 CONT William, later 6th Earl of Surrey (1166-1240) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adela (born c. 1170), married Robert of Naburn and William FitzWilliam and was also a mistress of King John. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel (died 30 November 1234), married Robert de Lacy and Gilbert de l'Aigle, Lord of Pevensey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilda, married Henry, Count of Eu and Henry de Stuteville. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamelin died in 1202 and the countess a year later. She was buried alongside him in the chapter house of Lewes Priory.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Source
2 CONT
2 CONT Johns, Susan M. - Warenne, Isabel de, suo jure countess of Surrey (d. 1203), magnate - Oxford Dictionary of National Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabel_de_Warenne,_4th_Countess_of_Surrey"
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
