2 CONT Henry I of Champagne (died March 17, 1181 ), known as "the Liberal", was count of Champagne from 1152 to 1181. He was the eldest son of Count Thibaut II of Champagne  (who was also Count Thibaut IV of Blois).
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry took part in the Second Crusade  under the leadership of Louis VII of France . He carried a letter of recommendation from Bernard of Clairvaux  addressed to Alexius I Comnenus , Byzantine Emperor ; he is listed among the not
2 CONC ables present at the assembly  held by Baldwin III of Jerusalem  at Acre  on 24 June  1148 .
2 CONT
2 CONT On his father's death, Henry chose to take Champagne, leaving the family's older holdings (including Blois , Chartres , Sancerre , and Chateaudun ) to his younger brothers. At the time this may have been surprising, for the other 
2 CONC territories were richer and better developed. Henry must have foreseen the economic possibilities of Champagne, and it is during his rule that the county achieved its high place as one of the richest and strongest of the French  p
2 CONC rincipalities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry established orderly rule over the nobles of Champagne, and could fairly reliably count on the aid of some 2,000 vassals, which just by itself made him a power few in France could equal. This order in turn made Champagne a sa
2 CONC fe place for merchants to gather, and under the count's protection the Champagne Fairs  became a central part of long-distance trade and finance in medieval  Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition, the count's court in Troyes  became a renowned literary center. Walter Map  was among those who found hospitality there. The scholar Stephen of Alinerre  was among Henry's courtiers, becoming chancellor  of the county
2 CONC  in 1176.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1179 Henry went to Jerusalem  again with a party of French knights including his relatives Peter of Courtenay  (brother of Louis VII) and Philip of Dreux , bishop of Beauvais . Henry returned towards Europe by the land route ac
2 CONC ross Asia Minor, and was captured and held to ransom by Kilij Arslan II , Seljuk  of Rüm . The ransom was paid by the Byzantine Emperor and Henry was released, but died soon afterwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1164, Henry married Marie of France, Countess of Champagne </wiki/Marie_of_France,_Countess_of_Champagne>, daughter of Louis VII of France  and Eleanor of Aquitaine .
2 CONT
2 CONT They had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Scholastique of Champagne (died 1219), married William IV of Macon 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry II (1166–1197) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie of Champagne  (died 1204), married Baldwin I of Constantinople  
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald  (1179–1201) 
