2 CONT '''2.         MARIE de Champagne ([1174]-Jerusalem 9 Aug 1204).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names the two daughters of "comitissa Maria Campaniensis" as "Colatiam uxorem comitis Guilelmi Matisconensis et Mariam ux
2 CONC orem comitis Balduini Flandrensis"[32].  The Chronicon Hanoniense records the betrothal in 1179 of "filia comitis Henrici Maria" and "filium [comitis Flandrie] Theobaldum", the latter presumably being an error for "Balduinum"[33].
2 CONC   William of Tyre (Continuator) specifies that the sister of Henri II Comte de Champagne was married to comte Baudouin, later emperor of the Latin Empire of Constantinople[34].  The Flandria Generosa names "Maria sorore Theobaldi 
2 CONC Campaniæ comitis" as wife of Count Baudouin[35].  She visited Palestine in 1204 en route to join her husband in Constantinople, received homage from Bohémond IV Prince of Antioch at Acre[36], but died soon after at Jerusalem.  Acc
2 CONC ording to Sturdza, she assumed that she was rejoining her husband in Palestine not knowing of the crusade's diversion to Constantinople and his election as emperor[37].  The Flandria Generosa specifies that she died at "Acharon"[3
2 CONC 8].  m (Betrothed 1179, 6 Jan 1186) BAUDOUIN de Flandre, son of BAUDOUIN V Comte de Hainaut [BAUDOUIN VIII Count of Flanders] & his wife Marguerite Ctss of Flanders (Jul 1171-in prison in Bulgaria 11 Jun 1205).  He succeeded his m
2 CONC other in 1194 as BAUDOUIN IX Count of Flanders, and his father in 1195 as BAUDOUIN VI Comte de Hainaut.  He was crowned as BAUDOUIN I Emperor of Constantinople 16 May 1204.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CHAMPAGNE%20NOBILITY.htm#HenriIChampagnedied1181B
2 CONT ----
2 CONT BAUDOUIN de Hainaut, son of BAUDOUIN V Comte de Hainaut [BAUDOUIN VIII Count of Flanders] & his wife Marguerite I Ctss of Flanders (Jul 1171-in prison in Bulgaria 11 Jun 1205).  The Chronicon Hanoniense records the birth "1171 men
2 CONC se Iulio…Valencenis" of "filium…Balduinum" to "Balduinus [et] Margharetam…Mathie comitis Boloniensis sororem"[554].  The Flandria Generosa names (in order) "Balduinum, Philippum et Henricum" as the three sons of Count Baudouin and
2 CONC  his wife Marguerite, specifying that Baudouin was later emperor of Constantinople[555].  He succeeded his mother in 1194 as BAUDOUIN IX Count of Flanders, and his father in 1195 as BAUDOUIN VI Comte de Hainaut.  Under the Treaty 
2 CONC of Dinant 26 Jul 1199, he acquired Namur.  He did homage to Philippe II King of France for Flanders and Hainaut, but then allied himself with Richard I King of England in Sep 1197.  War broke out with France, and by end 1198 Count
2 CONC  Baudouin had overrun northern Artois[556].  He was obliged to agree the Treaty of Péronne with France in Jan 1200 in order to secure the release of his brother Philippe de Namur from French custody, agreeing to give up his allian
2 CONC ce with England and receiving Saint-Omer, Aire and Guines in return[557].  He was among the first leaders to take the cross following the call of Pope Innocent III.  A Flemish fleet arrived at Acre end 1202 under the command of Je
2 CONC an de Nesle, châtelain de Bruges[558].  After the army of the Fourth Crusade took control of Constantinople 13 Apr 1204, a council of six Venetians and six Franks met to elect a new Latin emperor, as agreed in the Acti Partitio Im
2 CONC perii Romanae the previous March between the crusaders and Venice.  The votes of the Venetian block of electors ensured the success of Count Baudouin over the rival candidate, Bonifazio Marchese di Monferrato, Enrico Dandolo Doge 
2 CONC of Venice considering Baudouin as the less powerful candidate[559].  At the same time, in accordance with the terms of the March treaty, Tomaso Morosini (from Venice) was installed as first Latin patriarch of Constantinople, his f
2 CONC irst task being to crown Baudouin as BAUDOUIN I Emperor of Constantinople[560] at St Sophia 16 May 1204.  The constitution which was adopted gave little power to the emperor whose decisions were subject to review by a council of t
2 CONC enants-in-chief which also directed military operations[561].  “Balduinus...imperator...Romanie...Flandrie et Hainonie comes” notified “avunculo suo Gerardo Brugensium preposito ac Flandrie cancellario et...Willelmo castellano San
2 CONC cti Audomari et Gilleberto Insulensis, baillivis suis de Flandrie” that he had granted land to “Waltero clerico...de Curtraco” at the request of “Robertus de Sperleka” by charter dated Feb 1204 [O.S.] “in palacio nostro Blakerne”,
2 CONC  witnessed by “homines mei de Flandria:...Gislebertus de Ipra, Robelote de Waverin...homines nostri de Romania...Cono de Betunia fidelis et consanguineus noster, Gulfridus marescallus Campanie, Milo de Brebant, Manesulus de Insula
2 CONC ”[562].  The new patriarch declared the union of the Catholic and Orthodox churches, but the Greek aristocracy in Thrace rebelled.  Kalojan Tsar of Bulgaria intervened, defeated Baudouin near Adrianople 14 Apr 1205, and captured a
2 CONC nd transported him as a prisoner to Bulgaria where he died in prison soon after[563].  When news of Count Baudouin's death reached Flanders in Feb 1206, Philippe II King of France assumed his right as feudal overlord to the wardsh
2 CONC ip of his two daughters[564]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m (Betrothed 1179, 6 Jan 1186) MARIE de Champagne, daughter of HENRI I “le Libéral” Comte de Champagne & his wife Marie de France ([1174]-Jerusalem 9 Aug 1204).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names the two daughte
2 CONC rs of "comitissa Maria Campaniensis" as "Colatiam uxorem comitis Guilelmi Matisconensis et Mariam uxorem comitis Balduini Flandrensis"[565].  The Chronicon Hanoniense records the betrothal in 1179 of "filia comitis Henrici Maria" 
2 CONC and "filium [comitis Flandrie] Theobaldum", the latter presumably being an error for "Balduinum"[566].  William of Tyre (Continuator) specifies that the sister of Henri II Comte de Champagne was married to comte Baudouin, later Em
2 CONC peror[567].  The Flandria Generosa names "Maria sorore Theobaldi Campaniæ comitis" wife of Count Baudouin[568].  She visited Palestine in 1204 en route to join her husband in Constantinople, received homage from Bohémond IV Prince
2 CONC  of Antioch at Acre[569], but died soon after at Jerusalem.  The Flandria Generosa specifies that she died at "Acharon"[570].''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Baudouin IX of Flanders & his wife had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         JEANNE de Flandre (Valenciennes 1200-Marquette near Lille 5 Dec 1244, bur Marquette).  The Genealogica Comitum Flandriæ Bertiniana names (in order) "Iohannam et Margaretam" as the two daughters of "Balduinus"[571].  She
2 CONC  succeeded her father as JEANNE Ctss of Flanders and Ctss de Hainaut in Feb 1206 when news of his death reached Flanders, under the regency of her uncle Philippe Marquis de Namur.  The latter agreed to the demand of Philippe II Ki
2 CONC ng of France to send the countess and her sister to Paris to be educated[572].  King Philippe arranged her first marriage.  The De Rebus Hispaniæ of Rodericus Ximenes records the marriage of "Ferdinandum", other son of "Rex…Sanciu
2 CONC s", and "Flandriæ Comitissam"[573].  While returning to Flanders after her marriage, she and her husband were captured by Louis, son of King Philippe II, who occupied Aire and Saint-Omer, the occupation being ratified by the Treat
2 CONC y of Pont-à-Vedin 25 Feb 1212 as the price for their release[574].  After her husband's capture in 1214, Philippe II King of France forced on her the Treaty of Paris 24 Oct 1214, under which major fortresses in southern Flanders w
2 CONC ere destroyed, property restored to French partisans, and Flanders in effect ruled from Paris[575].  King Philippe refused to negotiate her husband's release unless she agreed to the annulment of her marriage and remarriage to Pie
2 CONC rre "Mauclerc" Duke of Brittany.  Civil war followed the appearance in 1224 of a hermit who claimed to be Jeanne's father returned from captivity and subsided only after his execution following a confrontation with Louis VIII King
2 CONC  of France 30 May 1225[576].  She negotiated the Treaty of Melun in 1226 under which her husband was returned on payment of 50,000 livres ransom[577].  The Genealogica Comitum Flandriæ Bertiniana names "Thome fratri comitis Sabaud
2 CONC ie" as husband of "Iohanna", whom she married after the death of "Ferrandus"[578].  The Annales Blandinienses record the marriage in 1237 of "Iohannam comitissam Flandrie" with "Thomas avunculus reginarum Francie et Anglie"[579]. 
2 CONC  The Annales Blandinienses record the death in 1244 of "Iohanna comitissa" and her burial at "Market"[580].  The Necrologio Sanctæ Waldetrudis records the death "Non Dec" of "Iohanne comitisse Flandrie et Hanoie"[581].  m firstly 
2 CONC ([Paris] 1 Jan 1212) Infante dom FERNANDO de Portugal, son of SANCHO I "o Poblador" King of Portugal & his wife Infanta doña Dulcia de Aragón (24 Mar 1188-Noyon 4 Mar or 26 Jul 1233, bur Marquette near Lille).  The Genealogica Com
2 CONC itum Flandriæ Bertiniana names "fratrem regis de Portigal, nomine Fernandum" husband of "Iohanna"[582].  He succeeded as FERRAND Count of Flanders and Hainaut in 1212, by right of his wife.  Although the protégé of Philippe II Kin
2 CONC g of France, he exiled several prominent Francophiles after arriving in Flanders and opened negotiations with England.  He refused to participate in King Philippe's projected invasion of England in 1213.  The French army devastate
2 CONC d Flanders in revenge, forcing Count Ferrand briefly to seek refuge in Zeeland.  He was captured at the battle of Bouvines 27 Jul 1214, and taken to Paris where he remained a prisoner[583].  He returned to Flanders in 1227 after p
2 CONC ayment of the ransom under the Treaty of Melun[584].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records that on the death in 1229 of "comite Namucensi Henrici puero" his sister "Sibilia comitissa Vienne" occupied "castrum Namuci
2 CONC " against the competing claim of Fernando Count of Flanders[585].  He founded the convent of Marquette near Lille.  The Continuatio Clarimariscensis records the death "1233 VI Kal Aug" of "Fernandus Flandriæ comes"[586].  The Anna
2 CONC les Blandinienses record the death in 1233 of "Ferrandus comes Flandrie et Haynonie" and his burial at "Merketo"[587].  The Chronica Andrensis records the death in 1233 "apud Noviomum" of "comes Flandrie Fernandus" and his burial 
2 CONC "iuxta Insulam"[588].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the death in 1233 of "Fernandus comes Flandrie" and his burial "in abbatia delle Marckete"[589].  m secondly (2 Apr 1237, without Papal dispensation despit
2 CONC e consanguinity within the prohibited degrees[590]) as his first wife, THOMAS de Savoie, son of THOMAS I Comte de Savoie & his wife Béatrix [Marguerite] de Faucigny (Château de Montmélian 1199-Chambéry 7 Feb 1259, bur Aosta Cathed
2 CONC ral).  The Genealogica Comitum Flandriæ Bertiniana names "Thome fratri comitis Sabaudie" as husband of "Iohanna", whom she married after the death of "Ferrandus"[591].  The Annales Blandinienses record the marriage in 1237 of "Ioh
2 CONC annam comitissam Flandrie" with "Thomas avunculus reginarum Francie et Anglie"[592].  He succeeded as THOMAS Count of Flanders and Hainaut in 1237, by right of his wife.  He returned to Savoy after his wife's death[593].  He was g
2 CONC iven the title Conte di Piemonte in 1247, and succeeded his brother in 1253 as THOMAS II Comte de Savoie, ruling jointly with his nephew.  Jeanne & her first husband had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         MARGUERITE de Flandre (2 Jun 1202-10 Feb 1280).  The Genealogica Comitum Flandriæ Bertiniana names (in order) "Iohannam et Margaretam" as the two daughters of "Balduinus"[598].  The Chronica Monasterii Sancti Bertini re
2 CONC cords that "secunda filia Margareta" was born after her parents left on their travels[599].  On the other hand, according to Villehardouin Comtesse Marie stayed behind when her husband left on Crusade, gave birth, and afterwards l
2 CONC eft for Acre where she died[600].  After her father's death, she was sent to Paris with her sister on the orders of Philippe II King of France[601].  Matthew of Paris names Bouchard as first husband of Marguerite in his descriptio
2 CONC n of the background to the war in Flanders in 1254[602].  Her first marriage was arranged by King Philippe II, her husband being a noble from Hainaut whose family had long supported French interests.  Her first husband demanded a 
2 CONC share of his late father-in-law's inheritance and, after complaining to Pope Innocent III, the marriage was annulled by the Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 on the grounds that Bouchard d'Avesnes had previously taken holy orders.  T
2 CONC he couple remained together until Bouchard was captured by his sister-in-law Ctss Jeanne in 1219.  He was released two years later on condition he separate from his wife[603].  The Iohannis de Thilrode Chronicon records the marria
2 CONC ge of "Marghareta" and "Willelmo de Dampetra"[604].  Matthew of Paris names Guillaume as second husband of Marguerite in his description of the background to the war in Flanders in 1254[605].  The Annales Blandinienses record the 
2 CONC succession in 1244 of "Margareta soror eius [=Iohanna comitissa}"[606].  She succeeded her sister in 1244 as MARGUERITE II Ctss of Flanders and Ctss de Hainaut, both her husbands having died.  Her children by her first marriage cl
2 CONC aimed their inheritance, but Louis IX King of France ruled in 1246 that Hainaut should be given to the Avesnes children and Flanders to the Dampierre children[607].  She abdicated 29 Dec 1278 in favour of her son Guy de Dampierre.
2 CONC   The Necrologio Sanctæ Waldetrudis records the death "IV Id Feb" of "Margarete Flandrie et Hanonie…comitisse"[608].  m firstly (before 23 Jul 1212, annulled 1215, separated [1221]) BOUCHARD d'Avesnes, son of JACQUES Seigneur d'Av
2 CONC esnes, de Leuze et de Condé & his wife Adeline de Guise ([1180]-1244, bur Clairefontaine).  Matthew of Paris names Bouchard as first husband of Marguerite in his description of the background to the war in Flanders in 1254[609].  
2 CONC m secondly ([18 Aug/15 Nov] 1223) GUILLAUME [II] Seigneur de Dampierre, son of GUY [II] Seigneur de Dampierre, Seigneur de Bourbon & his wife Mathilde de Bourbon, dame de Bourbon (after 1196-3 Sep 1231). 
2 CONT
