1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Infanta doña BERENGUELA de Navarra, Queen of England ([1163/5]-[1230], bur Abbaye de l’Espan (Piété Dieu), Mans, transferred 1821 to Le Mans Cathedral)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT d/o Infante don SANCHO de Navarra & Infanta doña SANCHA de Castilla 
2 CONT
2 CONT x (Cyprus 12 May 1191) RICHARD I King of England (1157-1199)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Infante don SANCHO de Navarra, son of GARCÍA IV King of Navarre & his first wife Marguerite de Laigle (1132-Pamplona 27 Jun 1194, bur Pamplona, Cathedral Santa María).  The "Corónicas" Navarras name "el rey don Sancho de Navarra" 
2 CONC as the son of "al rey don García de Navarra, que dixieron Garçía Remíriz" and his wife "la reyna dona Margerina"[587].  He succeeded his father in 1150 as SANCHO VI "el Sabio" King of Navarre.  The "Corónicas" Navarras record the 
2 CONC death "V Kal Iul" in 1194 of "Santius…rex Navarre"[588].  The Annales Compostellani record the death in 1194 of “Sancius Rex Navarræ”[589]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m (Carrión de los Condes 20 Jul 1153) Infanta doña SANCHA de Castilla, daughter of ALFONSO VII King of Castile & his first wife Berenguela de Barcelona ([1139]-5 Aug 1177, bur Pamplona, Cathedral Santa María).  The Anales Toledano
2 CONC s record the death in Aug 1177 of “la Reyna de Navarra, filla del Emperador”[590].  The "Corónicas" Navarras record the death "Non Aug" in 1179 of "Sancha…regina Navarre"[591]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Sancho VI & his wife had five children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >1.         '''Infanta doña BERENGUELA de Navarra ([1163/5]-[1230], bur Abbaye de l’Espan (Piété Dieu), Mans, transferred 1821 to Le Mans Cathedral).  The Nobiliario of Pedro Conde de Barcelos names "D. Berenguela Reina d´Ingalter
2 CONC ra, D. Blanca, D. Constança que murio en Arouca" as the daughters of "Sancho Rey de Navarra"[592].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Berengariam" wife of "rex Anglorum Richardus" as sister of "Blancham sororis re
2 CONC gis Navarreorum"[593].  Matthew of Paris names her and calls her daughter of the king of Navarre, when he records her marriage in Cyprus[594].  The Continuator of Florence of Worcester records the marriage "IV Id Mai" in Cyprus in
2 CONC  [1191] of King Richard and "Berengariam filiam regis Navarræ"[595].  This marriage was arranged by King Richard's mother Eléonore d'Aquitaine[596].  She arrived with Queen Eléonore at Naples in early 1191 and stayed with her futu
2 CONC re husband's sister Joanna dowager Queen of Sicily. She and Queen Joanna sailed with King Richard's fleet to Palestine and landed at Limassol, Cyprus in Apr 1191[597].  She was crowned Queen of England by the Bishop of Evreux imme
2 CONC diately after her marriage[598].  She sailed from Acre for France 29 Sep 1191 with her sister-in-law Queen Joanna[599].  The Necrology of Le Mans Cathedral records the death "10 Kal Jan 1230" of "Berengaria regina quondam uxor Ric
2 CONC hardi…regis Anglorum"[600].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the death "X Kal Jan 1230" of "regina Berengaria domna Cenomanensis relicta…regis Anglie Richardi"[601].  The Annals of Waverley record that “Berenga
2 CONC ria quondam regina Angliæ” founded “quandam abbatiam de ordine Cisterciensi in pago Cenomannensi…Pietatem Dei” adding that she was buried there[602].  m (Chapel of St George, Limassol, Cyprus 12 May 1191) RICHARD I King of England
2 CONC , son of HENRY II King of England & his wife Eléonore Dss d'Aquitaine (Beaumont Palace, Oxford 8 Sep 1157-Chalus 6 Apr 1199, bur Fontevrault Abbey).''' 
2 CONT >2.         Infante don SANCHO de Navarra (after 1170-Tudela 7 Apr 1234, bur Roncevalle).  
2 CONT >3.         Infanta doña BLANCA de Navarra (-12/14 Mar 1229).  
2 CONT >4.         Infanta doña CONSTANZA de Navarra.  
2 CONT >5.         Infante don FERNANDO de Navarra (-Tudela 16 Dec 1207).  
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NAVARRE.htm#Berengueladied1230
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Berengaria of Navarre''' (Spanish: '''Berenguela''', French: '''Bérengère'''; c. 1165-1170 – 23 December 1230) was Queen of the English as the wife of King Richard I of England. She was the eldest daughter of King Sancho VI of 
2 CONC Navarre and Sancha of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Marriage=
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria married Richard I of England on 12 May 1191 and was crowned the same day by the Archbishop of Bordeaux and Bishops of Evreux and Bayonne. As is the case with many of the medieval queens consort of the Kingdom of England
2 CONC , relatively little is known of her life. It seems that she and Richard did in fact meet once, years before their marriage, and writers of the time liked to claim that there was an attraction between them at that time. Richard had
2 CONC  been betrothed many years earlier to Princess Alys, sister of King Philip II of France. Alys, however, may have been the mistress of Richard's own father, King Henry II, and some said the mother of Henry's illegitimate child; a m
2 CONC arriage between Richard and Alys would therefore be technically impossible for religious reasons of affinity. Richard terminated his betrothal to Alys in 1190 while at Messina.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had Berengaria brought to him by his mother, Eleanor of Aquitaine. Since Richard was already on the Third Crusade, having wasted no time in setting off after his coronation, the two women had a long and difficult journey to cat
2 CONC ch up with him. They arrived in Sicily during Lent (when the marriage could not take place) in 1191 and were joined by Richard's sister Joan, the widowed Queen of Sicily. En route to the Holy Land, the ship carrying Berengaria and
2 CONC  Joan went aground off the coast of Cyprus, and they were threatened by the island's ruler, Isaac Comnenus. Richard came to their rescue, captured the island, overthrew Comnenus, and married Berengaria in the Chapel of St. George 
2 CONC at Limassol.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Queen consort=
2 CONT
2 CONT Whether the marriage was ever even consummated is a matter for conjecture. In any case, Richard certainly took his new wife with him for the first part of the crusade. They returned separately, but Richard was captured and impriso
2 CONC ned. Berengaria remained in Europe, attempting to raise money for his ransom. After his release, Richard returned to England and was not joined by his wife. The marriage was childless, and Berengaria was thought to be barren.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Richard returned to England, he had to regain all the territory that had either been lost by his brother John or taken by King Philip of France. His focus was on his kingdom, not his queen. Richard was ordered by Pope Celesti
2 CONC ne III to reunite with Berengaria and to show fidelity to her in future. Richard obeyed and took Berengaria to church every week thereafter. When he died in 1199, she was greatly distressed, perhaps more so at being deliberately o
2 CONC verlooked as Queen of England and Cyprus. Some historians believe that Berengaria honestly loved her husband, while Richard's feelings for her were merely formal, as the marriage was a political rather than a romantic union.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Queen dowager=
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria never visited England during King Richard's lifetime; during the entirety of their marriage, Richard spent less than six months in England. There is evidence, however, that she may have done so in the years following hi
2 CONC s death. The traditional description of her as "the only English queen never to set foot in the country" would still be literally true, as she did not visit England during the time she was Richard's consort. She certainly sent env
2 CONC oys to England several times, mainly to inquire about the pension she was due as dowager queen and Richard's widow, which King John failed to pay. Although Queen Eleanor intervened and Pope Innocent III threatened him with an inte
2 CONC rdict if he did not pay Berengaria what was due, King John still owed her more than £4000 when he died. During the reign of his son Henry III of England, however, her payments were made as they were supposed to be.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria eventually settled in Le Mans, one of her dower properties. She was a benefactress of the abbey of L'Epau, entered the conventual life, and was buried in the abbey. A skeleton thought to be hers was discovered in 1960 d
2 CONC uring the restoration of the abbey.
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berengaria_of_Navarre
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://thefreelancehistorywriter.com/2013/09/21/berengaria-of-navarre-queen-of-england/
2 CONT * https://ancestors.familysearch.org/en/LCP6-RCG/berenguela,-infanta-de--navarra-1163-1230
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berengaria_of_Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria of Navarre (Basque: Berengela, Spanish: Berenguela, French: Bérengère; c. 1165–1170 – 23 December 1230) was Queen of England as the wife of Richard I of England. She was the eldest daughter of Sancho VI of Navarre and S
2 CONC ancha of Castile. As is the case with many of the medieval English queens, little is known of her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Traditionally known as "the only English queen never to set foot in the country", she may in fact have visited England after her husband's death, but did not do so before, nor did she see much of Richard during her marriage, which
2 CONC  was childless. She did (unusually for the wife of a crusader) accompany him on the start of the Third Crusade, but mostly lived in his French possessions, where she gave generously to the church, despite difficulties in collectin
2 CONC g the pension she was due from Richard's brother and successor John after she became a widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT Berengaria was born in Navarre c. 1165 as the eldest daughter of Sancho VI of Navarre and Sancha of Castile.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1185, Berengaria was given the fief of Monreal in Navarre by her father.[2] Eleanor of Aquitaine promoted the engagement of Berengaria to her son Richard the Lionheart. An alliance with Navarre meant protection for the southern
2 CONC  borders of Eleanor's Duchy of Aquitaine and helped create better relations with neighbouring Castile, whose queen was Richard's sister Eleanor. Also, Navarre had assimilated the troubadour culture of Aquitaine and Berengaria's re
2 CONC putation was unbesmirched.[3] It seems that Berengaria and Richard did in fact meet once, years before their marriage, and writers have claimed that there was an attraction between them at that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1190, Eleanor met King Sancho VI in Pamplona, and he hosted a banquet in the Royal Palace of Olite in her honour.[4] The betrothal could not be celebrated openly, for Richard had been betrothed for many years to Alys, half-sist
2 CONC er of King Philip II of France. Richard terminated his betrothal to Alys in 1190 while at Messina. It has been suggested that Alys had become the mistress of Richard's own father, Henry II of England, and possibly the mother of an
2 CONC  illegitimate child; a marriage between Richard and Alys therefore would have been technically impossible for religious reasons of affinity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT Richard had Berengaria brought to him by his mother Eleanor of Aquitaine. Because Richard was already on the Third Crusade, having wasted no time in setting off after his coronation, the two women had a long and difficult journey 
2 CONC to catch up with him. They arrived at Messina in Sicily in 1191 during Lent (when the marriage could not take place) and were joined by Richard's sister Joan, the widowed queen of Sicily. Berengaria was left in Joan's custody.[5] 
2 CONC En route to the Holy Land, the ship carrying Berengaria and Joan ran aground off the coast of Cyprus, and they were threatened by the island's ruler, Isaac Comnenus. Richard came to their rescue, captured the island, and overthrew
2 CONC  Comnenus. Berengaria married Richard the Lionheart on 12 May 1191 in the Chapel of St. George at Limassol on Cyprus and was crowned the same day by the archbishop of Bordeaux and the bishops of Évreux and Bayonne.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen consort
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria's Alarm for the Safety of Her Husband, Richard Coeur de Lion, Awakened by the Sight of His Girdle Offered for Sale at Rome (Charles Allston Collins, 1850)
2 CONT Whether the marriage was ever even consummated is a matter for conjecture. In any case, Richard certainly took his new wife with him for the first part of the Third Crusade. This was unusual (although Richard's mother and Berengar
2 CONC ia's predecessor, Eleanor of Aquitaine, had, when queen of France, been with her husband throughout the Second Crusade). Berengaria returned well before Richard left the Holy Land; on his return to Europe, he was captured and impr
2 CONC isoned. Berengaria remained in Europe, based at Beaufort-en-Vallée, attempting to raise money for his ransom.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his release, Richard returned to England and was not joined by his wife. Richard then set about reclaiming the territories in France that had been lost by his brother John or taken by King Philip II. His focus was on his kin
2 CONC gdom, not his queen. He was ordered by Pope Celestine III to reunite with Queen Berengaria and to show fidelity to her in the future. Richard, now mostly spending his time in France, obeyed and took Berengaria to church every week
2 CONC  thereafter. When he died in 1199, she was distressed, perhaps more so at deliberately being overlooked as queen of England. Some historians believe that Berengaria honestly loved her husband, and Richard's feelings for her were m
2 CONC erely formal because the marriage was a political rather than a romantic union.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen dowager
2 CONT Berengaria never visited England during King Richard's lifetime, and Richard spent less than six months in England while they were married. There is evidence, however, that she may have done so in the years following his death, si
2 CONC nce she is believed to have been present at the translation of St Thomas Becket's remains at Canterbury in 1220.[7] The traditional description of her as "the only English queen never to set foot in the country" still would be tru
2 CONC e because she did not visit England during the time she was Richard's consort. She certainly sent envoys to England several times, mainly to inquire about the pension she was due as dowager queen and Richard's widow, which King Jo
2 CONC hn failed to pay. Although Queen Eleanor intervened and Pope Innocent III threatened him with an interdict if he did not pay Berengaria what was due, King John still owed her more than £4,000 (equivalent to £6,747,917 in 2023) whe
2 CONC n he died. The payments were eventually made during the reign of John's son Henry III of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady of Le Mans
2 CONT Berengaria became the Lady (Lord) of Le Mans,[8] one of her dower properties, where she eventually settled. She founded L'Épau Abbey in Le Mans in 1229, where she was buried. In 1240, Archbishop Rodrigo Jimenez de Rada of Toledo w
2 CONC rote of Berengaria that she lived "as a most praiseworthy widow and stayed for the most part in the city of Le Mans, which she held as part of her marriage dower, devoting herself to almsgiving, prayer and good works, witnessing a
2 CONC s an example to all women of chastity and religion and in the same city she came to the end of her days with a happy death."[9] A skeleton thought to be hers was rediscovered in 1960 during the restoration of the abbey. These rema
2 CONC ins are preserved beneath the stone effigy of the queen, which is now to be found in the chapter house of the abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Historical significance
2 CONT
2 CONT Coloured illustration of Berengaria's tomb effigy
2 CONT Historian Ann Trindade says about Berengaria that she "is remembered as a benefactor of several…religious congregations and institutions and was regarded as a model of piety."[10] She also states that “Berengaria’s life illustrate
2 CONC s very clearly the constraints under which medieval women, even aristocratic ones, were obliged to live.”[11] Berengaria's struggles are a good example of what many women faced in the thirteenth century, and “those long years of w
2 CONC idowhood reveal, on the basis of record, a strong courageous woman, independent, solitary, battling against difficult political and economic circumstances, with little interest in the trappings of courtly existence, sustained by h
2 CONC er faith in Christianity and her loyalty to the See of St Peter, not afraid to assert her rights against powerful enemies, both lay and clerical.”[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT In fiction
2 CONT Novels featuring Berengaria include:
2 CONT
2 CONT Propinquity by John Macgregor
2 CONT The Heart Of The Lion by Jean Plaidy
2 CONT Willow Maid by Maureen Peters
2 CONT My Lord Brother the Lionheart by Molly Costain Haycraft
2 CONT The Lute Player by Norah Lofts
2 CONT Standard of Honor by Jack Whyte
2 CONT The Talisman by Sir Walter Scott
2 CONT Winning His Spurs by G. A. Henty
2 CONT Valentina by Fern Michaels
2 CONT The Queen's Witch by Cecelia Holland
2 CONT Lionheart by Sharon Kay Penman
2 CONT The Passionate Brood by Margaret Campbell Barnes
2 CONT The Boy Knight by G.A. Henty
2 CONT Queen Without a Country by Rachel Bard
2 CONT Shield of Three Lions by Pamela Kaufman
2 CONT Banners of Gold by Pamela Kaufman
2 CONT The Autumn Throne by Elizabeth Chadwick
2 CONT On the stage
2 CONT Holy Warriors by David Eldridge, premiered at Shakespeare's Globe in July and August 2014.
2 CONT In the media
2 CONT In the 1923 film Richard the Lion-Hearted, Queen Berengaria is played by Kathleen Clifford. The 1935 film The Crusades, starring Loretta Young and Henry Wilcoxon, tells a fictionalised story of Richard and Berengaria's marriage. T
2 CONC he 1954 historical drama film King Richard and the Crusaders, starring George Sanders and Paula Raymond, shows Richard and Berengaria as married and on the Third Crusade. The 1960s British television series Richard the Lionheart f
2 CONC eatures their marriage prominently, with Berengaria played by Sheila Whittingham. All versions were highly romanticised and are not reliable sources of information about the queen. Berengaria was played by Laila Taher in the 1963 
2 CONC film Saladin. She was played by Zoë Wanamaker in the BBC 13-part drama The Devil's Crown (1978).
2 CONT
