1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernardo_I_de_La_Marche
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00048735&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The County of Marche (Occitan: la Marcha) was a medieval French county, approximately corresponding to the modern département of Creuse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marche first appeared as a separate fief about the middle of the 10th century when William III, duke of Aquitaine, gave it to one of his vassals named Boso, who took the title of count. In the 12th century it passed to the family 
2 CONC of Lusignan, sometime also counts of Angoulême and counts of Limousin, until the death of the childless Count Guy in 1308, when it was seized by Philip IV of France. In 1316 it was made an appanage for his youngest son the Prince,
2 CONC  afterwards Charles IV and a few years later (1327) it passed into the hands of the family of Bourbon. The family of Armagnac held it from 1435 to 1477, when it reverted to the Bourbons, and in 1527 it was seized by Francis I and 
2 CONC became part of the domains of the French crown. It was divided into Haute Marche and Basse Marche, the estates of the former being in existence until the 17th century. From 1470 until the Revolution the province was under the juri
2 CONC sdiction of the parlement of Paris.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://fabpedigree.com/s026/f017518.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The County of Marche (Occitan: la Marcha) was a medieval French county, approximately corresponding to the modern département of Creuse.  Marche first appeared as a separate fief about the middle of the 10th century when William I
2 CONC II, duke of Aquitaine, gave it to one of his vassals named Boso, who took the title of count. In the 12th century it passed to the family of Lusignan, sometime also counts of Angoulême and counts of Limousin, until the death of th
2 CONC e childless Count Guy in 1308, when it was seized by Philip IV of France. In 1316 it was made an appanage for his youngest son the Prince, afterwards Charles IV and a few years later (1327) it passed into the hands of the family o
2 CONC f Bourbon. The family of Armagnac held it from 1435 to 1477, when it reverted to the Bourbons, and in 1527 it was seized by Francis I and became part of the domains of the French crown. It was divided into Haute Marche and Basse M
2 CONC arche, the estates of the former being in existence until the 17th century. From 1470 until the Revolution the province was under the jurisdiction of the parlement of Paris. 
