0 @N6000000002187820713@ NOTE Philip I of Taranto
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Philip I of Taranto (1278–1332): of the Angevin house, titular Emperor of Constantinople, despot of Epirus, King of Albania, Prince of Achaea and Taranto, Lord of Durazzo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Charles II of Anjou, King of Naples, and Mary, daughter of Stephen V of Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life of Philip of Taranto
1 CONT 1278 Philip is born on November 10, 1278 in Naples.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1294 In September, 1294 in L'Aquila, Philip marries Thamar Angela Komnene Dukaina, daughter of Nicephoros I Dukas Komnenos, despot of Epirus, and Anna Kantakuzene; with this marriage, Philip becomes leader of the Aetholian league 
1 CONC and Prince of Corfu. He also buys the title and much of the possessions of the Despotate of Epirus.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1299 Philip is defeated and taken prisoner in the battle of La Falconara by Frederick III of Sicily.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1301 His father Charles II resigns to him the Kingdom of Albania and a claim to the Principality of Achaea, but Achaea is held by Isabelle Villehardouin and her husband, Philip of Savoy.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1307 Isabelle and Philip of Savoy are deposed, and Philip becomes ruling Prince of Achaea.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1309 Philip divorces his wife Thamar.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1312 Philip drops his claims on the despotate of Epirus.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1313 Philip marries Catherine, daughter of Charles of Valois, and becomes titular Emperor of Constantinople jure uxoris (in right of his wife). He loses the Principality of Achaea to his rival, Marguerite of Hainault, and her husb
1 CONC and, Louis of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1315 Philip leads a Neapolitan army to the aid of Florence, but both are defeated by Pisa at the Battle of Monte Catino.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1332 Philip dies on December 23, 1332.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Wives and sons
1 CONT Sons of his first marriage (1296), with Thamar Angela, ended with a divorce (1309):
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles (1296–1315, killed at the Battle of Monte Catino), Prince of Achaea, Vicar of Romania 
1 CONT Philip (1297–1330), Despot of Romania 
1 CONT Jeanne (1297–1317), married to Oshin I, King of Armenia and then Oshin of Korikos 
1 CONT Marguerite (1298–1340), married to Walter VI of Brienne, titular Duke of Athens 
1 CONT Robert (1299–1364), Prince of Taranto, titular Emperor of Constantinople 
1 CONT Bianca (1309–1337), married to Ramon Berenguer of Aragon 
1 CONT Sons of his second marriage (1313), with Catherine of Valois:
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis (1320–1362), Prince of Taranto and King of Naples 
1 CONT Marguerite (about 1325–1380), married Francis of Baux, Duke of Andria 
1 CONT Mary (1327–died young) 
1 CONT Philip II (1329–1374), King of Albania, Prince of Taranto and Achaea, titular Emperor of Constantinople 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Charles II Prince of Taranto
1 CONT 1294–1332 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Robert of Taranto 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Isabelle Villehardouin Prince of Achaea
1 CONT 1307–1313 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Marguerite of Hainault 
