0 @N6000000002187757991@ NOTE Henry III of Champagne
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry III the Fat (French: Henri le Gros) (c. 1244–1274) was Count of Champagne and Brie, and King of Navarre (as Henry I) from 1270 until 1274.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born as the youngest son of Theobald I of Navarre and Champagne (who had in 1234 became King Theobald I of Navarre) and Margaret of Bourbon. In December 1270 Henry succeeded his eldest brother Theobald V of Champagne (Theob
1 CONC ald II of Navarre) as King of Navarre and Count of Champagne.
1 CONT
1 CONT His proclamation at Pamplona, however, did not take place till March of the following year, and his coronation was delayed until May 1273. After a brief reign, characterized, it is said, by dignity and talent, he died in July 1274
1 CONC , suffocated, according to the generally received accounts, by his own fat.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his death with no male heir, the male line of the counts of Champagne and kings of Navarre became extinct.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1269 Henry married Blanche, daughter of Robert, Count of Artois, and niece of King Louis IX. He was succeeded by his only legitimate child, Joan I of Navarre; her 1284 marriage to Philip IV (who became King of France in the sam
1 CONC e year) united the crown of Navarre to that of France, with Champagne becoming part of the French royal domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Divine Comedy, Dante sees Henry sitting outside the gates of Purgatory, where he is grouped with a number of other European monarchs of the 13th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
1 CONT
