0 @N6000000002187757036@ NOTE Andrew II of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrew II (Hungarian: II. András or II. Endre, Slovak: Ondrej II) (1175-1235) was a son of Béla III and succeeded his nephew, the infant Ladislaus III, as King of Hungary in 1205.
1 CONT
1 CONT Few other royal reigns were as detrimental to the Hungarian realm as Andrew's. Valiant, enterprising, pious as he was, all these fine qualities were ruined by a reckless good nature which never thought of the future. He declared i
1 CONC n a decree that the generosity of a king should be limitless, and he followed this principle throughout his reign. He gave away everything - money, villages, domains, whole counties - to the utter impoverishment of the treasury, t
1 CONC hereby rendering the crown, for the first time in Hungarian history, dependent upon the great nobility eager for personal gain. In all matters of government, Andrew was equally reckless and haphazard. He was directly responsible f
1 CONC or the beginnings of the feudal anarchy which led to the extinction of the Árpád dynasty at the end of the 13th century. The great nobles did not even respect the lives of the royal family, for Andrew was recalled from a futile at
1 CONC tempt to reconquer Galicia through the murder of his first wife Gertrude of Meran in 1213 by rebellious nobles jealous of the influence of her relatives.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1215 he married Iolanthe (Yolande) of France, but in 1217 was compelled by Pope Honorius III to lead the Fifth Crusade to the Holy Land, which he undertook in hopes of being elected Latin emperor of Constantinople. The crusade 
1 CONC was not popular in Hungary, but Andrew contrived to collect 15,000 men together, whom he led to Venice. After the surrender of Hungarian claims on Zara (Zadar), about two-thirds of the crusaders were conveyed to Acre. Nevertheless
1 CONC  the whole expedition was a forlorn hope. The Kingdom of Jerusalem was by this time reduced to a strip of coast about 440 mi² in extent, and after a drawn battle with the Turks on the Jordan River on November 10 1217 and fruitless
1 CONC  assaults on the fortresses of the Lebanon and on Mount Tabor, Andrew started home (January 18, 1218) through Antioch (Antakya), Iconium (Konya), Constantinople, and Bulgaria. On his return he found the feudal barons in the ascend
1 CONC ant, and they extorted from him the Golden Bull.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrew's last exploit was to defeat an invasion of Frederick II of Austria in 1234. That same year he married his third wife, Beatrice of Este.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT Andrew had five children by his first wife, Gertrude:
1 CONT
1 CONT Maria of Hungary (1203-1221), married Tsar Ivan Asen II of Bulgaria 
1 CONT Bela IV of Hungary (1206-1270) 
1 CONT Saint Elizabeth of Hungary (1207-1231) 
1 CONT Kálmán, Duke of Croatia (1208-1241) 
1 CONT András, King of Galicia (1210-1234) 
1 CONT From his second marriage to Yolande de Courtney, he had one daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT Jolán (Yolande) of Hungary (1215-1251), married James I of Aragon 
1 CONT Andrew's third marriage to Beatrice d'Este produced one posthumous son:
1 CONT
1 CONT István (1236-1271), who was himself father of King Andrew III of Hungary 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ladislaus III King of Hungary
1 CONT 1205–1235 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Béla IV 
