0 @N6000000003949871822@ NOTE Colombia Encyclopedia (c) 1944 p.67  His reign was marked by controversy with barons and the great feudatories and by the issuance of the Golden Bull of 1222, which has been called the Hungarian Magna Carta. Powerful landed intere
1 CONC sts forced Andrew to spend royal funds so recklessly that the crown was soon impoverished and dependent on the feudatories, who soon reduced Hungary to a state of anarchy.
1 CONT After the accession of his brother, András demanded Croatia and Dalmatia as an appanage but this was refused.  He revolted, and by 1198 obtained his demands and became Duke of Croatia and Dalmatia[571].  He and subsequent dukes ac
1 CONC ted as the king of Hungary's deputy in the kingdom of Croatia.  He conquered western Hum (Herzegovina) down to the river Neretva in 1198[572].  The Continuatio Admuntensis records that he was arrested in 1203, suspected of plottin
1 CONC g to take over the kingdom, and imprisoned "in palacio Strigoniensi quod alio nomine Gran vocatur"[573].  He ousted his nephew in 1205 and succeeded as ANDRÁS II King of Hungary.  He played an active part in the dismemberment of G
1 CONC alich-Volynia after the death of Roman Mstislavich Prince of Galich in 1205, Hungary and Poland eventually agreeing the division of the territories between them under the treaty of Spisz in 1214, although Hungary expelled Poland f
1 CONC rom Peremyshl and Lyubachev in 1215/1216[574].  In 1211, King András hired the Order of Teutonic Knights, who had been expelled back to Europe from Palestine, to defend the eastern frontier of Transylvania against the Kumans[575],
1 CONC  but they attempted to establish their autonomy there under the protection of the Pope.  King András expelled the Teutonic Knights in 1225[576] on the pretext of their having disobeyed his orders.  King András set sail from Split 
1 CONC for Palestine on crusade in Oct 1217, but left Acre in early 1218 having achieved little besides acquiring a small collection of religious relics[577].  He returned by the land route, via Constantinople, but at the end of 1218 he 
1 CONC was seized in Bulgarian territory and released only after agreeing the marriage of his daughter to Ivan Asen II Tsar of Bulgaria[578].  He threatened war with Serbia after Grand Župan Stefan was crowned King of Serbia by the papal
1 CONC  legate in 1217, claiming that he alone had the right to this title, but did not carry out the threat[579].  King András's abuses caused the Hungarian nobles to rebel in 1222 and forced him to issue the Golden Bull, a charter defi
1 CONC ning the rights of the nobility and restricting the king's right to appoint foreigners to office without the consent of the Council[580], a reform forced by the rebellion of the lower nobility in Croatia according to Goldstein[581
1 CONC ].  In 1227, Bortz Khan of the Kumans swore allegiance to the king of Hungary after ordering the baptism of his people, rex Cumaniæ being added to the titles of the Hungarian king soon after[582].  King András attacked north-west 
1 CONC Bulgaria in 1232 and recaptured Beograd and Branicevo which he had been forced to cede as part of the dowry of his daughter Maria.  He crossed the Danube into Wallachia where the Hungarians created a Banate in the Severin region[5
1 CONC 83].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the death in 1235 of "Andreas rex Hungarie" and his burial "in civitate Waradino"[584]. 
0 @N6000000004533182090@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Andrew II (1175-1235) (Hungarian:II. András,Slovak:Ondrej II) was ason of Bela III of Hungary and succeeded his nephew, the infantLadislaus III, in 1205.
1 CONT
1 CONT No other king of Hungary, perhaps, was so mischievous to his country.Valiant, enterprising, pious as he was, all these fine qualities wereruined by a reckless good nature which never thought of the morrow. Hedeclares in one of his
1 CONC  decrees that the generosity of a king should belimitless, and he acted up to this principle throughout his reign. Hegave away everything, money, villages, domains, whole counties, to theutter impoverishment of the treasury, there
1 CONC by rendering the crown, forthe first time in Hungarian history, dependent upon the greatfeudatories, who, in Hungary as elsewhere, took all they could get andgave as little as possible in return. In all matters of government,Andre
1 CONC w was equally reckless and haphazard. He is directly responsiblefor the beginnings of the feudal anarchy which well-nigh led to theextinction of the monarchy at the end of the 13th century. The greatfeudatories did not even respec
1 CONC t the lives of the royal family, forAndrew was recalled from a futile attempt to reconquer Galicia (whichreally lay beyond the Hungarian sphere of influence), through themurder of his first wife Gertrude of Meran (1185 - September
1 CONC  24,1213), by rebellious nobles jealous of the influence of her relatives.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1215 he married Iolanthe (Yolande) of France, but in 1217 wascompelled by the pope to lead the Fifth Crusade to the Holy Land,which he undertook in hopes of being elected Latin emperor ofConstantinople. The crusade excited no e
1 CONC nthusiasm in Hungary, butAndrew contrived to collect 15,000 men together, whom he led toVenice; whence, not without much haggling and the surrender of all theHungarian claims upon Zara, about two-thirds of them were conveyed toAcr
1 CONC e. Nevertheless the whole expedition was a forlorn hope. TheChristian kingdom of Palestine was by this time reduced to a strip ofcoast about 440 sq. m. in extent, and after a drawn battle with theTurks on the Jordan (November 10),
1 CONC  and fruitless assaults on thefortresses of the Lebanon and on Mount Tabor, Andrew started home(January 18, 1218) through Antioch, Iconium, Constantinople andBulgaria. On his return he found the feudal barons in the ascendant,and 
1 CONC they extorted from him the Golden Bull.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrew's last exploit was to defeat an invasion of Frederick ofAustria in 1234. The same year he married his third wife, Beatrice ofEste.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT Andrew had five children by his first wife, Gertrude:
1 CONT
1 CONT Maria of Hungary (1203-1221), married Tsar Ivan Asen II of Bulgaria
1 CONT Bela IV of Hungary (1206-1270)
1 CONT Saint Elizabeth of Hungary (1207-1231)
1 CONT Kálmán, Duke of Croatia (1208-1241)
1 CONT András, King of Halicz (1210-1234)
1 CONT From his second marriage to Yolande de Courtney, he had one daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT Jolán (Yolande) of Hungary (1215-1251), married James I of Aragon
1 CONT Andrew's third marriage to Beatrice d'Este produced one posthumousson:
1 CONT
1 CONT István (1236-1271), who was himself father of King Andrew III ofHungary
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
