0 @N6000000002188376360@ NOTE Ferdinand I of Aragon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand I (Ferran - Catalan) (Medina del Campo, Castile, 27 November 1380 – Igualada, Catalonia, 2 April 1416), called of Antequera and also the Just or the Honest, was king of Aragon, Valencia, Majorca, Sardinia and (nominal) C
1 CONC orsica and king of Sicily, duke (nominal) of Athens and Neopatria, and count of Barcelona, Roussillon and Cerdanya (1412-1416). Regent of Castile (1406-1416). See Crown of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the younger son of king John I of Castile and Eleanor of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1406, upon the death of his elder brother, king Henry III of Castile, Ferdinand declined the Castilian crown and instead, with Henry's widow Catherine of Lancaster, became coregent during the minority of his nephew John II of C
1 CONC astile. In this capacity he distinguished himself by his prudent administration of domestic affairs.
1 CONT
1 CONT In a war with the Muslim kingdom of Granada, he conquered the town of Antequera (1410), and hence his surname.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Ferdinand's maternal uncle, the king Martin I of Aragon (Martin II of Sicily), died without surviving issue, Ferdinand was chosen king of Aragon in 1412 to succeed him in the Compromise of Caspe. The other candidate, count J
1 CONC ames II of Urgell (see counts of Urgell), revolted and Ferdinand dissolved the county of Urgell in 1413.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand created the title of prince of Girona for the heir of the Crown of Aragon in 1414.
1 CONT
1 CONT The most notable accomplishment of his brief reign was his agreement in 1416 to depose the antipope Benedict XIII, thereby helping to end the Great Schism, which had divided the Western Church for nearly 40 years.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is buried in the Aragonese royal pantheon of the monastery of Poblet, in a magnificent tomb ordered by his son Alfonso to Pere Oller in 1417.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lorenzo Valla wrote an official biography of Ferdinand (Historiarum Ferdinandi regis Aragonum libri sex).
1 CONT Contents
