0 @N6000000002188376744@ NOTE Ferdinand I of Portugal
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand I (Portuguese: Fernando, pronounced [f??'n?~du]; Lisbon, 31 October 1345 – 22 October 1383 in Lisbon), sometimes referred to as the Handsome (Portuguese: o Formoso) or rarely as the Inconstant (Portuguese: o Inconstante)
1 CONC , was the ninth King of Portugal and the Algarve, the second (but eldest surviving) son of Peter I and his wife, Constance of Castile. He succeeded his father in 1367.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of Peter of Castile in 1369, Ferdinand, as great-grandson of Sancho IV by the female line, laid claim to the vacant throne, for which the kings of Aragon and Navarre, and afterwards John of Gaunt, duke of Lancaster (m
1 CONC arried in 1370 to Constance, the eldest daughter of Peter), also became competitors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile Henry II of Castile, Peter's illegitimate brother, who had defeated Peter, assumed his crown and took the field. After one or two indecisive campaigns, all parties were ready to accept the mediation of Pope Gregory XI. T
1 CONC he conditions of the treaty, ratified in 1371, included a marriage between Ferdinand and Leonora of Castile. But before the union could take place Ferdinand had become passionately attached to Leonor Telles de Menezes, the wife of
1 CONC  one of his own courtiers. Having procured a dissolution of her previous marriage, he lost no time in making Leonor his queen.
1 CONT
1 CONT This strange conduct, although it raised a serious insurrection in Portugal, did not at once result in a war with Henry. However, the outward concord was soon disturbed by the intrigues of the duke of Lancaster, who prevailed on F
1 CONC erdinand to enter into a secret treaty for the expulsion of Henry from his throne. The war which followed was unsuccessful; and peace was again made in 1373. On the death of Henry in 1379, the duke of Lancaster once more put forwa
1 CONC rd his claims, and again found an ally in Portugal. According to the Continental annalists, the English proved as offensive to their allies as to their enemies in the field. So Ferdinand made a peace for himself at Badajoz in 1382
1 CONC . It stipulated that Beatrice, Ferdinand's daughter and heiress, would marry King John I of Castile, and thus secure the ultimate union of the two crowns.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand left no male heir when he died on October 22, 1383, and the direct Burgundian line, which had been in possession of the throne since the days of Count Henry (about 1112), became extinct. The stipulations of the treaty of
1 CONC  Badajoz were set aside, and John, Grand Master of the order of Aviz, Ferdinand's illegitimate brother, claimed the throne. This led to a period of war and political indefinition known as the 1383-1385 Crisis. John became the firs
1 CONC t king of the House of Aviz in 1385.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Ancestors
1 CONT Ferdinand's ancestors in three generations Ferdinand I of Portugal Father:
1 CONT Peter I of Portugal Father's father:
1 CONT Afonso IV of Portugal Father's father's father:
1 CONT Denis of Portugal
1 CONT Father's father's mother:
1 CONT Elizabeth of Aragon
1 CONT Father's mother:
1 CONT Beatrice of Castile Father's mother's father:
1 CONT Sancho IV of Castile
1 CONT Father's mother's mother:
1 CONT Maria de Molina
1 CONT Mother:
1 CONT Constance of Peñafiel Mother's father:
1 CONT Juan Manuel, Duke of Peñafiel Mother's father's father:
1 CONT Juan Manuel, Lord of Villena
1 CONT Mother's father's mother:
1 CONT Beatrice of Savoy
1 CONT Mother's mother:
1 CONT Constance of Aragon Mother's mother's father:
1 CONT James II of Aragon
1 CONT Mother's mother's mother:
1 CONT Blanche of Anjou
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages and descendants
1 CONT
1 CONT Fernando married Leonor Telles (or Teles) de Menezes, formerly the wife of the late nobleman João Lourenço da Cunha, Lord of Pombeiro, and daughter of Martin Alfonso Telles de Menezes (Martim Afonso Teles de Menezes). She gave him
1 CONC  two sons who both died young, and one daughter.
1 CONT Name Birth Death Notes
1 CONT By Leonor Telles de Menezes (c. 1340-April 27, 1386; married in 1371)
1 CONT Infanta Beatriz 1372 1408 Heiress of her father. Married King John (Juan) I of Castile and succeeded Ferdinand as the disputed tenth Portuguese monarch.
1 CONT Infante Pedro (Peter) 1370 or 1380 1370 or 1380  
1 CONT Infante Afonso 1371 or 1382 1371 or 1382  
1 CONT Illegitimate offspring
1 CONT Isabel of Portugal 1364 1395 Countess of Gijón and Noroña through marriage to Alfonso, Count of Gijón and Noroña, illegitimate son of Henry II of Castile.
