0 @N6000000006127668843@ NOTE Heinrich XV., Herzog von Bayern, der Natternberger, als Herzog von Niederbayern Heinrich III., (* 28. August 1312; † 18. Juni 1333 in Natternberg bei Deggendorf).Er erhielt den Beinamen „der Natternberger“, da er sich vorzugsweise
1 CONC  auf der Burg Natternberg aufhielt. Im Todesjahr seines Vaters Otto III. als einziger Sohn aus dessen zweiten Ehe mit der Agnes von Schlesien-Glogau, Tochter von Herzog HeinrichIII. geboren, unterstand er zuerst der Vormundschaft 
1 CONC Ludwig des Bayern, später unter der seines Vetters Heinrich XIV. Unter Heinrich XV. war Deggendorf das einzige Mal in seiner Geschichte kurzzeitig Residenzstadt des selbständigen HerzogtumsNiederbayern-Deggendorf (1331–1332), das 
1 CONC außer der Hauptstadt die Orte Cham, Dingolfing, Landau und Vilshofen umfasste. 1332 kam es zur gemeinsamen Regierung mit Heinrich XIV.
0 @N6000000000337133930@ NOTE (Research):John II Encyclop‘dia Britannica Article    born 1398, Medina del Campo, Leon died 1479, Barcelona   king of Aragon (1458-79) and also king of Navarre (1425-79); he was the instigator of the union of Castile and Aragon t
1 CONC hrough the historic marriage of his son Ferdinand with Isabella of Castile.   John was a younger son of Ferdinand of Antequera, elected king of Aragon (as Ferdinand I) in 1412. John and his brothers retained their positions and re
1 CONC venues in Castile, and he married Blanche, the heiress to Navarre, of which he claimed the kingship (1425-79). On his wife's death, he married Juana Enriques, daughter of the admiral of castile (1447), whose ambitions led to a con
1 CONC flict between John and his eldest son by his first marriage, Charles, prince of Viana.   In 1458 John succeeded his elder brother Alfonso V as king of Aragon, with Sicily and Sardinia. He was blamed for the mysterious death of his
1 CONC  son Charles, who had a strong following in Catalonia, where John's policies were deeply resented. The Catalans offered the crown to Peter of Portugal, who died, after which they appealed to the French for aid. In October 1469 Joh
1 CONC n arranged for his son by his second marriage, Ferdinand, to marry Isabella, the recognized heiress to Castile, despite the opposition of her brother, Henry IV of Castile. This marriage led to the union of Castile and Aragon and t
1 CONC he creation of the modern state of Spain. In Aragon, John's rule deepened the rift between Aragon proper and Catalonia.
