1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jeanne (or Joan or Johanna) of Navarre (c. 1271 – April 4, 1305), Queen Consort of France, was the daughter of king Henry I of Navarre and Blanche of Artois. In 1274, upon the death of her father, she became Countess of Champagne 
1 CONC and Queen Regnant of Navarre. Her mother was her guardian and regent in Navarre. Various powers, both foreign and Navarrese, sought to take advantage of the minority of the heiress and the weakness of the female regent, which caus
1 CONC ed Joan and her mother to seek protection at the court of Philip III of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the age of 13, Jeanne married his son and heir Philip IV of France on August 16, 1284, becoming Queen of France a year later. The crowns of Navarre and France were henceforth united for almost half a century. Their children wer
1 CONC e:
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite (1288–1300) 
1 CONT Louis X of France (October 4, 1289 – June 5, 1316) 
1 CONT Blanche (1290–1294) 
1 CONT Philip V of France (1293 – January 3, 1322) 
1 CONT Charles IV of France (1294 – February 1, 1328) 
1 CONT Isabelle (1296 – August 23, 1358), married Edward II of England 
1 CONT Robert (1297 – July 1308) 
1 CONT Their three surviving sons would all become Kings of France, in turn, and their only surviving daughter Queen of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jeanne led an army against the Count de Bar when he rebelled against her.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jeanne died under mysterious circumstances; one chronicler even accused her husband of having killed her.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Doherty, P.C. Isabella and the Strange Death of Edward II 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry I/III Queen of Navarre
1 CONT 1274–1305 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Louis I 
1 CONT Countess of Champagne
1 CONT 1274–1305 
