0 @N6000000002188559685@ NOTE Louis of Sicily
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis the Child (1337 – 16 October 1355) was king of Sicily, then called Trinacria, from 1342 to 1355.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father was Peter II, whom he succeeded at the age of five, and his mother was Elisabeth of Carinthia. His reign began under the joint regency of his uncle Giovanni, the duke of Randazzo, and his mother.
1 CONT
1 CONT He lived at Randazzo until 1347. In that year, the plague called the Black Death arrived. In 1348, the duke named Blasco II de Alagona, a Catalan, regent. Rivalry—before then mitigated by the duke—between the Italian noblesse (the
1 CONC  families of Chiaramonte, Palizzi, and Scaloro degli Uberti; called the filoangioini) and the Catalan noblesse (the families of Peralta, Alagona, and Ventimiglia; called the filoaragonesi) degenerated into civil war. Louis, a Cata
1 CONC lan by descent, sent the royal army after the Chiaramonte unto Milazzo. Only in 1350 did peace arrive.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1355, the king's cousin Frederick, lord of Aci and son of the duke of Randazzo, died. In an attempt to avoid the plague, Louis left the fortress Agira for the castle of Aci. He died in the Acese fortress on 16 October in that y
1 CONC ear, of the plague, and only seventeen years of age. He was buried in the cathedral of Catania next to Frederick II (III) and Giovanni, the old regent.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Peter II King of Sicily
1 CONT 1342–1355 Succeeded by
1 CONT Frederick III
0 @N6000000002187825557@ NOTE Stephen II, Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke Stephen II of Bavaria (1319–13 May 1375, Landshut) (German: Stephan II mit der Hafte, Herzog von Bayern), since 1347 Duke of Bavaria. He was the second son of Emperor Louis IV and Beatrix of Silesia-Glogau and a member of the
1 CONC  Wittelsbach dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father reunited Bavaria in 1340 but in 1349 the country was divided for the emperor's six sons again in Upper Bavaria, Lower Bavaria-Landshut und Bavaria-Straubing. Stephen II ruled from 1349 to 1353 together with his brothers
1 CONC  William I and Albert I in Holland and Lower Bavaria-Landshut, since 1353 only in Lower Bavaria-Landshut.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the temporary reconcilement of the Wittelsbach with Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor, who had finally confirmed all Wittelsbach possessions, Stephen joined Charles' march into Italy in 1354. But already the Golden Bull of 1356
1 CONC  caused a new conflict since only the Palatinate branch of the Wittelsbach and his brother Louis VI the Roman as margrave of Brandenburg were invested with the electoral dignity. Stephen II was the last son of Emperor Louis IV abs
1 CONC olved from the excommunication in 1362.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Duke Meinhard, the son of his older brother Louis V died in 1363, Stephen II succeeded also in Upper Bavaria and invaded Tyrol. To strengthen his position he confederated with Bernabò Visconti. Stephen finally renounced Tyrol
1 CONC  for Habsburg with the Peace of Schärding against a huge compensation after the death of Margarete Maultasch in 1369.
1 CONT
1 CONT His conflict with his brother Louis VI the Roman on the heritage of Meinhard finally caused also the loss of Brandenburg for the Wittelsbach dynasty since Louis then made Charles IV his contracted heir. However Stephen accepted hi
1 CONC s brother Otto, the last Wittelsbach regent of Brandenburg, as his nominal co-regent when he returned to Bavaria in 1373. Stephen was succeeded by his three sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married two times. First, 27 June 1328 with Elizabetta of Sicily, daughter of King Frederick III of Sicily. Second, he married 14 February 1359 with Margarete of Nuremberg, daughter of John II of Nuremberg and Elisabeth of 
1 CONC Henneberg. All children were from first marriage, includings three sons, who finally divided Bavaria among each other in 1392 and one daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Stephen III of Bavaria-Ingolstadt (1337–September 26, 1413, Niederschönfeld).
1 CONT    2. Frederick of Bavaria-Landshut (1339–December 4, 1393,Budweis).
1 CONT    3. John II of Bavaria-Munich (1341–1397).
1 CONT    4. Agnes (1338–?), married ca. 1356 King James I of Cyprus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Two of Stephen's sons (Stephen III and Frederick) and one grandson (John's son Ernest) were married with daughters of his ally Bernabò Visconti.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1447 Bavaria-Ingolstadt was united with Bavaria-Landshut, which was seized by Bavaria-Munich in 1503.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Louis IV Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT 1347–1375 Succeeded by
1 CONT John II
1 CONT Frederick
1 CONT Stephen III
0 @N6000000006127668886@ NOTE Herzog Stephan II. von Bayern (* 1319; † 1375 in Landshut) war seit 1347 Herzog von Bayern. Er war der zweite Sohn von Kaiser Ludwig IV. dem Bayern aus seiner ersten Ehe mit Beatrix von Schlesien-Glogau.Regierung  [Bearbeiten]Unte
1 CONC r seinem Vater war Stephan Inhaber der Reichslandvogtei in Schwaben und im Elsaß. 1347 folgte Stephan seinem verstorbenem Vater mit seinen fünf Brüdern als Herzog des seit Dezember 1340 wiedervereinigten Bayerns nach.Daneben hielt
1 CONC en die Brüder die Markgrafschaft Brandenburg, die Grafschaft Tirol sowie die niederländischen Grafschaften Holland, Seeland und Hennegau. Zwei Jahre nach dem Tode Ludwigs des Bayern wurden dann die wittelsbachischen Länder imLands
1 CONC berger Vertrag unter seinen Söhnen aufgeteilt. Stephan II. regierte zunächst 1349 bis 1353 zusammen mit seine beiden Halbbrüdern Wilhelm I. und Albrecht I. in Straubing-Holland und Niederbayern-Landshut, seit dem Regensburger Vert
1 CONC rag 1353 nurnoch in Niederbayern-Landshut.Nach der vorübergehenden Aussöhnung mit Karl IV., der den Wittelsbachern alle Reichslehen bestätigt hatte, nahm Stephan 1354 am Italienzug Karls teil, zerstritt sich mit ihm aber wieder, a
1 CONC ls 1356 mit der Goldene Bulle Bayerns Rechte auf die Kurübergangen wurden. 1362 wurde Stephan als letzter der Söhne Ludwig des Bayern von der Exkommunikation befreit.Als 1363 sein Neffe Meinhard, der Sohn seines Bruders Ludwig V. 
1 CONC starb, folgte Stephan auch in Oberbayern nach und marschierte in Tirol ein, gab es aber 1369 im Frieden von Schärding den Habsburgern gegen eine hohe Entschädigung preis. Der Streitmit seinem Halbbruder Ludwig VI. dem Römer um das
1 CONC  bayerische Erbe Meinhards, das Stephan entgegen dem Landsberger Vertrag an sich gerissen hatte, führte schließlich zum Verlust der Mark Brandenburg für die Wittelsbacher im Jahre 1373, da LudwigVI. daraufhin seine Brüder enterbte
1 CONC  und eine Erbverbrüderung mit Karl IV. schloss. Gleichwohl erkannte Stephan später den aus Brandenburg heimgekehrten Bruder Otto V. als Mitregenten in Bayern an.1368 regelte Stephan das für Bayern bedeutsame Salzwesen in Reichenha
1 CONC ll. Zur Sicherung auf den Straßen und im Land erließ Stefan II. 1374 den "Großen Brandbrief" und verpflichtete darauf Ritter und Städte, die er mehrmals befehdete.Ehe und Nachkommen Herzog Stephan II. heiratete am 27. Juni 1328 in
1 CONC  München die Prinzessin Elisabeth (1309-1349), Tochter König Friedrich II. von Sizilien a.d.H. Aragon und dessen Gattin Prinzessin Eleonore von Sizilien a.d.H. Anjou. Aus der Ehe gingen vier Kinderhervor:Stephan III. der Prächtige
1 CONC  (1337-1413) (Linie Bayern-Ingolstadt) Gift 1 1364 in Mailand Prinzessin Thaddäa von Mailand (1350-1381), Tochter Visconte Bernabo Visconti Gift 2 1401 in Köln Prinzessin Elisabeth von Kleve, Witwe von Reinold von Valkenburg Agnes
1 CONC  (1338-14??) 8 1356 König Jakob I. von Zypern, Jerusalem und Armenien a.d.H. Lusignan (1334-1398) Friedrich (1339-1393) (Linie Bayern-Landshut) Gift 1 1360 in Reichenhall Gräfin Anna von Neuffen (1327-1380) Gift 2 1381 in Landshut
