0 @N6000000002187757899@ NOTE Peter II of Courtenay
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Peter of Courtenay (French: Pierre de Courtenay, died 1219) was emperor of the Latin Empire of Constantinople from 1216-1217.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a son of Peter of Courtenay (d. 1183), the youngest son of Louis VI of France and his second Queen consort Adélaide de Maurienne. His mother was Elizabeth of Courtenay.
1 CONT
1 CONT Peter first married Agnes of Nevers, via whom he obtained the three counties of Nevers, Auxerre, and Tonnerre. He took for his second wife, Yolanda of Flanders (d. 1219), a sister of Baldwin and Henry of Flanders, who were afterwa
1 CONC rds the first and second emperors of the Latin Empire of Constantinople. Peter accompanied his cousin, King Philip Augustus, on the crusade of 1190 and fought (alongside his brother Robert) in the Albigensian Crusade in 1209 and 1
1 CONC 211, when he took part in the siege of Lavaur. He was present at the Battle of Bouvines in 1214.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his brother-in-law, the emperor Henry, died without sons in 1216, Peter was chosen as his successor, and with a small army set out from France to take possession of his throne. Consecrated emperor at Rome, in a church outside
1 CONC  the walls, by Pope Honorius III on April 9, 1217, he borrowed some ships from the Venetians, promising in return to conquer Durazzo for them; but he failed in this enterprise, and sought to make his way to Constantinople by land.
1 CONC  On the journey he was seized by the despot of Epirus, Theodore Komnenos Doukas, and, after an imprisonment of two years, died, probably by foul means. Peter thus never governed his empire, which, however, was ruled for a time by 
1 CONC his wife, Yolanda, who had succeeded in reaching Constantinople. Two of his sons, Robert and Baldwin, in turn held the throne of the Latin Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT By his first wife Agnes of Nevers he had one child, Mahaut de Courtenay (Maud, Matilda, d. 1254), countess of Nevers, Auxerre and Tonerre.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his second wife Yolanda of Flanders, he had 10 children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip (d. 1226), Marquis of Namur, who declined the offer of the crown of the Latin Empire 
1 CONT Robert of Courtenay (d. 1228), Latin Emperor 
1 CONT Henry (d. 1229), Marquis of Namur 
1 CONT Baldwin II of Constantinople (d. 1273) 
1 CONT Margaret, Marchioness of Namur, who married first Raoul d'Issoudun and then Henry count of Vianden 
1 CONT Elizabeth, who married Walter count of Bar and then Eudes sire of Monta
1 CONT Yolanda, who married Andrew II of Hungary 
1 CONT Eleanor, who married Philip of Montfort, Lord of Tyre 
1 CONT Marie de Courtenay, who married Theodore I Lascaris of the Empire of Nicaea 
1 CONT Agnes, who married Geoffrey II Villehardouin, Prince of Achaea 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry Latin Emperor
1 CONT 1216–1217 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Yolanda 
0 @N6000000005598867008@ NOTE Pierre is also styled as Emporer of Constantinople, Compte de Courtena y, de Nevers, d'Auxerre et de Tonnerre and Marquis de Namur.  In 1217 , Pierre was made Emperor of Constantinople, following his brothers-in -law, Baldwin VI -
1 CONC  Count of Hainault and Flanders, and Henry.  In Apri l of that year he was crowned by Pope Honorius III and set out to defe at Theodore Comnenius, Emperor of Thessalonica.  After uncessfully att empting to take Epirus, which he ha
1 CONC d promised to the Venetians, in ret urn for their help, he was attacked on all sides when he reached the m ountains of Albania and was subsequently slain, probably by order of C omenenius.
