0 @N6000000002187757126@ NOTE Béla III of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Béla III (Hungarian: III. Béla, Croatian: Bela II, Slovak: Belo III), (c. 1148 – 23 April 1196), King of Hungary and Croatia (1172-1196), Duke of Croatia and Dalmatia (1161-1163).
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early years 
1 CONT 2 Despotes Alexius 
1 CONT 3 Return to Hungary 
1 CONT 4 Internal policy 
1 CONT 5 Expansion 
1 CONT 6 Marriages and children 
1 CONT 7 Legacy 
1 CONT 8 Sources 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early years
1 CONT Béla was the second son of King Géza II by his wife Euphrosyne of Kiev. In 1161, his father granted him the duchy of Croatia and Dalmatia as appanage, which was later confirmed by his brother, Stephen III who ascended the throne a
1 CONC fter their father's death on 31 May 1162.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Despotes Alexius
1 CONT In 1164, the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus concluded a treaty with king Stephen III, by which Béla was sent to Constantinople to be educated at the Imperial court. Manuel I, who had no legitimate sons, intended that Béla sho
1 CONC uld marry his daughter, Maria Comnena, and eventually succeed him as Emperor. Béla received a Greek name, Alexius, and the newly created courtly title of despotes which enjoyed the highest position of honor below an emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1164 and 1165 Béla followed Manuel I on his campaigns against Hungary which aimed at the occupation of Béla's "paternal inheritance", i.e. Croatia, Dalmatia and Syrmia (Szerémség). However, when king Stephen III transferred the
1 CONC  three provinces to the Manuel I, they were incorporated into the Byzantine Empire. In the autumn of 1165, Mánuel assigned officially his daughter and Béla as his heirs. In the beginning of 1166, Manuel I and Béla co-chaired the s
1 CONC ynod of the Byzantine Church in Constantinople.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Alexius was born as a son of Manuel and his second wife Maria of Antioch in 1166, Béla's engagement to Maria was cancelled. Béla was deprived also of his title, and he was granted the lower title of kaisar. But Manuel helped 
1 CONC negotiate another marriage for him, this time to Agnes of Antioch, who was the half-sister of Maria of Antioch; therefore by his marriage Manuel I and Béla became brothers-in-law.
1 CONT
1 CONT After their marriage Béla and his wife went on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem where he made a donation for the Knights Hospitaller.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Return to Hungary
1 CONT When his brother, king Stephen III died childless on 4 March 1172, Béla became his rightfull heir. However, some barons preferred his younger brother, Géza, who was followed also by their mother Euphrosyne. Béla concluded an agree
1 CONC ment with Emperor Manuel, who provided him a larger amount of money, while he promised that he would not attack the Byzantine Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Béla arrived to Hungary, Lukas, Archbishop of Esztergom denied to crown him, because he had presented the Archbishop's envoy, and the Archbishop qualified the gift as attempt in simony. Thus, Béla was obliged to seek assistan
1 CONC ce from pope Alexander III, who authorised the Archbishop of Kalocsa to crown Béla on 13 January, 1173.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his coronation Béla had his younger brother arrested, but Géza managed to escape and went to Austria. Henry II, Duke of Austria did not extradite Géza to Hungary; therefore Béla made an alliance with duke Sobeslav II of Bohe
1 CONC mia and they attacked Austria. Géza tried to fleed to the court of Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor but he was arrested by the Czechs who gave him to Béla. In 1178, Béla provided assistance to the duke of Bohemia to force back the 
1 CONC attacks of the duke of Austria.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Internal policy
1 CONT Béla wanted to amend the administration of his kingdom and ordened that all the issues, which were discussed by the king, had to be put down in writing. This order led to the establishment of the Royal Chancellery in Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1179, after Archbishop Andrew of Kalocsa had insulted him, Béla persuaded Lukas, Archbishop of Esztergom to excommunicate him and he occupied the estates of the Archdiocese. Shortly afterwards, on the intercession of pope Alexa
1 CONC nder III Béla was reconciled with Archbishop Andrew.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Expansion
1 CONT After the death of Emperor Manuel I on 24 September 1180, reoccupied Croatia, Dalmatia and Smyrnia from the Byzantine Empire. In the same year Zadar also accepted his supremacy against Venice. In 1184 Béla occupied Beograd (Nándor
1 CONC fehérvár), Branicevo (Barancs) and the valley of the River Morava from the Empire. In 1185, Béla made peace with the new Emperor, Isaac II Angelos, who married Béla's daughter, Margaret and received the territories of the River Mo
1 CONC rava as her dowry.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his first wife's death proposed marriage to Theodora Comnene, one of Emperor Manuel I's nieces, but the Byzantine Church prohibited their marriage. Thus, Béla married Marguerite of France, the sister of king Philip II of Fra
1 CONC nce and widow of Henry, the oldest son of king Henry II of England in 1186.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1188, prince Vladimir II of Halych, who had been dethronized by his boyars, sought refuge in his court. Béla lead his armies against Halych and occupied it, but afterwards he granted the principality to his second son Andrew an
1 CONC d had prince Vladimir arrested.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 31 May 1189, Béla welcomed Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor who was making his Crusade to the Holy Land, and on the emperor's request he set his brother, Géza free, who left for the Byzantine Empire. When afterwards Emperor Fred
1 CONC erick I entered into a controversy with Emperor Isaac II, Béla was mediating between them. However, in the meantime prince Vladimir II escaped from Hungary and reoccupied his principality.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the autumn of 1191, Béla had a meeting with his son-in-law, Emperor Isaac II, whom he helped against Stefan Nemanja, the ruler of the Serbs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Before his death he took an oath to lead a Crusade to the Holy Land.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages and children
1 CONT #1. c. 1170: Agnes of Antioch (1154 – c. 1184), daughter of Raynald of Châtillon, prince of Antioch and his wife, Constance of Antioch
1 CONT
1 CONT King Emeric of Hungary (1174 – 30 September/November 1204) 
1 CONT Margaret (1175 – after 1223), wife firstly of Emperor Isaac II Angelos, secondly of King Boniface I of Thessalonica and thirdly of Nicolas of Saint-Omer 
1 CONT King Andrew II of Hungary (c. 1177 – 21 September 1235) 
1 CONT Constance (c. 1180 – 6 December 1240), wife of king Ottokar I of Bohemia 
1 CONT #2. 1185/1186: Marguerite of France (1158 – after 10 September 1197), daughter of king Louis VII of France and his second wife, Constance of Castile
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Legacy
1 CONT He was a powerful ruler, and his court was counted among the most brilliant in Europe.
1 CONT
1 CONT His remains were confidently identified by archeologists during late 19th century excavations at the ruined cathedral of Székesfehérvár where the Árpád monarchs had been crowned and buried. Béla's exceptional height, as documented
1 CONC  by contemporary sources, rendered the identification certain. Based on the examination of his skeleton he must have been over two metres tall, a really outstanding height at that time. His remains were afterwards reinterred at th
1 CONC e Mathias Church in Budapest, with those of his first wife Agnes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Through his mother, Bela descended from Harold II of England (whose descendants had been dispossessed as a result of the Norman Conquest). Through his son, Andrew II, Béla was an ancestor of Edward III of England. As a result, all
1 CONC  subsequent English and British monarchs could claim descent from Harold II.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Kristó Gyula - Makk Ferenc: Az Árpád-ház uralkodói (IPC Könyvek, 1996) 
1 CONT Korai Magyar Történeti Lexikon (9-14. század), foszerkeszto: Kristó Gyula, szerkesztok: Engel Pál és Makk Ferenc (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1994) 
1 CONT Ostrogorski, Georgjie. History of the Byzantine State, 1986 
1 CONT Magyarország Történeti Kronológiája I. – A kezdetektol 1526-ig, foszerkeszto: Benda Kálmán (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1981) 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Stephen III King of Hungary
1 CONT 1172–1196 Succeeded by
1 CONT Emeric 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Stephen IV King of Croatia
1 CONT 1172–1196 Succeeded by
1 CONT Mirko 
