0 @N6000000004533233910@ NOTE Notes for Philip V Roi of France:
1 CONT
1 CONT "The Tall" ; King of Navarre
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip V (Philip the Tall), c. 1294-1322, king of France (1317-22),son of King Philip IV. He became regent in 1316 on the death of hisbrother Louis X, who was survived by his pregnant wife and infantdaughter. On the death of John 
1 CONC I (1316), the posthumous son of Louis,Philip took the crown for himself in the absence of a direct male heirand was crowned (1317) king. This helped to establish the Salic law inFrance, which excluded females from the royal succes
1 CONC sion. Philip'sreign was notable for his frequent consultations of nationalassemblies and for his administrative, judiciary, and militaryreforms. He was succeeded by his brother, Charles IV.
0 @N6000000005598936350@ NOTE Historians have styled Philip as striking handsome and he continued th e work of his father and older brother.  He received the town of Poiti ers and its banlieu as an apanage.  At the time of his older brother L ouis' death in 13
1 CONC 16, Philip was in the provinces and made all haste t o return to the court.  He initially declared himself as regent for hi s yet to be born nephew Jean, King of France at the time of the death of his brother Louis, and after the 
1 CONC death of Jean proclaimed himself K ing.  His ascendancy to the throne was not disputed by Charles of Valo is, his fathers brother.  Upon becoming King, Philip called a gatherin g of nobles and clergy for the purpose of making his 
1 CONC position tight an d secure.  He also tried to regain the ground lost to the various lord s and clergy during the short reign of his older brother Louis.  He gr anted the burghers the right to bear arms in certain situations that s
1 CONC  upported his goals to regain the lost properties.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1320, at the conclusion of the war against Flanders begun during th e reign of Philip IV, France acquired some of the former Flemish terri tory.  Philip frequently convoked the Estates-General to discuss monet ary problems. He 
1 CONC made administrative reforms & attempted to unify coin age, weights, and measures but encountered strong opposition in the Es tates-General.  He imposed heavy fines on the Jewish population.  He i s critized for his effort to destr
1 CONC oy and elimanate those with the gene rally feared disease of leprosy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip died without a male heir.  In 1307 he was married to Jeanne de Bourgogne (daughter and heiress of Otto IV, count of Burgundy) and the y had three daughters: 1.) Jeanne, 2.) Marguerite and 3.) Isabelle.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip V the Tall was King of France from 1317 to 1322, a member of th e Capetian dynasty.  The 1319/1320 Oridnance of Philip extended the de velopment of the French 'Parlement' as the judicial section of the Fre nch 'curis Regis,
1 CONC  originally initiated by Philip III in 1278.  He was the second son of King Philippe IV and Jeanne of Navarre. Philippe bec ame regent for his infant nephew King Jean I, and when Jean lived onl y a few days, he proclaimed himself 
1 CONC King. There was much speculation t hat Philippe was responsible for the infant king's demise.  In proclai ming himself King, he displaced the legitimate heiress (his niece Joan ) and had himself crowed at Rheims on 9 january 1317.
1 CONT
1 CONT They also had a son whose name was either Philip or Louis and who die d in 1317.  In 1320, Philippe V managed to expand his territory at th e expense of Flanders as a result of war. On domestic matters, King Ph ilippe attempted to
1 CONC  institute government reforms and worked to standar dize weights and measures.  King Philippe V died at Longchamp, Paris a nd is interred in Saint Denis Basilica.  Without a male heir, he was s ucceeded by his younger brother, Cha
1 CONC rles IV.
