0 @N6000000002319310809@ NOTE Reigned 1216-1272. A minor (9 years old)  when he took the throne he
1 CONT did not take the reigns      of Government himself until 1234. Baronian
1 CONT discontent simmered, boiling over     in 1258 when Henry facing
1 CONT financial disaster attempted to raise large sums      from his
1 CONT magnates. Reforms were agreed upon but then renouced by Henry. Simon
1 CONT de Montford lead a rebellion against the King (the Barons Wars) which
1 CONT was     defeated after initial success, thereafter Hnery ceeded much of
1 CONT his power to
1 CONT      his son. Burke say he was born 10 Oct 1206 and married 14 Jan,
1 CONT crowned 1216.
1 CONT
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry III (born 1207, ruled 1216-1272). The elder son of King John and
1 CONT grandson of Henry II was a weak and incompetent ruler. He was born in
1 CONT Winchester on Oct. 1, 1207. Until he came of age, in 1227, the
1 CONT government was in the hands of regents.
1 CONT    In 1236 Henry married Eleanor of Provence. His extravagance, his
1 CONT lavish gifts to favorites and to his wife's French relatives, and an
1 CONT unsuccessful war in France in 1242 caused mounting opposition to him.
1 CONT In 1258 a group of barons, led by Simon de Montfort, agreed to grant
1 CONT the king money only if he accepted the Provisions of Oxford, a body of
1 CONT reforms to be carried out by a commission of barons.
1 CONT    Henry repudiated the reform measures in 1261. In the Barons' War
1 CONT that followed, King Henry and his son Edward were captured at Lewes in
1 CONT 1264. Edward escaped and rescued his father. After the defeat and death
1 CONT of Simon in 1265, Henry was restored to the throne. Thereafter,
1 CONT however, the gifted and respected Edward was king in all but name.
1 CONT Henry died at Westminster, on Nov. 16, 1272 (see Edward, Kings of
1 CONT England; Montfort).
1 CONT
