1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT Edward was born in 1239. He was married twice. Firstly at a very young
1 CONT age to Eleanor of Castile, who died in 1290, and whom bore him the
1 CONT children: Eleanor, Joan, Henry, Julian, Joan  of Acre, Alphonso,
1 CONT Isabel, Margaret, Berengaria, Mary, Alice, Elizabeth, Edward, Beatrice
1 CONT and  Blanche. His second wife, Margaret of France, daughter of the King
1 CONT of France, bore him Thomas,  Edmund and Eleanor.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was a special child to his father. He was born very late in
1 CONT Henry's life. He was named  after the canon, Edward the Confessor, and
1 CONT although his title says Edward I, there were three  Edwards previous to
1 CONT him.    It was Henry who arranged for the important marriage of Edward
1 CONT to Eleanor, the half-sister of  Alfonso X, King of Castile and Leon. It
1 CONT was an arranged marriage which bore many children and
1 CONT  was full of love.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was made Overlord of Ireland, before he became King, and was
1 CONT responsible for Gascony  and Wales. He was a typically spoilt
1 CONT adolescent,and liked to spend his time setting up jousting
1 CONT tournaments, in which many lives would be lost at a time. However
1 CONT though he had once recognised  the justice of Simon de Montfort's
1 CONT stance against his father, he rallied to help his father.    It was his
1 CONT role as a general that helped quash De Montfort, and after this he
1 CONT became an exemplary
1 CONT  figure in the ruling of England. He was his father's Regent and
1 CONT succeeded  nchallenged to the  throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT  He did not become King until the age of 35 and was devoted to personal
1 CONT and political integrity. He  was a devoted ruler of England and
1 CONT developed state relations all around the world. He also fought  many
1 CONT wars and used a great deal of the funds of England in these battles. He
1 CONT borrowed heavily  from the Jews in England. They were expert currency
1 CONT manipulators.    The King had tried to involve them in more productive
1 CONT occupations, but against all local feeling, and  the lack of profit to
1 CONT be attained, the Jews went back to their old vocations. In 1290 Edward
1 CONT expelled
1 CONT  the Jews from England. Being unable to borrow money, Edward had to
1 CONT impose high taxes on the  local populations, which was of course highly
1 CONT unpopular.
1 CONT
1 CONT  After having defeated and slain the last Welsh Prince of Wales,
1 CONT Llywelyn ap Gruffydd, Edward  offered his baby son to the Welsh people
1 CONT as the Prince of Wales, in a symbolic gesture. The Welsh  gave the
1 CONT English knowledge of the long bow, one of the most valued weapons
1 CONT before gunpowder,  and Edward encouraged its use.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pacification of the Welsh was not instant, and it took years for
1 CONT the land and power to be  delegated in face of harsh resentment.
1 CONT
1 CONT  War with Scotland was causing great problems. It concerned an attempt
1 CONT to reconcile the centre of  Scotland from Edinburgh to Scone, the
1 CONT natural but unrecognised seat of Scotland. When Alexander  III, King of
1 CONT Scotland died, the crown passed to his three year old grand daughter,
1 CONT Margaret.  When Margaret was aged six, Edward arranged for her to be
1 CONT betrothed to his heir Edward, which  would have led to a peaceful union
1 CONT of England and Scotland. Margaret died in a ship wreck on the  way to
1 CONT her coronation in Scotland, then the succession to the crown was
1 CONT disputed. Edward stepped  in to arbitrate and with a balanced
1 CONT Commission took eighteen months to choose John Balliol.    This
1 CONT decision led to a revolt, which Edward managed to overcome. He declared
1 CONT himself King of  Scotland and carried the Coronation Stone of Scotland
1 CONT from Scone Palace off to England. It was  this that led to the revolt
1 CONT by William Wallace who was eventually defeated by Edward.
1 CONT
1 CONT  In 1306 Robert the Bruce was declared King of the Scots by the
1 CONT Scottish people and in the  ensuing war he was at first defeated by
1 CONT Edward. In a later campaign to crush Robert the Bruce  Edward died at
1 CONT Burgh by Sands, after being the monarch of England for 35 years.
0 @N6000000002176661743@ NOTE From the house of Plantagenet, Edward expelled the Jews from England in 1290,
1 CONT but the conquest of Scotland, however, became the ruling passion of his
1 CONT life. In 1305, he captured Sir William Wallace and executed him, leading to
1 CONT a Scottish revolt and the coronation of Robert de Bruce as King of Scotland.
1 CONT While on his way to subdue the Scots for the third time during his reign,
1 CONT Edward I died en route near Carlisle in 1307.
0 @N6000000001956401333@ NOTE
1 CONT --By name Edward Longshanks son of Henry III and king of England in 1272–1307, during a period of rising national consciousness. He strengthened the crown and Parliament against the old feudal nobility. He subdued Wales, destroyin
1 CONC g its autonomy; and he sought (unsuccessfully) the conquest of Scotland. His reign is particularly noted for administrative efficiency and legal reform. He introduced a series of statutes that did much to strengthen the crown in t
1 CONC he feudal hierarchy. His definition and emendation of English common law has earned him the name of the “English Justinian.”
1 CONT --Early life.
1 CONT Edward was the eldest son of King Henry III and Eleanor of Provence. In 1254 he was given the duchy of Gascony, the French Oléron, the Channel Islands, Ireland, Henry's lands in Wales, and the earldom of Chester, as well as severa
1 CONC l castles. Henry negotiated Edward's marriage with Eleanor, half sister of Alfonso X of Leon and Castile. Edward married Eleanor at Las Huelgas in Spain (October 1254) and then traveled to Bordeaux to organize his scattered appana
1 CONC ge. He now had his own household and officials, chancery and seal, with an exchequer (treasury) at Bristol Castle; though nominally governing all his lands, he merely enjoyed the revenues in Gascony and Ireland. He returned to Eng
1 CONC land in November 1255 and attacked Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, prince of Gwynedd, to whom his Welsh subjects had appealed for support when Edward attempted to introduce English administrative units in his Welsh lands. Edward, receiving 
1 CONC no help from either Henry or the marcher lords, was defeated ignominiously. His arrogant lawlessness and his close association with his greedy Poitevin uncles, who had accompanied his mother from France, increased Edward's unpopul
1 CONC arity among the English. But after the Poitevins were expelled, Edward fell under the influence of Simon de Montfort, his uncle by marriage, with whom he made a formal pact. Montfort was the leader of a baronial clique that was at
1 CONC tempting to curb the misgovernment of Henry.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward reluctantly accepted the Provisions of Oxford (1258), which gave effective government to the barons at the expense of the king. On the other hand, he intervened dramatically to support the radical Provisions of Westminster 
1 CONC (October 1259), which ordered the barons to accept reforms demanded by their tenants. In the dangerous crisis early in 1260 he supported Montfort and the extremists, though finally he desertedMontfort and was forgiven by Henry (Ma
1 CONC y 1260). He was sent to Gascony in October 1260 but returned early in 1263. Civil war had now broken out between Henry and the barons, who were supported by London. Edward's violent behaviour and his quarrel with the Londoners har
1 CONC med Henry's cause. At the Battle of Lewes (May 14, 1264) his vengeful pursuit of the Londoners early in the battle contributed to Henry's defeat. Edward surrendered and became a hostage in Montfort's hands. He escaped at Hereford 
1 CONC in May 1265 and took charge of the royalist forces, penned Montfort behind the River Severn, and, by lightning strategy, destroyed a large relieving army at Kenilworth (August 1). On August 4 he trapped and slew Montfort at Evesha
1 CONC m and rescued Henry. Shattered and enfeebled, Henry allowed Edward effective control of government, and the latter's extreme policy of vengeance, especially against the Londoners, revived and prolonged rebel resistance. Finally, t
1 CONC he papal legate Ottobuono, Edward's uncle Richard, Earl of Cornwall, and other moderates persuaded Henry to the milder policy of the Dictum of Kenilworth (Oct. 31, 1266), and after some delay the rebels surrendered. Edward took th
1 CONC e cross (1268), intending to join the French king Louis IX on a crusade to the Holy Land, but was delayed by lack of money until August 1270. Louis died before Edward's arrival; and Edward, after wintering in Sicily, went to Acre,
1 CONC  where he stayed from May 1271 to September 1272, winning fame by his energy and courage and narrowly escaping death by assassination but achieving no useful results. On his way home he learned in Sicily of Henry III's death on No
1 CONC v. 16, 1272.
1 CONT --Accession and character.
1 CONT Edward had nominated Walter Giffard, archbishop of York, Philip Basset, Roger Mortimer, and his trusted clerk Robert Burnell to safeguard his interests during his absence. After Henry's funeral, the English barons all swore fealty
1 CONC  to Edward (Nov. 20, 1272). His succession by hereditary right and the will of his magnates was proclaimed, and England welcomed the new reign peacefully, Burnell taking charge of the administration with his colleagues' support. T
1 CONC he quiet succession demonstrated England's unity only five years after a bitter civil war. Edward could journey homeward slowly, halting in Paris to do homage to his cousin Philip III for his French lands (July 26,1273), staying s
1 CONC everal months in Gascony and reaching Dover on Aug. 2, 1274, for his coronation at Westminster on August 19. Now 35 years old, Edward had redeemed a bad start. He had been arrogant, lawless, violent, treacherous, revengeful, and c
1 CONC ruel; his Angevin rages matched those of Henry II. Loving his own way and intolerant of opposition, he had still proved susceptible to influence by strong-minded associates. Hehad shown intense family affection, loyalty to friends
1 CONC , courage, brilliant military capacity, and a gift for leadership; handsome, tall, powerful, and tough, he had the qualities men admired. He loved efficient, strong government, enjoyed power, and had learned to admire justice, tho
1 CONC ugh in his own affairs it was often the letter, not the spirit of the law that he observed. Having mastered his anger, he had shown himself capable of patient negotiation, generosity, and even idealism; and he preferred the societ
1 CONC y and advice of strong counselors with good minds. As long as Burnell and Queen Eleanor lived, the better side of Edward triumphed, and the years until about 1294 were years of great achievement. Thereafter, his character deterior
1 CONC ated for lack of domestic comfort and independent advice. He allowed his autocratic temper full rein and devoted his failing energies to prosecution of the wars in France and against Scotland.
1 CONT --Parliament and statutes.
1 CONT Shrewdly realistic, Edward understood the value of the “parliaments,” which since 1254 had distinguished English government and which Montfort had deliberately employed to publicize government policy and to enlist widespread, acti
1 CONC ve support by summoning representatives of shires and boroughs to the council to decide important matters. Edward developed this practice swiftly, not to share royal power with his subjects but to strengthen royal authority with t
1 CONC he support of rising national consciousness. From 1275 to 1307 he summoned knights and burgesses to his parliaments in varying manners. The Parliament of 1295, which included representatives of shires, boroughs, and the lesser cle
1 CONC rgy, is usually styled the Model Parliament, but the pattern varied from assembly to assembly, as Edward decided. By 1307, Parliament, thus broadly constituted, had become the distinctive feature of English politics, though its po
1 CONC wers were still undefined and its organization embryonic.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward used these parliaments and other councils to enact measures of consolidation and reform in legal, procedural, and administrative matters of many kinds. The great statutes promulgated between 1275 and 1290 are the glory of h
1 CONC is reign. Conservative and definitory rather than original, they owed much to Burnell, Edward's chancellor. With the vast developments and reorganization of the administrative machine that Burnell coordinated, they created a new e
1 CONC ra in English government. The quo warranto inquiry, begun in 1275, the statutes of Gloucester (1278) and of Quo Warranto (1290) sought with much success to bring existing franchises under control and to prevent the unauthorized as
1 CONC sumption of new ones. Tenants were required to show “by what warrant” or right they held their franchises. Edward strove, unsuccessfully, to restore the feudal army and strengthen local government institutions by compelling minor 
1 CONC landowners to assume the duties of knighthood. His land legislation, especially the clause de donis conditionalibus in the miscellaneous Second Statute of Westminster (1285) and the statute Quia Emptores (Third Statute of Westmins
1 CONC ter, 1290), eventually helped to undermine feudalism, quite contrary to his purpose. By the Statute of Mortmain (1279) the crown gained control of the acquisition of land by ecclesiastical bodies. The Statute of Winchester (1285) 
1 CONC codified and strengthened the police system for preserving public order. The Statute of Acton Burnell (1283) and the Statute of Merchants (1285) showed practical concern for trade and merchants. These are but the most famous of ma
1 CONC ny statutes aimed at efficiency and sound administration.
1 CONT --Wars.
1 CONT Meanwhile, Edward destroyed the autonomous principality of Wales, which, under Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, had expanded to include all Welsh lordships and much territory recovered from the marcher lords. Domestic difficulties had compel
1 CONC led Henry III to recognize Llywelyn's gains by the Treaty of Shrewsbury (1267), but Edward was determined to reduce Llywelyn and used Llywelyn's persistent evasion of his duty to perform homage as a pretext for attack. He invaded 
1 CONC Wales by three coordinated advances with naval support (1277), blockaded Llywelyn in Snowdonia, starved him into submission, and stripped him of all his conquests since 1247. He then erected a tremendous ring of powerful castles e
1 CONC ncircling Gwynedd and reorganized the conquered districts as shires and hundreds. When English rule provoked rebellion, he methodically reconquered the principality, killing both Llywelyn (1282) and his brother David (1283). By th
1 CONC e Statute of Wales (1284) he completed the reorganization of the principality on English lines, leaving the Welsh marchers unaffected. A further Welsh rising in 1294–95 was ruthlessly crushed, and Wales remained supine for more th
1 CONC an 100 years.
1 CONT
1 CONT After 1294, matters deteriorated. Queen Eleanor had died in 1290, Burnell in 1292, and Edward never thereafter found such good advisers. The conquest and fortification of Wales had badly strained his finances; now endless wars wit
1 CONC h Scotland and France bankrupted him. He quarrelled bitterly with both clergy and barons, behaving as a rash and obstinate autocrat who refused to recognize his limitations. Philip III and Philip IV of France had both cheated him 
1 CONC of the contingent benefits promised by the Treaty of Paris (1259). By constant intervention on pretext of suzerainty they had nibbled at his Gascon borders and undermined the authority of his administration there. After doing homa
1 CONC ge toPhilip IV in 1286, Edward visited Gascony to reorganize the administration and restore authority. On returning to England in 1289 he had to dismiss many judges and officials for corruption and oppression during his absence. I
1 CONC n 1290, having systematically stripped the Jews of their remaining wealth, he expelled them from England. French intervention in Gascony was now intensified; affrays between English and French sailors inflamed feelings; and in 129
1 CONC 3 Philip IV tricked Edward's brother Edmund, earl ofLancaster, who was conducting negotiations, into ordering a supposedly formal and temporary surrender of the duchy, which Philip then refused to restore. The Welsh risingand Scot
1 CONC tish troubles prevented Edward from taking action, and when at last, in 1297, he sailed to attack France from Flanders, his barons refused to invade Gascony, and William Wallace's rising forced him to return. He made peace with Ph
1 CONC ilip (1299) and by Boniface VIII's persuasion married Philip's sister Margaret, and eventually recovered an attenuated Gascon duchy.
1 CONT
1 CONT For more than 100 years relations between England and Scotland had been amicable, and the border had been remarkably peaceful. Edward inaugurated 250 years of bitter hatred, savage warfare, and bloody border forays. The deaths of 
1 CONC Alexander III of Scotland (1286) and his granddaughter Margaret, the Maid of Norway (1290), whom Edward planned to marry to his heir, Edward of Caernarvon (afterward Edward II), ended the line of succession. Many dubious claimants
1 CONC  arose, and the Scottish magnates requested Edward's arbitration. Edward compelled the nobles and the claimants to recognize his suzerainty, and only then adjudged John de Balliol king (1292). Balliol did homage and was crowned, b
1 CONC ut Edward's insistence on effective jurisdiction, as suzerain, in Scottish cases eventually provoked the Scottish nobles to force Balliol to repudiate Edward's claims and to ally with France (1295). Edward invaded and conquered Sc
1 CONC otland (1296), removing to Westminster the coronation stoneof Scone. Wallace led a revolt in 1297, and Edward, though brilliantly victorious at Falkirk (July 22, 1298), could not subdue the rebellion despite prolonged campaigning 
1 CONC (1298–1303).
1 CONT --Last years.
1 CONT The strain of these years provoked heavy collisions between Edward and his magnates.He had quarrelled violently with his archbishops of Canterbury, John Peckham (1279–92) and Robert Winchelsey (1293–1313), over ecclesiastical libe
1 CONC rties and jurisdiction. In 1297 Winchelsey, obeying Pope Boniface VIII's bull Clericis Laicos (1296), rejected Edward's demands for taxes from the clergy, whereupon Edward outlawed the clergy. His barons now defied his orders to i
1 CONC nvade Gascony and, when Edward went to Flanders, compelled the regents to confirm the charters of liberties, with important additions forbidding arbitrary taxation (1297), thereby forcing Edward to abandon the campaign and eventua
1 CONC lly to make peace with France. Although Pope Clement V, more pliant than Boniface, allowed Edward to exile Winchelsey and intimidate the clergy (1306), the barons had exacted further concessions (1301) before reconciliation. Edwar
1 CONC d renewed the conquest of Scotland in 1303, captured Stirling in 1304, and executed Wallace as a traitor in 1305; but when Scotland seemed finally subjected, Robert I the Bruce revived rebellion and was crowned in 1306. On his way
1 CONC  toreconquer Scotland, Edward died near Carlisle.
0 @N6000000002240801662@ NOTE From the house of Plantagenet, Edward expelled the Jews from England in 1290,
1 CONT but the conquest of Scotland, however, became the ruling passion of his
1 CONT life. In 1305, he captured Sir William Wallace and executed him, leading to
1 CONT a Scottish revolt and the coronation of Robert de Bruce as King of Scotland.
1 CONT While on his way to subdue the Scots for the third time during his reign,
1 CONT Edward I died en route near Carlisle in 1307.
0 @N6000000002240749470@ NOTE Reigned 1272-1307
1 CONT In the Barons war 1264-67 he defeated the Barons at Evesham (1265) as
1 CONT King
1 CONT he is noted for encouraging Parliamentary institutions at the expense of
1 CONT feudalism and for subduing Wales on which he imposed the English system
1 CONT of
1 CONT administration. He later tried to assert his authority over Scotland
1 CONT and died
1 CONT while on his way to fight Robert Bruce.
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT Edward I (born 1239, ruled 1272-1307) was one of England's greatest
1 CONT kings. He was a handsome man, with fair hair and ruddy cheeks, and so
1 CONT tall that he was nicknamed Longshanks. He delighted in tournaments and
1 CONT hunting, but he was also practical and hardworking. For seven years
1 CONT before he came to the throne, he was the real ruler for his weak
1 CONT father, Henry III (see Henry, Kings of England; Montfort). He was in
1 CONT the Holy Land involved in the crusades when his father died, but there
1 CONT was no question that he would take the throne.
1 CONT    Edward has been called the "English Justinian" because, like the
1 CONT Roman emperor Justinian, he organized the laws. His laws were not
1 CONT restatements of existing customs but statutes in the modern sense. Many
1 CONT of them, particularly the land laws, had a long-lasting influence. A
1 CONT statute of 1285 limited church courts to strictly church matters a
1 CONT change that Edward's great-grandfather, Henry II, had been unable to
1 CONT make because of the murder of Thomas a Becket (see Becket; Henry, Kings
1 CONT of England). Edward also stopped paying a feudal tribute to the pope.
1 CONT    Parliament grew in strength during Edward's reign because he
1 CONT continued the policy of Simon de Montfort in summoning to it
1 CONT representatives of the towns and lesser knights. His parliament of 1295
1 CONT is known as the Model Parliament (see Parliament). In 1297 he
1 CONT reaffirmed the Magna Carta in the famous confirmation of the charters.
1 CONT All of Edward's moves were not fair and admirable ones, however; he
1 CONT forced Jews out of England in 1290.
1 CONT    Soon after coming to the throne, Edward conquered Wales and gave to
1 CONT his infant son, Edward, the title prince of Wales (see Wales). Until
1 CONT 1289 the care of his French possessions, principally Aquitaine, in
1 CONT Southern France, absorbed much of his attention. For the rest of his
1 CONT life, his main concern was Scotland.
1 CONT    He conquered the country in 1296; but in 1297 all Scotland rose in
1 CONT revolt against him under the popular leader, William Wallace. Edward
1 CONT defeated Wallace at Falkirk the next year, but the Scots still
1 CONT resisted. Near the end of Edward's reign Scotland found a new leader in
1 CONT Robert Bruce. In 1307 King Edward, then 70 years old, led an army
1 CONT toward Scotland but died before he reached the border.
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT Edward was born in 1239. He was married twice. Firstly at a very young
1 CONT age to Eleanor of Castile, who died in 1290, and whom bore him the
1 CONT children: Eleanor, Joan, Henry, Julian, Joan  of Acre, Alphonso,
1 CONT Isabel, Margaret, Berengaria, Mary, Alice, Elizabeth, Edward, Beatrice
1 CONT and  Blanche. His second wife, Margaret of France, daughter of the King
1 CONT of France, bore him Thomas,  Edmund and Eleanor.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was a special child to his father. He was born very late in
1 CONT Henry's life. He was named  after the canon, Edward the Confessor, and
1 CONT although his title says Edward I, there were three  Edwards previous to
1 CONT him.    It was Henry who arranged for the important marriage of Edward
1 CONT to Eleanor, the half-sister of  Alfonso X, King of Castile and Leon. It
1 CONT was an arranged marriage which bore many children and
1 CONT  was full of love.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was made Overlord of Ireland, before he became King, and was
1 CONT responsible for Gascony  and Wales. He was a typically spoilt
1 CONT adolescent,and liked to spend his time setting up jousting
1 CONT tournaments, in which many lives would be lost at a time. However
1 CONT though he had once recognised  the justice of Simon de Montfort's
1 CONT stance against his father, he rallied to help his father.    It was his
1 CONT role as a general that helped quash De Montfort, and after this he
1 CONT became an exemplary
1 CONT  figure in the ruling of England. He was his father's Regent and
1 CONT succeeded  nchallenged to the  throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT  He did not become King until the age of 35 and was devoted to personal
1 CONT and political integrity. He  was a devoted ruler of England and
1 CONT developed state relations all around the world. He also fought  many
1 CONT wars and used a great deal of the funds of England in these battles. He
1 CONT borrowed heavily  from the Jews in England. They were expert currency
1 CONT manipulators.    The King had tried to involve them in more productive
1 CONT occupations, but against all local feeling, and  the lack of profit to
1 CONT be attained, the Jews went back to their old vocations. In 1290 Edward
1 CONT expelled
1 CONT  the Jews from England. Being unable to borrow money, Edward had to
1 CONT impose high taxes on the  local populations, which was of course highly
1 CONT unpopular.
1 CONT
1 CONT  After having defeated and slain the last Welsh Prince of Wales,
1 CONT Llywelyn ap Gruffydd, Edward  offered his baby son to the Welsh people
1 CONT as the Prince of Wales, in a symbolic gesture. The Welsh  gave the
1 CONT English knowledge of the long bow, one of the most valued weapons
1 CONT before gunpowder,  and Edward encouraged its use.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pacification of the Welsh was not instant, and it took years for
1 CONT the land and power to be  delegated in face of harsh resentment.
1 CONT
1 CONT  War with Scotland was causing great problems. It concerned an attempt
1 CONT to reconcile the centre of  Scotland from Edinburgh to Scone, the
1 CONT natural but unrecognised seat of Scotland. When Alexander  III, King of
1 CONT Scotland died, the crown passed to his three year old grand daughter,
1 CONT Margaret.  When Margaret was aged six, Edward arranged for her to be
1 CONT betrothed to his heir Edward, which  would have led to a peaceful union
1 CONT of England and Scotland. Margaret died in a ship wreck on the  way to
1 CONT her coronation in Scotland, then the succession to the crown was
1 CONT disputed. Edward stepped  in to arbitrate and with a balanced
1 CONT Commission took eighteen months to choose John Balliol.    This
1 CONT decision led to a revolt, which Edward managed to overcome. He declared
1 CONT himself King of  Scotland and carried the Coronation Stone of Scotland
1 CONT from Scone Palace off to England. It was  this that led to the revolt
1 CONT by William Wallace who was eventually defeated by Edward.
1 CONT
1 CONT  In 1306 Robert the Bruce was declared King of the Scots by the
1 CONT Scottish people and in the  ensuing war he was at first defeated by
1 CONT Edward. In a later campaign to crush Robert the Bruce  Edward died at
1 CONT Burgh by Sands, after being the monarch of England for 35 years.
