0 @N6000000003658772974@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT BÉRENGÈRE (or BERENGUELA) OF CASTILE-LEÓN, born in 1204.  She married at Burgos in Castile in 1223 (as his 3rd wife) JEAN DE BRIENNE, Count of Brienne (as regent for his nephew), 1205-1221, King Consort of Jerusalem, 1210-1212, Ki
1 CONC ng-Regent of Jerusalem, 1212-1225, Emperor-Regent of Constantinople, son of Érard II de Brienne ... by Agnes. ... He was born 1169-74. They had three sons, Alphonse ..., Jean, and Louis, Knt. ... and one daughter, Marie. ... Jean 
1 CONC married (1st) at Tyre 14 Sept. 1210 MARIE DE MONFERRAT [[sic]], Queen of Jerusalem. ... Marie was born at Tyre in the summer of 1191.  They had one daughter, Isabel (wife of Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor).  He was elected King 
1 CONC of Jerusalem in 1210, in right of his wife, Marie, who died soon afterwards in 1212, leaving a daughter and heiress, in whose right he subsequently reigned.  He married (2nd) in 1213 STÉPHANIE OF ARMENIA, daughter and heiress of L
1 CONC eo II, King of Armenia.  They had one son, Jean.  Stéphanie died in 1219 during the siege of Damietta.  Jean was a legatee in the 1222 will of Philippe Auguste, King of France.  In 1223 he went to France and assisted in the corona
1 CONC tion of Louis VIII, King of France, at Paris.  The same year he also visited Henry III, King of England, and received rich gifts from the king and English nobles.  In 1228 he commanded the Papal troops that ravaged Emperor Frederi
1 CONC ck's lands in southern Italy.  He was elected Latin Emperor of Constantinople in 1229, as colleague for the minor, Baldwin II.  He was crowned Emperor in the Cathedral of St. Sophia in 1231.  JEAN I (DE BRIENNE), Emperor-Regent of
1 CONC  Constantinople, died at Constantinople 21 March 1237.  His widow, Bérengère, died 12 April 1237.
0 @N6000000003949751254@ NOTE John, byname JOHN OF BRIENNE, French JEAN DE BRIENNE (b. c. 1148--d. March 1237,  Constantinople), count of Brienne who became titular king of Jerusalem (1210-29) and Latin emperor of Constantinople (1231-37).
1 CONT A penniless younger son of the French count Erard II of Brienne and Agnes of Montbéliard, John passed most of his life as a minor noble until befriended by King Philip II Augustus of France, who arranged for him to marry Mary (Mar
1 CONC ie) of Montferrat, queen of the crusader state of Jerusalem, in 1210. John reached the Palestinian town of Acre on Sept. 13, 1210, married Mary the following day, and was crowned at Tyre on October 3. Mary died in 1212, and John w
1 CONC as named regent for their infant daughter, Yolande de Brienne, who inherited the crown as Isabella II. In 1214 John married Princess Stephanie of Armenia, daughter of the Armenian king Leo II, and later had a son by her.
1 CONT As regent, John arranged a five-year truce with al-Malik al-'Adil, sultan of Egypt and Syria, in July 1212, and during the truce he persuaded Pope Innocent III to launch the Fifth Crusade in support of his daughter's kingdom. In 1
1 CONC 218 he joined the crusading force from the West in an expedition against the Egyptian port of Damietta. After quarrelling with the crusade leader, the cardinal legate Pelagius, John left Egypt in February 1220, returning in July 1
1 CONC 221 to witness the humiliating defeat of the crusaders and the abandonment of the siege of Damietta.
1 CONT Stephanie died in 1219; John then married Berengaria, daughter of Ferdinand III of Castile, and in 1225 gave his daughter Isabella in marriage to the Holy Roman emperor Frederick II, trying to retain his rights as regent of the ki
1 CONC ngdom of Jerusalem. Immediately following the marriage, however, Frederick began to contest these rights.
1 CONT In 1228 John was invited to Constantinople to be regent and co-emperor with the young Baldwin II and arranged a match between Baldwin and his four-year-old daughter by Berengaria. Crowned in 1231, John helped fend off attacks by t
1 CONC he Bulgarian tsar Ivan Asen II and the Nicaean emperor John III Vatatzes, but shortly before his death he was forced to appeal to the West for help. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97]
