1 CONT authority the Provinces held by the Albigences in the south of France. On his
1 CONT death, his son Louis IX, not yet 13 years old, was taken to Reims by his
1 CONT mother to be crowned King of France.  Mother Blanche was not a woman to be
1 CONT discouraged  by adversity, such as was shown at the coronation by a lack of
1 CONT participation by many of the most powerful nobles.
0 @N6000000003949665092@ NOTE Crusader against the Albigensians    At the age of twelve Louis married the formidable Blanche of Castile and they had ten children. In 1212 he took Saint Omer and Aire as he regarded Flanders too powerful and too near his own cou
1 CONC nty of Artois. In 1216 the English King John offered him the English throne in exchange for support against the powerful English barons. Louis went to England to support the rebels but, after initial success, was defeated at sea. 
1 CONC In 1217 peace was signed and Louis was secretly paid 10,000 marks.
1 CONT In 1223 he succeeded his father as king of France, and in 1224 seized Poitou. In 1226 he launched a successful crusade against the Albigensian heretics, capturing the major fortress of Avignon before returning towards Paris becaus
1 CONC e of illness. He died at Montpensier shortly afterwards. 
0 @N6000000004533182921@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Louis VIII the Lion (French: Louis VIII le Lion) (September 5, 1187 -November 8, 1226) reigned as King of France from 1223 to 1226. He wasa member of the Capetian dynasty. Louis was born in Paris, France, theson of King Philippe I
1 CONC I Auguste and Isabelle of Hainaut.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a prince, Louis won battles against the armies of King John ofEngland. In 1216 the English barons rebelled against the veryunpopular King John and offered the throne to Louis. In May of 1216,Prince Louis of France and his army 
1 CONC invaded England in what has becomeknown as the First Barons' War, but after a year and a half of war,Louis had to give up on his desire to become the King of England andsigned the Treaty of Lambeth.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis succeeded his father on July 14, 1223; his coronation took placeon August 6 of the same year in the cathedral at Reims. As king, hecontinued to seek revenge on the Angevins and seized Poitou andSaintonge from them in 1224. T
1 CONC here followed the seizure of Avignon andLanguedoc.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 1 November 1223, he issued an ordinance that prohibited hisofficials from recording debts owed to Jews, thus reversing thepolicies set by his father King Philippe II Auguste. Usuary (debt withinterest) was illegal for Christian
1 CONC s to practice, according to Churchlaw it was seen as a vice to profit from others misfortune (likegambling), and was punishable by excommunication, a severe punishment.However since Jews were not Christian, they could not beexcomm
1 CONC unicated, and thus fell in to a legal gray area which secularrulers would sometimes exploit by allowing (or requesting) Jews toprovide usuary services, often for personal gain to the secular ruler,and to the discontent of the Chur
1 CONC ch. Louis' prohibition was oneattempt at resolving this legal problem which was a constant source offriction in Church and State courts. Twenty six barons accepted, butThibaut IV, a powerful baron from Champagne did not, since he 
1 CONC had anagreement with the Jews that guaranteed him extra income throughtaxation. Thibaut IV would become a major opposition force to capetiandominance, and his hostility was manifest during the reign of LouisVIII. For example, duri
1 CONC ng the siege of Avignon, he peformed only themiinimum service of 40 days, and left home amid charges of treachery.
1 CONT
1 CONT While returning to Paris, King Louis became ill with dysentery, anddied on November 8, 1226 in the chateau at Montpensier, Auvergne.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Saint Denis Basilica houses the tomb of Louis VIII. His son, LouisIX, succeeded him on the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage On May 23, 1200, at the age of twelve, Louis married Blancheof Castile (March 4, 1188 - November 26, 1252).
1 CONT
1 CONT Issue:
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippe - (September 9, 1209 - 1218)
1 CONT Louis IX - (April 25, 1214 - August 25, 1270)
1 CONT Robert - (September 25, 1216 - February 9, 1250)
1 CONT Jean - (July 21, 1219 - 1232)
1 CONT Alphonse of Toulouse - (November 11, 1220 - August 21, 1271)
1 CONT Philippe Dagobert - (February 20, 1222 - 1232)
1 CONT Isabelle - (June 1225 - February 23, 1269)
1 CONT Etienne - (born and died 1226)
1 CONT Charles I of Sicily - (March 1227 - January 7, 1285)
