0 @N6000000005598936230@ NOTE 1233-6
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.dcs.hull.ac.uk/cgi-bin/gedlkup/n=royal?royal01741
1 CONT
1 CONT [On his succession to the crown, Louis commanded King Henry III,ofEngland to attend his coronation, as a vassal, but instead, Henry sent todemand restitution of allt he provinces taken from his father byPhilippe-Auguste (KingPhili
1 CONC ppe II).  This was sufficient to rekindlehostilities.  Prompted by Pope Honorius III, he commenced the second waragainst the Albigenses, 1226, and received their submission, afteroverpowering Abignon.]
1 CONT Louis is described as short, thin and pale faced.  He had a placid tem per and led a chaste life.  As a child, Louis was not robust and had c ritical bout with disentery in 1191.  He carried on the work of his fa ther, but lacked 
1 CONC the stamina of his father.  Though he left the reputa tion of a saint,he was also a warrior prince.  He fought King John of England and Ferrand of Flander, defeating both in battle.  In mid 1214 , Louis broke north from the area o
1 CONC f Poitou and challenged the positio n of King John.  His force included 1,200 men-at-arms, 2,000 light hor se and 7,000 foot.  In 1215, Louis laid claim to the English throne a s a result of his marriage to Blanche of Castile.  At
1 CONC  the time he was styled as a 'cold colourless - capable soldier with a streak of callou sness'.  In November of 1215, Louis led a contingent of knights up th e Thames. Within a few months he came close to taking London, in asser t
1 CONC ion of his fictional claim to the English crown.  Louis maintained th at since he was no part of the truce between his father Phillippe and John, that he was still at war with King John whom he considered to b e a usuper.  On 20 M
1 CONC ay 1216, Louis set sail for England and was sighte d off the coast of Thanet in East Kent on the 21st of May.  His fleet numbered between 6 and 700 vessels commanded by Eustace the Monk - a w ell-to-do adventurer.  They made landi
1 CONC ng,  at Thanet on 22 March 1216 .  Louis entered London on 2 June 1216, where he was received at St. P auls.  He received homage from the mayor of London as well as Robert F itzWalter and then made for Winchester where John was he
1 CONC ld up.  He ent ered Winchester on 14 June 1216 having already taken Reigate castle i n Surrey, Guildford castle and Farnham.  Louis did not take the ruse o f John and stayed in the area of Winchester where he then turned back towa
1 CONC rds Oldham.  Louis granted Marlborough castle to Robert de Dreux .  During the negotiations during 1217 for the withdrawal from England , Louis promised to interced with his father Phillippe II in regard t o the restoration of Hen
1 CONC ry II's Angevin Continental fiefs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Throughout his reign as King of France, he was absorbed by two aims, t o destroy the power of the Plantagenets in England and to conquer sout hern France.  After the death of his father, Louis retained all the co unselors of his f
1 CONC ather.   Invited by English lords in rebellion agains t their king, Louis invaded England in 1216, although his action cause d his excommunication by Pope Innocent III. The death of John and the accession of Henry III as King of E
1 CONC ngland lost Louis much support amon g the English nobility. After his defeat (1217) at Lincoln, he withdre w. In June of 1224, Louis bought the castle of Montreuil from Guillaum e de Masnieres.  In 1224 he conquered Poitou from th
1 CONC e English. To mak e his peace with the church, he pledged to go on crusade, and in 1226 he resumed the Albigensian Crusade and conquered most of Languedoc.  H e also established royal power in the area of Poitou. He continued hi s
