0 @N6000000002187755596@ NOTE Louis VIII of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis VIII the Lion (French: Louis VIII le Lion) (September 5, 1187 – November 8, 1226) reigned as King of France from 1223 to 1226. He was a member of the Capetian dynasty. Louis was born in Paris, France, the son of King Philipp
1 CONC e II Auguste and Isabelle of Hainaut.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1216 the English barons rebelled in the First Barons' War against the unpopular King John and offered the throne to Louis. Louis invaded and was proclaimed king in London in May 1216, although he was not crowned. There was litt
1 CONC le resistance when the prince entered London. At St Paul's Cathedral, Louis was accepted as ruler with great pomp and celebration in the presence of all of London. Many nobles, including the King of Scotland, gathered to give homa
1 CONC ge to him. On June 14 he captured Winchester and soon conquered over half of the English kingdom. Since other English kings such as Edward V and Edward VIII were not crowned but only proclaimed, there is a good case for including 
1 CONC Louis in the list of Kings of England. After a year and a half of war, most of the rebellious barons had defected and so Louis had to give up his claim to be the King of England by signing the Treaty of Lambeth in 1217.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Coronation of Louis VIII and Blanche of Castile at Reims in 1223; a miniature from the Grandes Chroniques de France, painted in the 1450s, kept at the National Library of FranceLouis succeeded his father on July 14, 1223; his coro
1 CONC nation took place on August 6 of the same year in the cathedral at Reims. As king, he continued to seek revenge on the Angevins and seized Poitou and Saintonge from them in 1224. There followed the seizure of Avignon and Languedoc
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT On 1 November 1223, he issued an ordinance that prohibited his officials from recording debts owed to Jews, thus reversing the policies set by his father King Philippe II Auguste. Usury (lending money with interest) was illegal fo
1 CONC r Christians to practice, according to Church law it was seen as a vice in which people profited from others' misfortune (like gambling), and was punishable by excommunication, a severe punishment. However since Jews were not Chri
1 CONC stian, they could not be excommunicated, and thus fell in to a legal gray area which secular rulers would sometimes exploit by allowing (or requesting) Jews to provide usuary services, often for personal gain to the secular ruler,
1 CONC  and to the discontent of the Church. Louis' prohibition was one attempt at resolving this legal problem which was a constant source of friction in Church and State courts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Twenty six barons accepted, but Thibaut IV, a powerful baron from Champagne did not, since he had an agreement with the Jews that guaranteed him extra income through taxation. Thibaut IV would become a major opposition force to Ca
1 CONC petian dominance, and his hostility was manifest during the reign of Louis VIII. For example, during the siege of Avignon, he peformed only the minimum service of 40 days, and left home amid charges of treachery.
1 CONT
1 CONT While returning to Paris, King Louis became ill with dysentery, and died on November 8, 1226 in the chateau at Montpensier, Auvergne.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Saint Denis Basilica houses the tomb of Louis VIII. His son, Louis IX, succeeded him on the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Marriage
1 CONT On May 23, 1200, at the age of twelve, Louis married Blanche of Castile (March 4, 1188 – November 26, 1252).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Issue
1 CONT Philippe (September 9, 1209 – 1218) 
1 CONT Louis IX (April 25, 1214 – August 25, 1270) 
1 CONT Robert (September 25, 1216 – February 9, 1250) 
1 CONT Jean (July 21, 1219 – 1232) 
1 CONT Alphonse of Toulouse (November 11, 1220 – August 21, 1271) 
1 CONT Philippe Dagobert (February 20, 1222 – 1232) 
1 CONT Isabel (June 1225 – February 23, 1269) 
1 CONT Etienne (born and died 1226) 
1 CONT Charles I of Sicily (March 1227 – January 7, 1285) 
