0 @N6000000002187840220@ NOTE Margaret of Burgundy, Queen of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret of Burgundy, (French: Marguerite de Bourgogne) (1290 – August 14, 1315), was the first queen consort of King Louis X of France and Navarre.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite was a princess of the ducal House of Burgundy, a branch of the Capetian dynasty. She was the eldest daughter of duke Robert II of Burgundy (1248-1306) and Agnes of France (1260-1327), the youngest daughter of Louis IX o
1 CONC f France and Marguerite Berenger of Provence.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1305, Marguerite married her cousin once removed, Louis of France, King of Navarre, who in 1314 acceded to the French throne as Louis X. They had one daughter, Jeanne (born 1311).
1 CONT
1 CONT Early in 1314, Marguerite was allegedly caught in adultery, her sister-in-law Isabella of France being a witness against her, and was imprisoned for the last two years of her life. Imprisoned along with her was her sister-in-law B
1 CONC lanche of Burgundy. Marguerite was allegedly strangled on her husband's orders, in order to allow him to remarry. Marguerite is portrayed in La Reine Étranglée, a novel in the famous Les Rois Maudits ("The Accursed Kings") series 
1 CONC of historical novels by Maurice Druon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite's daughter, Jeanne, later became Queen regnant of Navarre as Jeanne II of Navarre (1311-1349). Her paternity was under doubts of bastardy because of her mother's adultery. However Jeanne was Marguerite's undoubted daugh
1 CONC ter and thus a full potential heir to Burgundy. She was also a granddaughter of Louis IX of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite de Bourgogne was a sister of:
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke Hugh V of Burgundy (c. 1294-1315) 
1 CONT Duke Eudes IV of Burgundy (c. 1295-1349) 
1 CONT Joan the Lame, Queen consort of Philip VI of France. 
1 CONT In 1361, Marguerite's rights of ainesse became important in the premature death of Duke Philip I of Burgundy (her grandnephew), since the closest Burgundian heirs were descendants of Marguerite and of her sister, Joan the Lame. Ma
1 CONC rguerite's grandson and heir Charles II of Navarre claimed the duchy on the basis of primogeniture, but Joan the Lame's son John II of France on the basis of proximity, being one generation closer to the Burgundian dukes. The case
1 CONC  was ruled in favour of John, who became Duke of Burgundy, later bestowing the Duchy upon his son, Philip the Bold.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Philip of France Queen consort of Navarre
1 CONT 1305–1315 Succeeded by
1 CONT Clemence d'Anjou 
