1 CONT Gaunt's three wives in history books for certain, but it would appear the
1 CONT most likely would be the first wife.
0 @N6000000002188376349@ NOTE Katherine of Lancaster
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Katherine of Lancaster (also known as Katherine Plantagenet; 1372-1418) was the daughter of John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster, and his second wife, Constance of Castile (the daughter, and rightful heir of King Peter of Castile,
1 CONC  who died at the hands of his half brother Henry II). She was born in Hertford Castle, her father's chief country home, on March 31, 1373.[1] Katherine became Queen of Castile through her marriage to Henry III.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Marriage
1 CONT     * 2 Widowhood
1 CONT           o 2.1 Regency with Ferdinand
1 CONT           o 2.2 Solo regency
1 CONT     * 3 Death and burial
1 CONT     * 4 Ancestors
1 CONT           o 4.1 Coat of Arms
1 CONT     * 5 Notes
1 CONT     * 6 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage
1 CONT
1 CONT After King John I of Portugal defeated King John I of Castile at the battle of Aljubarota, South Leiria, in 1385, fully establishing Portuguese independence, Katherine's parents, the Duke and Duchess of Lancaster, were encouraged 
1 CONC to press their claim for the Castilian throne. [2] In 1386, Katherine joined her father and mother in an expedition to Castile to gain John of Gaunt's rights to the throne. England and Portugal entered into an alliance against Cas
1 CONC tile in 1386 and solidified their ties through the marriage of King John I and Katherine's half-sister, Philippa. [3]
1 CONT
1 CONT John of Gaunt had ruled Santiago de Compostela, Vigo, and Pontevedra with ease, but in 1387 had to withdraw to Portugal because of an unsuccessful invasion of Leon. It was then that he accepted the proposal of King John I of Casti
1 CONC le, to marry Katherine to his son, the future Henry III, and that Constance, Duchess of Lancaster should renounce all claims to the Castilian throne. A final treaty in regards to this proposal was ratified at Bayonne in Gascony on
1 CONC  July 8, 1388. The marriage helped to restore a semblance of legitimacy to the Trastamara line. Furthermore, together with the truce of Leulingham and the one made at Moncao, the betrothal helped to end the Spanish period of the H
1 CONC undred Years War [4] .
1 CONT
1 CONT On August 5, 1388, Katherine announced that she entered into the marriage freely and fully accepted the treaty. The treaty had included a dower of the towns of Soria, Almazan, Atienza, Deza, and Molina. By 17 September 1388 Kather
1 CONC ine was married to the nine-year-old Henry in Palencia Cathedral. Her husband took over the throne after the death of his father in 1390, but only in 1393 he was declared of age and began to rule. Katherine's only contribution to 
1 CONC Henry's rule was the bearing of his three children and her devotion to the religious patronage of the Dominican Order. In September of 1390 Katherine accepted the authority of the Avignon Papacy, under Antipope Clement VII and bec
1 CONC ame a staunch supporter.
1 CONT
1 CONT The couple's three children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Maria of Castile (1401-1458), who married Alfonso V "the Magnanimous", King of Aragon and Naples,
1 CONT     * Catharina of Castile (1403-1439), who wed Enrique of Aragón, Duque de Villena,
1 CONT     * Juan II (1405-1454), who succeeded his father as King of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Widowhood
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Regency with Ferdinand
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry III died in 1406, and according to his will, his widow, Katherine, and his brother, Ferdinand I of Aragon were to be joint regents during John II's minority, sharing their power with a royal council. Of those three parties, 
1 CONC Ferdinand was to be the one with the greatest share of power [5]. However, the custody of Juan II was given to two nobles, Diego López de Stúñiga and Juan Fernandez de Velasco. Katherine prepared to defend herself and her househol
1 CONC d in a famous Spanish castle, the Alcázar of Segovia, due to the fact that she was not willing to relinquish her year-old son. Ferdinand was eventually able to make a deal that allowed Katherine to maintain custody of her son.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand ordered Mudéjars (peasants, carpenters, seaman, and shipbuilders) to wear a symbol; a blue moon on their clothing. They were not allowed to leave their homes, nor were they allowed to work or trade with Christians. The J
1 CONC ewish, too were not allowed to work or trade with Christians. This was an attempt by John II to suppress religious minorities, which was supported by Katherine and only lasted until her death. Furthermore, tensions between the reg
1 CONC ents led to a division of rule. The royal council awarded Katherine control over the Northern part of the Kingdoms of Castile, and Leon.
1 CONT
1 CONT As Katherine became increasingly involved in the wars of Ferdinand against Granada in the south, Castile's alliance with France suffered and she was able to strengthen her relations with Portugal, where her half-sister Philippa wa
1 CONC s queen, and with England, where her half-brother Henry IV ruled since 1399. Katherine and her half-brother fostered the trade between Castile and England. Her international policies were beneficial to the Castilian communities, b
1 CONC ut her co-regents did not always act in their best interests. Because of Katherine's opposition to Ferdinand, she supported the position of Antipope Benedict XIII and initially spoke up against the Council of Constance (1414-1418)
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Solo regency
1 CONT
1 CONT When Ferdinand died in 1416, Katherine's authority was reduced, due to the fact that his rivals no longer supported her. The government became very conciliar. Katherine, sickly due to a stroke, relinquished the custody of her son.
1 CONT
1 CONT There is one vivid account of Katherine towards the end of her life recorded by Fernán Pérez de Guzmán. It alludes to the fact that she probably inherited physical characteristics from her father, and that she was a sickly woman. 
1 CONC He describes her as being very tall and fat, pink with white in her complexion and fair. He states that she moved as though she was a man. He also says that she was virtuous and reserved, in both her person and her reputation. She
1 CONC  was said to be generous and magnificent in her ways, although she did play "favourites" and was greatly influenced by them. Despite her "favouritism", she was twice as likely to banish women from her household.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death and burial
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen Katherine died at Valladolid on June 2, 1418, of a stroke, possibly precipitated by her great obesity, leaving her thirteen year-old son at the mercy of self-interested courtiers. She is buried with her husband in the Capill
1 CONC a de los Reyes Nuevos in Cathedral of Toledo. Her monumental effigy shows her with a long face and a highly arched forehead. She is the namesake of her great granddaughter Katherine of Aragon, first of the six wives of Henry VIII 
1 CONC of England.
1 CONT
