0 @N6000000002188378545@ NOTE Leonor of Aragon (1402–1445)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor of Aragon (c. 1402 – 19 February 1445) was the daughter of Ferdinand I of Aragon and Eleanor of Alburquerque.
1 CONT
1 CONT She married Duarte (Edward), King of Portugal on 22 September 1428. They had nine children, of which five survived to adulthood.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Issue
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Prince John, October 1429 – 14 August 1433.
1 CONT     * Princess Philippa 27 November 1430 – 24 March 1439.
1 CONT     * Afonso V of Portugal 15 January 1432 – 28 August 1481; who succeeded Duarte as 12th King of Portugal.
1 CONT     * Princess Maria, 7 December 1432 – 8 December 1432.
1 CONT     * Prince Ferdinand, 17 November 1433 – 18 September 1470; Duke of Viseu and father of future king Manuel I of Portugal.
1 CONT     * Princess Leonor, 18 September 1434 – 3 September 1467; married Frederick III, Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT     * Prince Edward, 12 July 1435 – 12 July 1435.
1 CONT     * Princess Catherine, 26 November 1436 – 17 June 1463.
1 CONT     * Princess Joana, 31 March 1439 – 13 June 1475; married King Henry IV of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Regent
1 CONT
1 CONT When her husband died on 13 September 1438, she was appointed as regent of Portugal in his will. However, she was inexperienced and, as an Aragonese, unpopular with the people, who preferred the late king's brother Pedro, Duke of 
1 CONC Coimbra. Negotiations for a compromise arrangement were drawn out over several months, but were complicated by the interference of the Count of Barcelos and the Archbishop of Lisbon, as also by her giving birth to a posthumous dau
1 CONC ghter in March 1439, and by the death of her eldest daughter, Philippa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eventually, the Cortes appointed Pedro the sole regent. Eleanor continued conspiring, but was forced to go into exile in Castile in December 1440. She died at Toledo in February 1445 and is buried in Batalha, Portugal. 
