0 @N6000000002187855331@ NOTE Robert III of Flanders
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert III of Flanders (1249 – September 17, 1322), also called Robert of Bethune and nicknamed The Lion of Flanders was Count of Nevers 1273-1322 and Count of Flanders 1305–1322.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert was the oldest son of Guy of Dampierre from his first marriage with Mathilda of Bethune. His father essentially gave up the rule of Flanders to him in November 1299, during his war with Philip IV of France. Both father and 
1 CONC son were taken into captivity in May 1300, and Robert was not released until 1305.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert married twice. His first wife was Blanche (d. 1269), daughter of Charles I of Sicily, in 1265. They had one son, Charles, who died young. His second wife was Yolande (d. June 11, 1280), Countess of Nevers, daughter of Eudes
1 CONC  of Burgundy, in c. 1271. They had five children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Louis (d. July 24, 1322, Paris), Count of Nevers, married December 1290 Jeanne of Rethel (d. aft. March 12, 1328), Countess of Rethel
1 CONT    2. Robert (d. 1331), Count of Marle, married c. 1323 Jean of Brittany (1296 – March 24, 1363), Lady of Nogent-le-Rotrou.
1 CONT    3. Jeanne (d. October 15, 1333), married 1288 Enguerrand IV de Coucy (d. 1310), Viscount of Meaux
1 CONT    4. Yolande (d. 1313), married c. 1287 Walter II of Enghien (d. 1309)
1 CONT    5. Matilda, married c. 1314 Matthias of Lorraine (d. c. 1330), Lord of Warsberg
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert of Bethune gained military fame in Italy, when he fought at the side of his father in law, Charles I of Sicily (1265-1268) against the last Hohenstaufens, Manfred and Konradin. Together with his father he took part in 1270 
1 CONC in the Eighth Crusade, led by Saint Louis. After his return form the Crusade he continued to be a loyal aid for his father, politically and military, in the fight against the attempts of the French King Philip IV the Fair to add F
1 CONC landers to the French crown lands.
1 CONT
1 CONT Guy of Dampierre broke all feudal bonds with the French king (January 20, 1297) mainly under his influence. When the resistance seemed hopeless Robert allowed himself to be taken prisoner, together with his father and his brother 
1 CONC William of Crèvecoeur, and taken to the French King (May 1300). Shortly before that he had become the de facto ruler of Flanders. He was locked in the castle of Chinon. Contrary to popular belief, and the romantic portrayal by Hen
1 CONC drik Conscience in his novel about these events (The Lion of Flanders), he did not take part in the Battle of the Golden Spurs.
1 CONT
1 CONT In July 1305, after his father had died in captivity, he was allowed to return to his county. The execution of the Treaty of Athis-sur-Orge would mark the rule of Count Robert. Initially he achieved some success in moving the coun
1 CONC tryside and the cities to fulfill their duties. However, in April 1310 he started to radically resist the French, with support of his subjects and his family. Both diplomatically and military he managed to make a stance against th
1 CONC e French King. When he marched to Lille in 1319 the militia from Ghent refused to cross the Leie with him. When his grandson Louis I of Nevers pressured him as well, Robert gave up the battle and went to Paris in 1320 to restore f
1 CONC eudal bonds with the French King.
1 CONT
1 CONT But even after that, he would hamper the execution of the Treaty of Athis-sur-Orge. Robert died in 1322 and was succeeded by his grandson, Louis, Count of Nevers and Rethel.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was buried in Flanders in the Sint-Martin's Cathedral in Ieper, as was his explicit wish. His body was only allowed to be transferred to the abby of Flines (near Dowaai) when Lille and Dowaai were again part of the County of Fl
1 CONC anders. His first wife and his father were also buried in this abby.
1 CONT
1 CONT Within Flemish minded circles, Robert of Bethune, alias The Lion of Flandres (De Leeuw van Vlaanderen), is often seen as a symbol of Flemish pride and freedom. This was in particular due to the romantic, albeit somewhat incorrect,
1 CONC  portrayal of him by Hendrik Conscience. In reality The Lion of Flanders probably did not even speak Dutch.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Count Robert III of Flanders
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Guy Count of Flanders
1 CONT 1305–1322 Succeeded by
1 CONT Louis I
0 @N6000000005076727367@ NOTE Comte de Flandre
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert III de Dampierre, Comte de Flandre was born in 1249.1 He was the son of Guy de Dampierre, Comte de Flandre and Matilda de Bethune. He married Yolande de Bourgogne, daughter of Eudes de Bourgogne, Comte de Nevers and Mathild
1 CONC a de Bourbon, in 1272.1 He married Blanche d'Anjou, daughter of Charles I Stephen d'Anjou, King of Naples and Sicily and Beatrice, Comtesse de Provence, in 1265. He died in 1322.1
1 CONT      Robert III de Dampierre, Comte de Flandre gained the title of Comte de Flandre in 1305.1
1 CONT Child of Robert III de Dampierre, Comte de Flandre and Yolande de Bourgogne
1 CONT Louis de Flandre, Comte de Rethel+ d. 13221
1 CONT Children of Robert III de Dampierre, Comte de Flandre and Blanche d'Anjou
1 CONT Charles de Flandre
1 CONT Matilda de Flandre
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 74. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
0 @N6000000002187757300@ NOTE Philip of Courtenay
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip I of Courtenay (1243, Constantinople – 1283, Viterbo) was titular Emperor of Constantinople 1273–1283. He was the son of Baldwin II of Constantinople and Marie of Brienne.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his youth, his father was forced to mortgage him to Venetian merchants to raise money for the support of his empire, which was lost to the Empire of Nicaea in 1261.
1 CONT
1 CONT By the Treaty of Viterbo in 1267, his father agreed to marry him to Beatrice, daughter of Charles I of Sicily and Beatrice of Provence. Her maternal grandparents were Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence and Beatrice of Savoy.
1 CONT
1 CONT The marriage was performed in October 1273 at Foggia; shortly thereafter, Baldwin died, and Philip inherited his claims on Constantinople. Although Philip was recognized as emperor by the Latin possessions in Greece, much of the a
1 CONC ctual authority devolved on the Angevin kings of Naples and Sicily. Philip and Beatrice had a daughter, Catherine (1274 – January 2, 1307 or 1308, Paris). Philip died in 1283.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Baldwin II titular Emperor of Constantinople
1 CONT 1273–1283 Succeeded by
1 CONT Catherine I of Courtenay 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Peter Lock, The Franks in the Aegean 1204–1500, New York, 1995. 
