0 @N6000000002188307813@ NOTE John I of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John I the Posthumous
1 CONT King of France and Navarre, Count of Champagne (more...)
1 CONT Tomb Effigy of John the Posthumous
1 CONT Reign 15 November – 20 November 1316
1 CONT Titles King of Navarre (15 November-20 November 1316)
1 CONT Count of Champagne (15 November-20 November 1316)
1 CONT Born 15 November 1316(1316-11-15)
1 CONT Paris, France
1 CONT Died 20 November 1316 (aged 0)
1 CONT Paris, France
1 CONT Buried Saint Denis Basilica
1 CONT Predecessor Louis X
1 CONT Successor Philip V
1 CONT Royal House House of Capet
1 CONT Father Louis X (1289-1316)
1 CONT Mother Clémence d'Anjou (1293-1328)
1 CONT French Monarchy
1 CONT Direct Capetians
1 CONT Louis X
1 CONT    Joan II of Navarre
1 CONT    John I
1 CONT John I
1 CONT
1 CONT John I (15 November 1316 – 20 November 1316), called the Posthumous, was King of France and Navarre, and Count of Champagne, as the son and successor of Louis X, for the five days he lived.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born a king of the House of Capet and the posthumous son of Louis X and Clémence d'Anjou.
1 CONT
1 CONT John lived for only a few days and many believed his uncle, the future King Philip V, caused his death in order to gain the throne. There were also stories that Philip had the child kidnapped and substituted a dead child in his pl
1 CONC ace. During the 1350s, a man claiming to be King John I appeared in Provence. He was quickly put in prison and died there.
1 CONT
1 CONT John reigned for five days under his uncle's regency, until his death on November 20, 1316. The infant King was buried in Saint Denis Basilica. He was succeeded by his uncle, Philip V. The other claimant was John's half-sister, th
1 CONC e then four-year-old Princess Joan (Jeanne in French), daughter of Louis X's marriage with Marguerite of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was at this point that the question of the force of Salic Law was resolved regarding the succession to the Throne of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan, as a female, had a disputed claim to the throne of France: a female could not succeed to the throne of France if following the Salic law; however, if following the feudal law (which had thus far controlled the inheritance of
1 CONC  almost all fiefs in France), Joan would have been the next monarch of France. (She did, however, have undeniable rights in the succession of Navarre where females are allowed - witness that kingdom being brought to the Capetians 
1 CONC by Louis' own mother. However, these claims were ignored until after the deaths of her uncles Philip V and Charles IV.)
1 CONT
1 CONT A practical point obviously having impact on this legal interpretation was the rumour that Joan was a product of her mother's adultery and not at all a daughter of Louis X. By interpreting the law as allowing only male succession 
1 CONC Joan's position was quashed altogether, and the danger of a bastard succeeding was avoided without even examining her real birth.
1 CONT
1 CONT These events form part of the narrative of Les Rois Maudits (The Accursed Kings), a series of historical novels by Maurice Druon.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Ancestors
1 CONT John's ancestors in three generations John I of France Father:
1 CONT Louis X of France Paternal Grandfather:
1 CONT Philip IV of France Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Philip III of France
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Isabella of Aragon
1 CONT Paternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Joan I of Navarre Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Henry I of Navarre
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Blanche of Artois
1 CONT Mother:
1 CONT Clémence d'Anjou Maternal Grandfather:
1 CONT Charles Martel of Anjou Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Charles II of Naples
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Maria Arpad of Hungary
1 CONT Maternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Clementia of Habsburg Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Rudolph I of Germany
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Gertrude of Hohenburg
1 CONT
