0 @N6000000002187746091@ NOTE Philippa of Hainault
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa of HainaultPhilippa of Hainault (~1314 – August 15, 1369) was the Queen consort of Edward III of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa was born in Valenciennes (then in Flanders, now France) and was the daughter of William III, Count of Hainaut and Jeanne of Valois, the grandaughter of Philip III of France. She married Edward at York Minster, on October,
1 CONC  1327, nine months after his accession to the English throne and, unlike many of her predecessors, she did not alienate the English people by retaining her foreign retinue upon her marriage or bringing large numbers of foreigners 
1 CONC to the English court.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa accompanied Edward on his expeditions to Scotland (1333) and Flanders (1338-40), where she won acclaim for her gentleness and compassion. She is also remembered by history as the tender-hearted woman, who interceded with 
1 CONC her husband and persuaded him to spare the lives of the Burghers of Calais whom he had planned to execute as an example to the townspeople.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa and Edward had fourteen children, including five sons who lived into adulthood and whose rivalry would eventually bring about the long-running civil wars known as the Wars of the Roses. Their sons are listed below:
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward the Black Prince 
1 CONT Lionel of Antwerp 
1 CONT John of Gaunt 
1 CONT Edmund of Langley 
1 CONT Thomas of Woodstock 
1 CONT Another three sons died in infancy. They also had six daughters. These children are listed below:
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabella 
1 CONT Joan 
1 CONT William 
1 CONT Blanche 
1 CONT Joan 
1 CONT Mary 
1 CONT Margaret 
1 CONT Thomas 
1 CONT William 
1 CONT Philippa died of dropsy in Windsor Castle, and was buried at Westminster Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Queen's College in Oxford is named after her. It was founded by one of her chaplains, Robert de Eglesfield, in her honour.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Counts of Hainaut family tree 
1 CONT Counts of Holland family tree 
1 CONT Father:
1 CONT William III, Count of Hainaut Mother:
1 CONT Jeanne of Valois 
0 @N6000000001783280533@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa was born in Valenciennes (then in Flanders, now France) and was the daughter of William I, Count of Hainaut and Jeanne of Valois, the granddaughter of Philip III of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT She married Edward at York Minster, on 24 January 1328, eleven months after his accession to the English throne and, unlike many of her predecessors, she did not alienate the English people by retaining her foreign retinue upon he
1 CONC r marriage or bringing large numbers of foreigners to the English court.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa accompanied Edward on his expeditions to the Kingdom of Scotland (1333) and Flanders (1338-40), where she won acclaim for her gentleness and compassion. She is best remembered as the tender-hearted woman who interceded wi
1 CONC th her husband and persuaded him to spare the lives of the Burghers of Calais (1346) whom he had planned to execute as an example to the townspeople following his successful siege. She acted as a regent on several occasions when h
1 CONC e was on the continent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa had grown portly in her later years, and this added to the view most of her English subjects had of her as a friendly, homely, motherly woman whom the nation greatly loved. Philippa outlived 9 of her 14 children; two of w
1 CONC hom were lost during the Black Death outbreak (1348).
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa of Hainault  and Edward III married 24 January 1328, eleven months after his accession to the English throne. They had fourteen children, including five sons who lived into adulthood and whose rivalry would eventually bri
1 CONC ng about the long-running civil wars known as the Wars of the Roses. Their sons are listed below:
1 CONT Edward, the Black Prince (1330-76)
1 CONT Lionel of Antwerp, 1st Duke of Clarence (1338-68)
1 CONT John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster (1340-99)
1 CONT Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York (1341-1402)
1 CONT Thomas of Woodstock, 1st Duke of Gloucester (1355-97)
1 CONT
1 CONT Another three sons and two daughters died in infancy.
1 CONT
1 CONT There were four surviving daughters, listed below:
1 CONT Isabella of England (1332-1379)
1 CONT Joan of England (1334-1348)
1 CONT Mary Plantagenet (1344-1362)
1 CONT Margaret Plantagenet (1346-1361)
1 CONT
1 CONT Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT Through her children, Philippa reintroduced the bloodline of an earlier English King, Stephen, into the royal family. She was descended from Stephen through Matilda of Brabant, the wife of Floris IV, Count of Holland. Their daught
1 CONC er Adelaide of Holland married John I of Avesnes, Count of Hainaut, Philippa's paternal great-grandfather. Matilda of Brabant in turn was the great-granddaughter of Stephen through her mother Matilda of Boulogne, the wife of Henry
1 CONC  I, Duke of Brabant.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa was also a descendant of Harold II of England through his daughter Gytha of Wessex, married to Vladimir II Monomakh of Kiev. His bloodline, however, had been reintroduced to the English royal family by Philippa's mother-i
1 CONC n-law, Isabella of France, a granddaughter of Isabella of Aragon, the wife of Philip III of France. Isabella of Aragon's mother, Violant of Hungary, was a daughter of Andrew II of Hungary, a grandson of Géza II by Euphrosyne of Ki
1 CONC ev, herself a granddaughter of Gytha. Through her maternal great-grandmother, Maria of Hungary, she was descended from Elisabeth of Bosnia (born before 1241), a daughter of Kuthen, Khan of the Cumens and his Slavic wife, Galicie o
1 CONC f Halicz, thus bringing Western Asian blood into the English royal line.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Queen's College, Oxford is named after Philippa. It was founded by one of her chaplains, Robert de Eglesfield, in her honour.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT On 15 August 1369 Philippa died of an illness akin to dropsy in Windsor Castle, and was buried at Westminster Abbey. By all accounts, her 40 year marriage to Edward had been happy.
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippa_of_Hainault>
