0 @N6000000004533823884@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Philippa of Hainault (~1314 - August 15, 1369) was the Queen consortof Edward III of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa was born in Flanders (modern Belgium) and was the daughter ofWilliam III, Count of Hainaut and Jeanne de Valois, the grandaughterof Philip III of France. She married Edward at York Minster, onOctober, 1327, nine months af
1 CONC ter his accession to the English throneand, unlike many of her predecessors, she did not alienate the Englishpeople by retaining her foreign retinue upon her marriage or bringinglarge amounts of foreigners to the English court.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa accompanied Edward on his expeditions to Scotland (1333) andFlanders (1338-40), where she won acclaim for her gentleness andcompassion. She is also remembered by history as the tender-heartedwoman, who interceded with her
1 CONC  husband and persuaded him to spare thelives of the Burghers of Calais whom he had planned to execute as anexample to the townspeople.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa and Edward had thirteen children, including five sons wholived into adulthood and whose rivalry would eventually bring aboutthe long-running civil wars known as the Wars of the Roses. Their sonsare listed below:
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward the Black Prince
1 CONT Lionel of Antwerp
1 CONT John of Gaunt
1 CONT Edmund of Langley
1 CONT Thomas of Woodstock
1 CONT They also had several daughters.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa died of dropsy in Windsor Castle, and was buried atWestminster Abbey.
