0 @N6000000002187755652@ NOTE Henry I of Champagne
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Henry I of Champagne (died March 17, 1181), known as "the Liberal", was count of Champagne from 1152 to 1181.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the eldest son of Count Thibaut II of Champagne (who was also Count Thibaut IV of Blois). On his father's death, Henry chose to take Champagne, leaving the family's older holdings (including Blois, Chartres, Sancerre, and C
1 CONC hateaudun) to his younger brothers. At the time this may have been surprising, for the other territories were richer and better developed. Henry must have had foreseen the economic possibilities of Champagne, and it is during his 
1 CONC rule that the county achieved its high place as one of the richest and strongest of the French principalities.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry established orderly rule over the nobles of Champagne, and could fairly reliably count on the aid of some 2,000 vassals, which just by itself made him a power few in France could equal. This order in turn made Champagne a sa
1 CONC fe place for merchants to gather, and under the count's protection the Champagne Fairs became a central part of long-distance trade and finance in medieval Europe.
1 CONT
1 CONT In addition, the count's court in Troyes became a renowned literary center.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry married Marie of Champagne, daughter of Louis VII of France and Eleanor of Aquitaine. He was succeeded by their eldest son Henry II of Champagne. After Henry II became king of Jerusalem, the younger son Thibaut III became co
1 CONC unt.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Theobald II Count of Champagne
1 CONT 1152–1181 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry II 
