0 @N6000000002187761089@ NOTE Henry II of Champagne
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry II of Champagne (July 29, 1166 – September 10, 1197), was count of Champagne from 1181 to 1197, and king of Jerusalem from 1192 to 1197, although he never used the title of king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was the eldest son of Count Henry I of Champagne and Marie of France, a daughter of King Louis VII of France and Eleanor of Aquitaine. His father died in 1181, and his mother ruled as regent until 1187.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1190 Henry left for the East, after having his barons swear to recognize his younger brother Thibaut as his successor should he fail to return. He joined the Third Crusade, arriving ahead of his uncles, King Philip II of France
1 CONC  and King Richard I of England. Initially, he was one of the leaders of the French contingent at the siege of Acre before Philip's arrival. He is said to have been a member of the group involved in the abduction of Isabella of Jer
1 CONC usalem, to get her to consent to a divorce from Humphrey IV of Toron so that she could be married to Conrad of Montferrat. Henry was related to Conrad through both his maternal grandparents. According to Baha al-Din, he was wounde
1 CONC d at Acre on 15 November.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later on in the campaign, Henry shifted his allegiances to Richard. In April 1192, King Richard sent Henry as his representative from Acre to Tyre, to inform Conrad of Montferrat of his election as King of Jerusalem. Henry then re
1 CONC turned to Acre. A few days later, Conrad was murdered by two Hashshashin. Henry came back to Tyre two days later, ostensibly to help organise Conrad's coronation, but found that a funeral was being prepared instead. He was immedia
1 CONC tely betrothed to the newly-widowed - and pregnant - Queen Isabella of Jerusalem. They were married just eight days after Conrad's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT The marriage was glossed romantically by some of the chroniclers: that Isabella was so taken with Henry's physical attractions (he was 20 years younger than Conrad) that she asked him to marry her. Since she was already known to b
1 CONC e pregnant with Conrad's child (Maria of Montferrat), the marriage was considered scandalous by some, but it was politically vital for her to acquire another husband to defend the kingdom. However, some consultation with the Haute
1 CONC  Cour might have been expected.The couple went on to have two daughters, Alice and Philippa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry asked for permission from his uncle Richard, who gave it promptly: however, since Richard was suspected of Conrad's murder, this raises further questions about the whole episode. Indeed, Henry, who was known to the Arabs as 
1 CONC "al-kond Herri", later sought an alliance with the Hashshashin, and was invited to visit their fortress stronghold, al-Kahf. To demonstrate his authority, the grand master of the Hashshashin beckoned to two adherents, who immediat
1 CONC ely flung themselves from the ramparts to their deaths. The Hashshashin then offered to commit a murder for Henry, as an honour to their guest. Henry demured, concluded the treaty, and departed. Patrick A. Williams has suggested H
1 CONC enry himself as a suspect in Conrad's murder, although it would have been a risky undertaking without his uncle's support.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry died in 1197, falling from a first-floor window at his palace in Acre. There are varying accounts in different manuscripts of the Old French Continuation of William of Tyre, also known as The Chronicle of Ernoul. The majorit
1 CONC y suggest that a window-lattice or balcony gave way as he leaned against it. A servant, possibly a dwarf named Scarlet, also fell, after trying to save him by catching hold of his hanging sleeve, but he weighed too little to pull 
1 CONC the king (who was tall and strongly-built) back. Another version suggests that Henry had been watching a parade from the window, when a party of Pisan envoys entered the room. Turning to greet them, he stepped backwards and overba
1 CONC lanced. Whatever the exact circumstances, Henry was killed outright; the servant, who suffered a fractured femur, raised the alarm, but later died of his injury. Some accounts suggest that Henry might have survived if his servant 
1 CONC had not landed on top of him.
1 CONT
1 CONT His widow Queen Isabella remarried soon after his death. Her fourth (and last) husband was Amalric of Lusignan, king of Cyprus. Henry's heir-general was his eldest daughter Alice who was soon married to her stepbrother King Hugh I
1 CONC  of Cyprus and whose heirs represent the senior line of Counts of Champagne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry left behind several difficulties for Champagne. He had borrowed a great deal of money to finance his expedition to Jerusalem, and for his marriage; and the succession to the county of Champagne would later be contested by hi
1 CONC s daughters. In 1213, supporters of his nephew Theobald IV of Champagne alleged to a papal legate that the annulment of Isabella's marriage to Humphrey of Toron (who was still alive during her marriage to Henry) was invalid, and t
1 CONC herefore the girls were illegitimate. However, this was questionable: the legitimacy of Isabella's daughter by Conrad, Maria, and the right of her descendants to the throne of Jerusalem was never challenged, and if Maria was legit
1 CONC imate, so too were Isabella's daughters by Henry. Theobald eventually had to buy off both Alice and Philippa at considerable cost.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Morgan, M. R. The Chronicle of Ernoul and the Continuations of William of Tyre,1973 
1 CONT Payne, Robert. The Dream and the Tomb, 1984 
1 CONT Runciman, Steven. A History of the Crusades, vol. 3, 1954 
1 CONT Wheeler, Bonnie. Eleanor of Aquitaine: Lord and Lady, 2002 
1 CONT Williams, Patrick A. "The Assassination of Conrad of Montferrat: Another Suspect?", Traditio, vol. XXVI, 1970. 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Isabella and Conrad King of Jerusalem
1 CONT 1192–1197
1 CONT (with Isabella) Succeeded by:
1 CONT Isabella and Amalric II 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry I Count of Champagne
1 CONT 1181–1197 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Thibaut III 
